const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"An Apartment in Orsino's Palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Orsino, Curio, Lords; Musicians attending.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["If music be the food of love, play on,","Give me excess of it; that, surfeiting,","The appetite may sicken and so die.—","That strain again;—it had a dying fall;","O, it came o'er my ear like the sweet south,","That breathes upon a bank of violets,","Stealing and giving odour.—Enough; no more;","'Tis not so sweet now as it was before.","O spirit of love, how quick and fresh art thou!","That, notwithstanding thy capacity","Receiveth as the sea, nought enters there,","Of what validity and pitch soever,","But falls into abatement and low price","Even in a minute! so full of shapes is fancy,","That it alone is high-fantastical."],"id":2,"m":"If music is what feeds love, keep playing. Give me too much of it, so that my appetite, overfed, grows sick and dies. Play that tune again; it had a falling, dying close. Oh, it reached my ear like a soft south wind breathing over a bank of violets, taking their scent and carrying it on. Enough, no more; it isn't as sweet now as it was before. Oh spirit of love, how restless and hungry you are! Although you can swallow as much as the sea, nothing enters you, however fine and high its value, that doesn't lose its worth within a minute! Desire is so full of changing shapes that nothing is more fantastical."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURIO","lines":["Will you go hunt, my lord?"],"id":3,"m":"Will you go hunting, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["What, Curio?"],"id":4,"m":"Hunt what, Curio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CURIO","lines":["The hart."],"id":5,"m":"The hart."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Why, so I do, the noblest that I have:","O, when mine eyes did see Olivia first,","Methought she purg'd the air of pestilence;","That instant was I turn'd into a hart;","And my desires, like fell and cruel hounds,","E'er since pursue me.—How now! what news from her?"],"id":6,"m":"Why, I am doing that already, with the noblest heart I have. Oh, when I first saw Olivia, I thought she cleansed the air of disease. At that moment I was turned into a hunted deer, and my desires, like fierce and cruel hounds, have chased me ever since. Now then, what news from her?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine.","id":7},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["So please my lord, I might not be admitted,","But from her handmaid do return this answer:","The element itself, till seven years' heat,","Shall not behold her face at ample view;","But like a cloistress she will veiled walk,","And water once a-day her chamber round","With eye-offending brine: all this to season","A brother's dead love, which she would keep fresh","And lasting in her sad remembrance."],"id":8,"m":"If it please you, my lord, I wasn't allowed in, but her maid gave me this answer. The sky itself won't see her face openly until seven summers have passed; she will walk veiled like a nun and wet her room once a day with stinging tears. All this is to preserve her love for her dead brother, which she wants to keep fresh and lasting in her sad memory."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["O, she that hath a heart of that fine frame","To pay this debt of love but to a brother,","How will she love when the rich golden shaft","Hath kill'd the flock of all affections else","That live in her; when liver, brain, and heart,","These sovereign thrones, are all supplied and fill'd,—","Her sweet perfections,—with one self king!—","Away before me to sweet beds of flowers:","Love-thoughts lie rich when canopied with bowers."],"id":9,"m":"Oh, if she has a heart so finely made that she pays this debt of love just to a brother, how will she love when Cupid's golden arrow has killed every other feeling that lives in her, when her liver, brain and heart, those royal thrones of passion, thought and feeling, are all filled and ruled by one single king! Go ahead of me to sweet beds of flowers; thoughts of love grow rich under a canopy of leaves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":10},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The sea-coast.","id":11},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Viola, Captain, and Sailors.","id":12},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["What country, friends, is this?"],"id":13,"m":"What country is this, friends?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["This is Illyria, lady."],"id":14,"m":"This is Illyria, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And what should I do in Illyria?","My brother he is in Elysium.","Perchance he is not drown'd—What think you, sailors?"],"id":15,"m":"And what am I to do in Illyria? My brother is in heaven. Perhaps he isn't drowned. What do you think, sailors?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["It is perchance that you yourself were sav'd."],"id":16,"m":"It was only by chance that you yourself were saved."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["O my poor brother! and so perchance may he be."],"id":17,"m":"Oh my poor brother! Then perhaps by chance he was too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["True, madam; and, to comfort you with chance,","Assure yourself, after our ship did split,","When you, and those poor number sav'd with you,","Hung on our driving boat, I saw your brother,","Most provident in peril, bind himself,—","Courage and hope both teaching him the practice,—","To a strong mast that liv'd upon the sea;","Where, like Arion on the dolphin's back,","I saw him hold acquaintance with the waves","So long as I could see."],"id":18,"m":"True, madam, and to comfort you with that chance, be sure of this: after our ship split, when you and the few saved with you were clinging to our drifting boat, I saw your brother, very sensible in danger, tie himself (courage and hope taught him how) to a strong mast that floated on the sea. There, like Arion riding the dolphin, I saw him keep company with the waves for as long as I could see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["For saying so, there's gold!","Mine own escape unfoldeth to my hope,","Whereto thy speech serves for authority,","The like of him. Know'st thou this country?"],"id":19,"m":"For telling me that, here's gold! My own escape gives me hope, and your words support it, that he escaped too. Do you know this country?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Ay, madam, well; for I was bred and born","Not three hours' travel from this very place."],"id":20,"m":"Yes, madam, well; I was born and raised less than three hours' journey from this very spot."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Who governs here?"],"id":21,"m":"Who rules here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["A noble duke, in nature","As in name."],"id":22,"m":"A noble duke, noble by nature as well as by name."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["What is his name?"],"id":23,"m":"What is his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Orsino."],"id":24,"m":"Orsino."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Orsino! I have heard my father name him.","He was a bachelor then."],"id":25,"m":"Orsino! I have heard my father mention him. He was a bachelor then."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["And so is now,","Or was so very late; for but a month","Ago I went from hence; and then 'twas fresh","In murmur,—as, you know, what great ones do,","The less will prattle of,—that he did seek","The love of fair Olivia."],"id":26,"m":"And he still is, or was very recently. When I left here only a month ago, there was fresh gossip (you know how ordinary people chatter about what great people do) that he was courting the beautiful Olivia."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["What's she?"],"id":27,"m":"Who is she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["A virtuous maid, the daughter of a count","That died some twelvemonth since; then leaving her","In the protection of his son, her brother,","Who shortly also died; for whose dear love,","They say, she hath abjured the company","And sight of men."],"id":28,"m":"A virtuous young woman, the daughter of a count who died about a year ago. He left her in the care of his son, her brother, who soon died too. For love of him, they say, she has sworn off the company and even the sight of men."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["O that I served that lady!","And might not be delivered to the world,","Till I had made mine own occasion mellow,","What my estate is."],"id":29,"m":"Oh, if only I could serve that lady, and not have the world know who I am until I've made my own moment ripe for it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["That were hard to compass:","Because she will admit no kind of suit,","No, not the duke's."],"id":30,"m":"That would be hard to arrange, because she won't receive any kind of request, not even the Duke's."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["There is a fair behaviour in thee, captain;","And though that nature with a beauteous wall","Doth oft close in pollution, yet of thee","I will believe thou hast a mind that suits","With this thy fair and outward character.","I pray thee, and I'll pay thee bounteously,","Conceal me what I am; and be my aid","For such disguise as, haply, shall become","The form of my intent. I'll serve this duke;","Thou shalt present me as an eunuch to him;","It may be worth thy pains, for I can sing,","And speak to him in many sorts of music,","That will allow me very worth his service.","What else may hap to time I will commit;","Only shape thou silence to my wit."],"id":31,"m":"There is a good honesty in your manner, captain. Although nature often hides corruption behind a beautiful outside, I will believe that your mind matches your fair appearance. I beg you, and I'll pay you generously, hide who I am and help me with a disguise that suits my plan. I'll serve this duke. You will present me to him as a eunuch; it will be worth your trouble, because I can sing and play him many kinds of music, which will make me worth employing. Whatever else happens I'll leave to time. Just keep quiet and fit in with my scheme."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Be you his eunuch and your mute I'll be;","When my tongue blabs, then let mine eyes not see."],"id":32,"m":"You be his eunuch and I'll be your mute. If my tongue ever blabs, may my eyes go blind."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I thank thee. Lead me on."],"id":33,"m":"Thank you. Lead the way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":34},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"A Room in Olivia's House.","id":35},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch and Maria.","id":36},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What a plague means my niece, to take the death of her","brother thus? I am sure care's an enemy to life."],"id":37,"m":"What the plague does my niece mean by taking her brother's death like this? I'm sure that grief is bad for your health."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["By my troth, Sir Toby, you must come in earlier o' nights;","your cousin, my lady, takes great exceptions to your ill hours."],"id":38,"m":"Honestly, Sir Toby, you must come in earlier at night. Your relative, my lady, strongly objects to your late hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Why, let her except, before excepted."],"id":39,"m":"Well, let her object all she likes, as the lawyers say."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Ay, but you must confine yourself within the modest limits","of order."],"id":40,"m":"Yes, but you must keep yourself within the decent limits of good order."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Confine? I'll confine myself no finer than I am: these","clothes are good enough to drink in, and so be these boots too;","an they be not, let them hang themselves in their own straps."],"id":41,"m":"Keep myself in? I'll not make myself any neater than I am. These clothes are good enough to drink in, and so are these boots. If they aren't, let them hang themselves by their own straps."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["That quaffing and drinking will undo you: I heard my lady","talk of it yesterday; and of a foolish knight that you brought in","one night here to be her wooer."],"id":42,"m":"All this guzzling and drinking will ruin you. I heard my lady talking about it yesterday, and about a foolish knight you brought here one night to court her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Who? Sir Andrew Ague-cheek?"],"id":43,"m":"Who? Sir Andrew Aguecheek?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Ay, he."],"id":44,"m":"Yes, him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["He's as tall a man as any's in Illyria."],"id":45,"m":"He's as fine a man as anyone in Illyria."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["What's that to the purpose?"],"id":46,"m":"What's that got to do with it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Why, he has three thousand ducats a year."],"id":47,"m":"Why, he has an income of three thousand ducats a year."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Ay, but he'll have but a year in all these ducats; he's a","very fool, and a prodigal."],"id":48,"m":"Yes, but those ducats will only last him a year. He's a complete fool and a spendthrift."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Fye that you'll say so! he plays o' the viol-de-gambo,","and speaks three or four languages word for word without book,","and hath all the good gifts of nature."],"id":49,"m":"Shame on you for saying so! He plays the viola da gamba, speaks three or four languages word for word without a book, and has every natural gift."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["He hath indeed,—almost natural: for, besides that he's a","fool, he's a great quarreller; and, but that he hath the gift of","a coward to allay the gust he hath in quarrelling, 'tis thought","among the prudent he would quickly have the gift of a grave."],"id":50,"m":"He has indeed: he's almost a natural idiot. Besides being a fool, he's a great one for picking quarrels, and if he didn't have a coward's gift for cooling his appetite for fighting, sensible people think he'd soon have the gift of a grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["By this hand, they are scoundrels and subtractors that","say so of him. Who are they?"],"id":51,"m":"By this hand, the people who say that about him are scoundrels and detractors. Who are they?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["They that add, moreover, he's drunk nightly in your company."],"id":52,"m":"The same people who add that he's drunk every night in your company."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["With drinking healths to my niece; I'll drink to her as","long as there is a passage in my throat and drink in Illyria.","He's a coward and a coystril that will not drink to my niece","till his brains turn o' the toe like a parish-top. What, wench!","Castiliano-vulgo! for here comes Sir Andrew Ague-face."],"id":53,"m":"From drinking toasts to my niece. I'll drink to her as long as there's a passage in my throat and drink in Illyria. Any man who won't drink to my niece till his head spins like a top is a coward and a low knave. Look out, girl! Best behaviour! Here comes Sir Andrew Agueface."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek.","id":54},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Sir Toby Belch! how now, Sir Toby Belch!"],"id":55,"m":"Sir Toby Belch! How are you, Sir Toby Belch!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Sweet Sir Andrew?"],"id":56,"m":"Sweet Sir Andrew!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Bless you, fair shrew."],"id":57,"m":"Bless you, pretty little mouse."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["And you too, sir."],"id":58,"m":"And you too, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Accost, Sir Andrew, accost."],"id":59,"m":"Accost, Sir Andrew, accost."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["What's that?"],"id":60,"m":"Who's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["My niece's chamber-maid."],"id":61,"m":"My niece's maid."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Good Mistress Accost, I desire better acquaintance."],"id":62,"m":"Good Mistress Accost, I'd like to know you better."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["My name is Mary, sir."],"id":63,"m":"My name is Mary, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Good Mistress Mary Accost,—"],"id":64,"m":"Good Mistress Mary Accost..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["You mistake, knight: accost is, front her, board her,","woo her, assail her."],"id":65,"m":"You've got it wrong, knight. To accost means to face her, board her, woo her, attack her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["By my troth, I would not undertake her in this company.","Is that the meaning of accost?"],"id":66,"m":"Honestly, I wouldn't take her on in front of all these people. Is that what accost means?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Fare you well, gentlemen."],"id":67,"m":"Goodbye, gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["An thou let part so, Sir Andrew, would thou mightst never","draw sword again."],"id":68,"m":"If you let her leave like that, Sir Andrew, I hope you never draw your sword again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["An you part so, mistress, I would I might never draw","sword again. Fair lady, do you think you have fools in hand?"],"id":69,"m":"If you leave like that, mistress, I hope I never draw my sword again. Fair lady, do you think you are dealing with fools?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Sir, I have not you by the hand."],"id":70,"m":"Sir, I don't have you by the hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Marry, but you shall have; and here's my hand."],"id":71,"m":"Well, you shall have, and here's my hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Now, sir, thought is free. I pray you, bring your hand to","the buttery-bar and let it drink."],"id":72,"m":"Now, sir, I can think what I like. Please, bring your hand to the bar of the pantry and let it have a drink."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Wherefore, sweetheart? what's your metaphor?"],"id":73,"m":"Why, sweetheart? What does your figure of speech mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["It's dry, sir."],"id":74,"m":"It's dry, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Why, I think so; I am not such an ass but I can keep my","hand dry. But what's your jest?"],"id":75,"m":"Why, I think so. I'm not such an ass that I can't keep my hand dry. But what's your joke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["A dry jest, sir."],"id":76,"m":"A dry joke, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Are you full of them?"],"id":77,"m":"Are you full of them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Ay, sir, I have them at my fingers' ends: marry, now I let","go your hand I am barren."],"id":78,"m":"Yes, sir, I have them at my fingertips. But now that I let go of your hand, I'm empty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Maria.","id":79},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["O knight, thou lack'st a cup of canary: When did I see","thee so put down?"],"id":80,"m":"Oh knight, you need a cup of sweet wine. When have I ever seen you so knocked down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Never in your life, I think; unless you see canary put","me down. Methinks sometimes I have no more wit than a Christian","or an ordinary man has; but I am great eater of beef, and, I","believe, that does harm to my wit."],"id":81,"m":"Never in your life, I think, unless you've seen wine knock me down. Sometimes I think I have no more wit than an ordinary Christian or any common man. But I'm a great eater of beef, and I believe that harms my wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["No question."],"id":82,"m":"No doubt about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["An I thought that, I'd forswear it. I'll ride home","to-morrow, Sir Toby."],"id":83,"m":"If I thought that, I'd give it up. I'll ride home tomorrow, Sir Toby."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Pourquoy, my dear knight?"],"id":84,"m":"Pourquoi, my dear knight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["What is pourquoy? do or not do? I would I had bestowed","that time in the tongues that I have in fencing, dancing, and","bear-baiting. Oh, had I but followed the arts!"],"id":85,"m":"What does 'pourquoi' mean? Go or don't go? I wish I had spent the time on languages that I've spent on fencing, dancing and bear-baiting. Oh, if only I'd studied the arts!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Then hadst thou had an excellent head of hair."],"id":86,"m":"Then you would have had an excellent head of hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Why, would that have mended my hair?"],"id":87,"m":"Why, would that have improved my hair?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Past question; for thou seest it will not curl by nature."],"id":88,"m":"No question, because you can see it won't curl naturally."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["But it becomes me well enough, does't not?"],"id":89,"m":"But it suits me well enough, doesn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Excellent; it hangs like flax on a distaff; and I hope to","see a houswife take thee between her legs and spin it off."],"id":90,"m":"Excellent. It hangs like flax on a spinning stick, and I hope to see a housewife take you between her legs and spin it off."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Faith, I'll home to-morrow, Sir Toby; your niece will","not be seen; or, if she be, it's four to one she'll none of me;","the count himself here hard by woos her."],"id":91,"m":"Honestly, I'll go home tomorrow, Sir Toby. Your niece won't see anyone, or if she does, it's four to one she won't have me. The Count himself, close by here, is wooing her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["She'll none o' the Count; she'll not match above her","degree, neither in estate, years, nor wit; I have heard her","swear't. Tut, there's life in't, man."],"id":92,"m":"She won't have the Count. She won't marry above her own rank, in wealth, age or intelligence; I've heard her swear it. Come on, there's still hope, man."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I'll stay a month longer. I am a fellow o' the strangest","mind i' the world; I delight in masques and revels sometimes","altogether."],"id":93,"m":"I'll stay a month longer. I'm a man with the strangest mind in the world; I sometimes love masked balls and parties more than anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Art thou good at these kick-shaws, knight?"],"id":94,"m":"Are you good at these trifles, knight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["As any man in Illyria, whatsoever he be, under the","degree of my betters; and yet I will not compare with an old man."],"id":95,"m":"As good as any man in Illyria, whoever he is, as long as he isn't my superior; and yet I won't compare myself with an expert."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What is thy excellence in a galliard, knight?"],"id":96,"m":"How good are you at the galliard, knight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Faith, I can cut a caper."],"id":97,"m":"Honestly, I can cut a caper."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["And I can cut the mutton to't."],"id":98,"m":"And I can cut the mutton to go with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["And, I think, I have the back-trick simply as strong as","any man in Illyria."],"id":99,"m":"And I think I can do the backward step as well as any man in Illyria."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Wherefore are these things hid? wherefore have these","gifts a curtain before them? are they like to take dust, like","Mistress Mall's picture? why dost thou not go to church in a","galliard and come home in a coranto? My very walk should be a","jig; I would not so much as make water but in a sink-a-pace. What","dost thou mean? is it a world to hide virtues in? I did think, by","the excellent constitution of thy leg, it was formed under the","star of a galliard."],"id":100,"m":"Why are these talents hidden? Why do these gifts have a curtain in front of them? Are they likely to gather dust, like some lady's portrait? Why don't you go to church dancing a galliard and come home in a running dance? My very walk would be a jig; I wouldn't even piss except in a five-step dance. What do you mean by it? Is this a world to hide your virtues in? Judging by the excellent shape of your leg, I thought it was formed under a dancing star."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Ay, 'tis strong, and it does indifferent well in","flame-colour'd stock. Shall we set about some revels?"],"id":101,"m":"Yes, it's strong, and it looks pretty good in a flame-coloured stocking. Shall we start some partying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What shall we do else? were we not born under Taurus?"],"id":102,"m":"What else would we do? Weren't we born under Taurus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Taurus? that's sides and heart."],"id":103,"m":"Taurus? That rules the sides and the heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["No, sir; it is legs and thighs. Let me see thee caper: ha,","higher: ha, ha!—excellent!"],"id":104,"m":"No, sir, it rules the legs and thighs. Let me see you jump. Ha, higher! Ha, ha! Excellent!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":105},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"A Room in Orsino's Palace.","id":106},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Valentine, and Viola in man's attire.","id":107},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["If the duke continue these favours towards you, Cesario,","you are like to be much advanced; he hath known you but three","days, and already you are no stranger."],"id":108,"m":"If the Duke keeps showing you these favours, Cesario, you're likely to go far. He has only known you three days, and already you're no stranger."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["You either fear his humour or my negligence, that you call","in question the continuance of his love. Is he inconstant, sir,","in his favours?"],"id":109,"m":"You must fear either his moods or my carelessness, if you question whether his affection will last. Is he changeable in his favours, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALENTINE","lines":["No, believe me."],"id":110,"m":"No, believe me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Orsino, Curio, and Attendants.","id":111},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I thank you. Here comes the count."],"id":112,"m":"Thank you. Here comes the Count."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Who saw Cesario, ho?"],"id":113,"m":"Has anyone seen Cesario?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["On your attendance, my lord; here."],"id":114,"m":"Here, my lord, at your service."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Stand you awhile aloof.—Cesario,","Thou know'st no less but all; I have unclasp'd","To thee the book even of my secret soul:","Therefore, good youth, address thy gait unto her;","Be not denied access, stand at her doors,","And tell them there thy fixed foot shall grow","Till thou have audience."],"id":115,"m":"Stand aside for a while. Cesario, you know everything; I have opened to you the book of my most secret soul. So, good youth, take yourself to her. Don't be refused entry; stand at her doors and tell them your foot will take root there until you are heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Sure, my noble lord,","If she be so abandon'd to her sorrow","As it is spoke, she never will admit me."],"id":116,"m":"Surely, my noble lord, if she is as lost in grief as people say, she will never let me in."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Be clamorous and leap all civil bounds,","Rather than make unprofited return."],"id":117,"m":"Make a noise and break every rule of politeness rather than come back with nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Say I do speak with her, my lord. What then?"],"id":118,"m":"Suppose I do speak with her, my lord. What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["O, then unfold the passion of my love,","Surprise her with discourse of my dear faith:","It shall become thee well to act my woes;","She will attend it better in thy youth","Than in a nuncio of more grave aspect."],"id":119,"m":"Oh, then reveal my passionate love and overwhelm her with talk of my devotion. It will suit you well to act out my suffering; she will listen to it better from a youth like you than from a messenger with a more serious face."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I think not so, my lord."],"id":120,"m":"I don't think so, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Dear lad, believe it,","For they shall yet belie thy happy years","That say thou art a man: Diana's lip","Is not more smooth and rubious; thy small pipe","Is as the maiden's organ, shrill and sound,","And all is semblative a woman's part.","I know thy constellation is right apt","For this affair:—some four or five attend him:","All, if you will; for I myself am best","When least in company:—prosper well in this,","And thou shalt live as freely as thy lord,","To call his fortunes thine."],"id":121,"m":"Dear boy, believe it. Anyone who says you are a man doesn't know your youth: the goddess Diana's lips are not smoother or redder than yours, and your little voice is like a girl's, high and clear, and everything about you looks like a woman's part. I know your stars make you just right for this business. Four or five of you go with him, or all of you if you like, since I'm happiest when I have least company. Succeed in this, and you will live as freely as your lord and share his fortune as your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I'll do my best","To woo your lady. [Aside] Yet, a barful strife!","Whoe'er I woo, myself would be his wife."],"id":122,"m":"I'll do my best to woo your lady. [Aside] Yet what a struggle! Whoever I woo for him, I'd like to be his wife myself."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"V","loc":"A Room in Olivia's House.","id":123},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Maria and Feste.","id":124},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Nay; either tell me where thou hast been, or I will not open","my lips so wide as a bristle may enter in way of thy excuse: my","lady will hang thee for thy absence."],"id":125,"m":"No, either tell me where you've been or I won't open my lips wide enough to let a hair through in your defence. My lady will hang you for being away."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Let her hang me: he that is well hanged in this world needs","to fear no colours."],"id":126,"m":"Let her hang me. A man who is well hanged in this world doesn't need to fear any enemy's flags."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Make that good."],"id":127,"m":"Explain that."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["He shall see none to fear."],"id":128,"m":"He won't see any to be afraid of."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["A good lenten answer: I can tell thee where that saying was","born, of, I fear no colours."],"id":129,"m":"A thin, meagre answer. I can tell you where that saying 'I fear no colours' came from."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Where, good Mistress Mary?"],"id":130,"m":"Where, good Mistress Mary?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["In the wars; and that may you be bold to say in your foolery."],"id":131,"m":"From the wars, and you can be bold enough to say so in your fooling."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Well, God give them wisdom that have it; and those that are","fools, let them use their talents."],"id":132,"m":"Well, God give wisdom to those who have it; and as for fools, let them use the talents they have."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Yet you will be hanged for being so long absent: or to be","turned away; is not that as good as a hanging to you?"],"id":133,"m":"Still, you'll be hanged for being away so long, or else thrown out. Isn't that as good as a hanging to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Many a good hanging prevents a bad marriage; and for turning","away, let summer bear it out."],"id":134,"m":"Many a good hanging prevents a bad marriage; and as for being thrown out, the summer will make that bearable."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["You are resolute, then?"],"id":135,"m":"So you're determined, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Not so, neither: but I am resolved on two points."],"id":136,"m":"Not exactly, but I am fixed on two points."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["That if one break, the other will hold; or if both break,","your gaskins fall."],"id":137,"m":"So that if one breaks, the other will hold; or if both break, your trousers fall down."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Apt, in good faith, very apt! Well, go thy way; if Sir Toby","would leave drinking, thou wert as witty a piece of Eve's flesh","as any in Illyria."],"id":138,"m":"Clever, honestly, very clever! Well, off you go. If Sir Toby would give up drinking, you'd be as witty a daughter of Eve as any in Illyria."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Peace, you rogue; no more o' that; here comes my lady: make","your excuse wisely; you were best."],"id":139,"m":"Quiet, you rogue, no more of that. Here comes my lady. You'd better make your excuse wisely."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":140},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia and Malvolio.","id":141},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Wit, and't be thy will, put me into good fooling! Those wits","that think they have thee do very oft prove fools; and I, that am","sure I lack thee, may pass for a wise man. For what says","Quinapalus? Better a witty fool than a foolish wit.—God bless","thee, lady!"],"id":142,"m":"Wit, if it's your will, help me fool well! Clever people who think they have you often turn out to be fools; and I, who am sure I lack you, may pass for a wise man. For what does the philosopher Quinapalus say? 'Better a witty fool than a foolish wit.' God bless you, lady!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Take the fool away."],"id":143,"m":"Take the fool away."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Do you not hear, fellows? Take away the lady."],"id":144,"m":"Don't you hear, fellows? Take away the lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Go to, you're a dry fool; I'll no more of you: besides, you","grow dishonest."],"id":145,"m":"Enough; you're a dull fool. I've had enough of you. Besides, you're becoming unreliable."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Two faults, madonna, that drink and good counsel will amend:","for give the dry fool drink, then is the fool not dry; bid the","dishonest man mend himself: if he mend, he is no longer","dishonest; if he cannot, let the botcher mend him. Anything","that's mended is but patched; virtue that transgresses is but","patched with sin, and sin that amends is but patched with virtue.","If that this simple syllogism will serve, so; if it will not,","what remedy? As there is no true cuckold but calamity, so","beauty's a flower:—the lady bade take away the fool; therefore,","I say again, take her away."],"id":146,"m":"Two faults, madonna, that drink and good advice will cure. Give the dry fool a drink, and then the fool is not dry. Tell the dishonest man to mend his ways: if he mends, he is no longer dishonest; if he can't, let the tailor mend him. Anything that's mended is only patched: virtue that goes wrong is only patched with sin, and sin that reforms is only patched with virtue. If this simple logic works, good; if it doesn't, what can be done? Just as the only true cuckold is misfortune, so beauty is a flower that fades. The lady said take away the fool; so, I say again, take her away."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Sir, I bade them take away you."],"id":147,"m":"Sir, I told them to take away you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Misprision in the highest degree!—Lady, Cucullus non facit","monachum; that's as much to say, I wear not motley in my","brain. Good madonna, give me leave to prove you a fool."],"id":148,"m":"A mistake of the highest order! Lady, 'the hood does not make the monk', which is as much as to say that I don't wear a fool's costume in my brain. Good madonna, let me prove that you are a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Can you do it?"],"id":149,"m":"Can you do it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Dexteriously, good madonna."],"id":150,"m":"Dexterously, good madonna."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Make your proof."],"id":151,"m":"Go on, prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I must catechize you for it, madonna.","Good my mouse of virtue, answer me."],"id":152,"m":"I must question you like a catechism, madonna. My good little mouse of virtue, answer me."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Well, sir, for want of other idleness, I'll 'bide your proof."],"id":153,"m":"Well, sir, since I have nothing better to do, I'll put up with your proof."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Good madonna, why mourn'st thou?"],"id":154,"m":"Good madonna, why are you in mourning?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Good fool, for my brother's death."],"id":155,"m":"Good fool, because my brother has died."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I think his soul is in hell, madonna."],"id":156,"m":"I think his soul is in hell, madonna."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["I know his soul is in heaven, fool."],"id":157,"m":"I know his soul is in heaven, fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["The more fool you, madonna, to mourn for your brother's soul","being in heaven.—Take away the fool, gentlemen."],"id":158,"m":"Then you're even more of a fool, madonna, to mourn for your brother's soul when it's in heaven. Take away the fool, gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What think you of this fool, Malvolio? doth he not mend?"],"id":159,"m":"What do you think of this fool, Malvolio? Isn't he improving?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Yes; and shall do, till the pangs of death shake him.","Infirmity, that decays the wise, doth ever make the better fool."],"id":160,"m":"Yes, and he will keep improving until the pains of death shake him. Old age, which makes the wise decay, always makes a fool better at being a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["God send you, sir, a speedy infirmity, for the better","increasing your folly! Sir Toby will be sworn that I am no fox;","but he will not pass his word for twopence that you are no fool."],"id":161,"m":"God send you an early old age, sir, to increase your foolishness! Sir Toby will swear that I'm no fox, but he won't bet twopence that you're no fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["How say you to that, Malvolio?"],"id":162,"m":"What do you say to that, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["I marvel your ladyship takes delight in such a barren","rascal; I saw him put down the other day with an ordinary fool","that has no more brain than a stone. Look you now, he's out of","his guard already; unless you laugh and minister occasion to him,","he is gagged. I protest I take these wise men that crow so at","these set kind of fools, no better than the fools' zanies."],"id":163,"m":"I'm amazed that your ladyship enjoys such an empty-headed rascal. I saw him beaten the other day in a battle of wits by an ordinary fool who has no more brain than a stone. Look at him now, he's already lost for words. Unless you laugh and give him openings, he's dumbstruck. I declare that I consider these wise men who crow with delight at these trained fools no better than the fools' stooges."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O, you are sick of self-love, Malvolio, and taste with a","distempered appetite. To be generous, guiltless, and of free","disposition, is to take those things for bird-bolts that you deem","cannon bullets. There is no slander in an allowed fool, though he","do nothing but rail; nor no railing in known discreet man, though","he do nothing but reprove."],"id":164,"m":"Oh, you are sick with self-love, Malvolio, and you taste everything with a sickly appetite. Being generous, innocent and good-natured means taking things you think are cannonballs as if they were blunt toy arrows. There's no slander in a licensed fool, even if he does nothing but mock; just as there's no mockery in a man known to be sensible, even if he does nothing but criticise."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Now Mercury endue thee with leasing, for thou speakest well of","fools!"],"id":165,"m":"Now may Mercury, god of tricksters, teach you how to lie, because you speak well of fools!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Maria.","id":166},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Madam, there is at the gate a young gentleman much desires","to speak with you."],"id":167,"m":"Madam, there's a young gentleman at the gate who very much wants to speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["From the Count Orsino, is it?"],"id":168,"m":"From Count Orsino, is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["I know not, madam; 'tis a fair young man, and well attended."],"id":169,"m":"I don't know, madam. He's a handsome young man, with a good number of attendants."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Who of my people hold him in delay?"],"id":170,"m":"Which of my household is keeping him waiting?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Sir Toby, madam, your kinsman."],"id":171,"m":"Sir Toby, madam, your relative."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Fetch him off, I pray you; he speaks nothing but madman.","Fie on him!"],"id":172,"m":"Get him away from there, please; he talks nothing but nonsense. Shame on him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Maria","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Go you, Malvolio: if it be a suit from the count, I am sick, or","not at home; what you will to dismiss it."],"id":174,"m":"You go, Malvolio. If it's a message from the Count, I'm sick or not at home; say whatever you like to get rid of him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Malvolio.","id":175},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Now you see, sir, how your fooling grows old, and people dislike","it."],"id":176,"m":"Now you see, sir, how your fooling has grown stale, and people dislike it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Thou hast spoke for us, madonna, as if thy eldest son should","be a fool: whose skull Jove cram with brains, for here he comes—","one of thy kin, has a most weak pia mater."],"id":177,"m":"You have spoken up for us fools, madonna, as if your eldest son were going to be a fool. May Jove cram his skull with brains, because here comes one of your relatives who has a very weak brain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch.","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["By mine honour, half drunk!—What is he at the gate, cousin?"],"id":179,"m":"Upon my honour, half drunk! Who is at the gate, cousin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["A gentleman."],"id":180,"m":"A gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["A gentleman? What gentleman?"],"id":181,"m":"A gentleman? What gentleman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Tis a gentleman here.—A plague o' these pickle-herrings!—How","now, sot?"],"id":182,"m":"There's a gentleman here. A plague on these pickled herrings! Well now, you idiot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Good Sir Toby,—"],"id":183,"m":"Good Sir Toby..."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Cousin, cousin, how have you come so early by this lethargy?"],"id":184,"m":"Cousin, cousin, how did you come to be so sluggish so early in the day?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Lechery! I defy lechery. There's one at the gate."],"id":185,"m":"Lechery! I defy lechery. There's someone at the gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Ay, marry; what is he?"],"id":186,"m":"Yes indeed, but who is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Let him be the devil an he will, I care not: give me","faith, say I. Well, it's all one."],"id":187,"m":"Let him be the devil if he wants, I don't care. Give me faith, I say. Well, it's all the same."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What's a drunken man like, fool?"],"id":189,"m":"What is a drunk man like, fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Like a drowned man, a fool, and a madman: one draught above","heat makes him a fool; the second mads him; and a third drowns","him."],"id":190,"m":"Like a drowned man, a fool and a madman. One drink too many makes him a fool, the second makes him mad, and a third drowns him."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Go thou and seek the coroner, and let him sit o' my coz;","for he's in the third degree of drink; he's drowned: go, look","after him."],"id":191,"m":"Go and find the coroner and let him hold an inquest on my cousin, because he's in the third stage of drink: he's drowned. Go and look after him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["He is but mad yet, madonna; and the fool shall look to the","madman."],"id":192,"m":"He's only mad so far, madonna, and the fool will look after the madman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Feste.","id":193},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Malvolio.","id":194},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Madam, yond young fellow swears he will speak with you. I","told him you were sick; he takes on him to understand so much,","and therefore comes to speak with you; I told him you were","asleep; he seems to have a foreknowledge of that too, and","therefore comes to speak with you. What is to be said to him,","lady? he's fortified against any denial."],"id":195,"m":"Madam, that young fellow swears he will speak with you. I told him you were sick; he claims he knows that, and so he has come to speak with you. I told him you were asleep; he seems to have known that already too, and so he has come to speak with you. What shall we say to him, lady? He is armed against every refusal."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Tell him, he shall not speak with me."],"id":196,"m":"Tell him he will not speak with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Has been told so; and he says he'll stand at your door","like a sheriff's post, and be the supporter of a bench, but he'll","speak with you."],"id":197,"m":"He's been told so, and he says he'll stand at your door like a post outside a sheriff's house, or prop up a bench, but he will speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What kind of man is he?"],"id":198,"m":"What kind of man is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Why, of mankind."],"id":199,"m":"Why, the human kind."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What manner of man?"],"id":200,"m":"What sort of man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Of very ill manner; he'll speak with you, will you or no."],"id":201,"m":"A very ill-mannered sort. He'll speak with you whether you want to or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Of what personage and years is he?"],"id":202,"m":"What does he look like, and how old is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Not yet old enough for a man, nor young enough for a boy;","as a squash is before 'tis a peascod, or a codling, when 'tis","almost an apple: 'tis with him e'en standing water, between boy","and man. He is very well-favoured, and he speaks very shrewishly;","one would think his mother's milk were scarce out of him."],"id":203,"m":"Not old enough to be a man, nor young enough to be a boy; like a pea pod before the peas form, or an unripe apple that's nearly an apple. He's at the turn of the tide, between boy and man. He's very good-looking, and he speaks very sharply; you'd think his mother's milk was hardly out of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Let him approach. Call in my gentlewoman."],"id":204,"m":"Let him come in. Call my lady-in-waiting."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Gentlewoman, my lady calls."],"id":205,"m":"Gentlewoman, my lady is calling you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":206},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Maria.","id":207},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Give me my veil; come, throw it o'er my face;","We'll once more hear Orsino's embassy."],"id":208,"m":"Give me my veil. Come, throw it over my face. We'll hear Orsino's message once more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Viola.","id":209},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["The honourable lady of the house, which is she?"],"id":210,"m":"Which of you is the honourable lady of the house?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Speak to me; I shall answer for her. Your will?"],"id":211,"m":"Speak to me; I'll answer for her. What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Most radiant, exquisite, and unmatchable beauty,—I pray you,","tell me if this be the lady of the house, for I never saw her: I","would be loath to cast away my speech; for, besides that it is","excellently well penned, I have taken great pains to con it. Good","beauties, let me sustain no scorn; I am very comptible, even to","the least sinister usage."],"id":212,"m":"Most radiant, exquisite and unequalled beauty... Please tell me if this is the lady of the house, because I have never seen her. I'd hate to waste my speech, because besides being extremely well written, I have taken great trouble to learn it by heart. Good beauties, don't mock me; I am very sensitive, even to the slightest unkindness."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Whence came you, sir?"],"id":213,"m":"Where have you come from, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I can say little more than I have studied, and that","question's out of my part. Good gentle one, give me modest","assurance, if you be the lady of the house, that I may proceed in","my speech."],"id":214,"m":"I can say little more than I have learned, and that question isn't in my part. Gentle lady, give me reasonable assurance that you are the lady of the house, so that I may go on with my speech."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Are you a comedian?"],"id":215,"m":"Are you an actor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["No, my profound heart: and yet, by the very fangs of malice","I swear, I am not that I play. Are you the lady of the house?"],"id":216,"m":"No, my wise lady; and yet, I swear by the very fangs of malice, I am not what I am playing. Are you the lady of the house?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["If I do not usurp myself, I am."],"id":217,"m":"If I am not stealing my own place, I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Most certain, if you are she, you do usurp yourself; for","what is yours to bestow is not yours to reserve. But this is from","my commission: I will on with my speech in your praise, and then","show you the heart of my message."],"id":218,"m":"If you are she, then you are certainly stealing from yourself, because what is yours to give away is not yours to keep. But this isn't part of my instructions. I'll go on with my speech in your praise, and then show you the heart of my message."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Come to what is important in't: I forgive you the praise."],"id":219,"m":"Get to the important part; I'll excuse you the praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Alas, I took great pains to study it, and 'tis poetical."],"id":220,"m":"Oh dear, I took great trouble to learn it, and it's poetic."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["It is the more like to be feigned; I pray you keep it in. I","heard you were saucy at my gates; and allowed your approach,","rather to wonder at you than to hear you. If you be not mad, be","gone; if you have reason, be brief: 'tis not that time of moon","with me to make one in so skipping a dialogue."],"id":221,"m":"Then it's all the more likely to be false; please keep it to yourself. I heard you were rude at my gates, and I let you in more to marvel at you than to listen to you. If you aren't mad, go; if you are sane, be brief. I'm not in the mood to take part in such a light-headed conversation."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Will you hoist sail, sir? here lies your way."],"id":222,"m":"Will you set sail, sir? Here's your way out."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["No, good swabber; I am to hull here a little longer.—","Some mollification for your giant, sweet lady."],"id":223,"m":"No, good deck-scrubber, I'll stay anchored here a little longer. Please calm down your giant, sweet lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Tell me your mind."],"id":224,"m":"Tell me what you have to say."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I am a messenger."],"id":225,"m":"I am a messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Sure, you have some hideous matter to deliver, when the","courtesy of it is so fearful. Speak your office."],"id":226,"m":"You must have something terrible to deliver, when the manner of it is so alarming. State your business."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["It alone concerns your ear. I bring no overture of war, no","taxation of homage; I hold the olive in my hand: my words are as","full of peace as matter."],"id":227,"m":"It concerns your ears alone. I bring no declaration of war and no demand for tribute. I hold the olive branch in my hand; my words are full of peace as well as substance."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Yet you began rudely. What are you? what would you?"],"id":228,"m":"Yet you began rudely. Who are you? What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["The rudeness that hath appeared in me have I learned from my","entertainment. What I am and what I would are as secret as","maidenhead: to your ears, divinity; to any other's, profanation."],"id":229,"m":"Any rudeness in me I learned from how I was received. Who I am and what I want are as private as virginity: to your ears, holy; to anyone else's, profane."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Give us the place alone: we will hear this divinity."],"id":230,"m":"Leave us alone; we will hear this holy message."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Maria.","id":231},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Now, sir, what is your text?"],"id":232,"m":"Now, sir, what is your text?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Most sweet lady,—"],"id":233,"m":"Most sweet lady..."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["A comfortable doctrine, and much may be said of it.","Where lies your text?"],"id":234,"m":"A comforting doctrine, and there's much to say about it. Where is your text found?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["In Orsino's bosom."],"id":235,"m":"In Orsino's heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["In his bosom? In what chapter of his bosom?"],"id":236,"m":"In his heart? In which chapter of his heart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["To answer by the method, in the first of his heart."],"id":237,"m":"To answer in the same style, in the first chapter of his heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O, I have read it; it is heresy. Have you no more to say?"],"id":238,"m":"Oh, I have read it; it's heresy. Have you nothing more to say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Good madam, let me see your face."],"id":239,"m":"Good madam, let me see your face."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Have you any commission from your lord to negotiate with my","face? you are now out of your text: but we will draw the curtain","and show you the picture. Look you, sir, such a one I was this","present. Is't not well done?"],"id":240,"m":"Has your lord told you to negotiate with my face? Now you've strayed from your text. But we'll draw back the curtain and show you the picture. Look, sir, this is what I looked like at this present moment. Isn't it well painted?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Unveiling.","id":241},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Excellently done, if God did all."],"id":242,"m":"Excellently painted, if God did it all."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["'Tis in grain, sir; 'twill endure wind and weather."],"id":243,"m":"The colour is fast, sir; it will last through wind and weather."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["'Tis beauty truly blent, whose red and white","Nature's own sweet and cunning hand laid on:","Lady, you are the cruel'st she alive,","If you will lead these graces to the grave,","And leave the world no copy."],"id":244,"m":"It's true beauty, perfectly blended, whose red and white were laid on by nature's own sweet and skilful hand. Lady, you are the cruellest woman alive if you take these graces to the grave and leave the world no copy."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O, sir, I will not be so hard-hearted; I will give out","divers schedules of my beauty. It shall be inventoried; and every","particle and utensil labelled to my will: as, item, two lips","indifferent red; item, two grey eyes with lids to them; item, one","neck, one chin, and so forth. Were you sent hither to praise me?"],"id":245,"m":"Oh, sir, I won't be so hard-hearted. I'll issue various lists of my beauty. It will be catalogued, and every part and item labelled and added to my will, like: item, two lips, reasonably red; item, two grey eyes, with lids; item, one neck, one chin, and so on. Were you sent here to praise me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I see you what you are: you are too proud;","But, if you were the devil, you are fair.","My lord and master loves you. O, such love","Could be but recompens'd though you were crown'd","The nonpareil of beauty!"],"id":246,"m":"I see what you are: you are too proud. But even if you were the devil, you are beautiful. My lord and master loves you. Oh, love like his could only be repaid even if you were crowned the greatest beauty without equal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["How does he love me?"],"id":247,"m":"How does he love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["With adorations, fertile tears,","With groans that thunder love, with sighs of fire."],"id":248,"m":"With worship, with endless tears, with groans that thunder love, with fiery sighs."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Your lord does know my mind; I cannot love him:","Yet I suppose him virtuous, know him noble,","Of great estate, of fresh and stainless youth;","In voices well divulged, free, learn'd, and valiant,","And, in dimension and the shape of nature,","A gracious person: but yet I cannot love him;","He might have took his answer long ago."],"id":249,"m":"Your lord knows my mind; I cannot love him. Yet I believe he is virtuous, I know he is noble, very rich, young and pure; he is well spoken of, generous, learned and brave, and in his build and figure a gracious person. But still I cannot love him. He could have taken his answer long ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["If I did love you in my master's flame,","With such a suffering, such a deadly life,","In your denial I would find no sense,","I would not understand it."],"id":250,"m":"If I loved you with my master's passion, with such suffering, a life like dying, I would find no sense in your refusal; I would not understand it."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Why, what would you?"],"id":251,"m":"Why, what would you do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Make me a willow cabin at your gate,","And call upon my soul within the house;","Write loyal cantons of contemned love,","And sing them loud, even in the dead of night;","Holla your name to the reverberate hills,","And make the babbling gossip of the air","Cry out Olivia! O, you should not rest","Between the elements of air and earth,","But you should pity me."],"id":252,"m":"I'd make myself a hut of willow branches at your gate and call out to my soul inside your house. I'd write faithful songs of rejected love and sing them loudly, even in the dead of night. I'd shout your name to the echoing hills and make the chattering echo in the air cry out 'Olivia!' Oh, you would get no rest anywhere between the air and the earth until you pitied me."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["You might do much. What is your parentage?"],"id":253,"m":"You might achieve a lot. What is your family background?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Above my fortunes, yet my state is well: I am a gentleman."],"id":254,"m":"Better than my present fortunes, but my position is good. I am a gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Get you to your lord;","I cannot love him: let him send no more;","Unless, perchance, you come to me again,","To tell me how he takes it. Fare you well:","I thank you for your pains: spend this for me."],"id":255,"m":"Go back to your lord. I cannot love him; let him send no more messengers, unless, perhaps, you come to me again to tell me how he takes it. Goodbye. Thank you for your trouble; spend this for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I am no fee'd post, lady; keep your purse;","My master, not myself, lacks recompense.","Love make his heart of flint that you shall love;","And let your fervour, like my master's, be","Placed in contempt! Farewell, fair cruelty."],"id":256,"m":"I'm no hired messenger, lady; keep your purse. It's my master, not me, who lacks a reward. May Love turn the heart of the man you love to stone, and may your passion, like my master's, be treated with contempt! Goodbye, beautiful cruelty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":257},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What is your parentage?","'Above my fortunes, yet my state is well:","I am a gentleman.'—I'll be sworn thou art;","Thy tongue, thy face, thy limbs, actions, and spirit,","Do give thee five-fold blazon. Not too fast:—soft, soft!","Unless the master were the man.—How now?","Even so quickly may one catch the plague?","Methinks I feel this youth's perfections","With an invisible and subtle stealth","To creep in at mine eyes. Well, let it be.—","What, ho, Malvolio!—"],"id":258,"m":"'What is your family background?' 'Better than my fortunes, but my position is good. I am a gentleman.' I'll swear you are. Your speech, your face, your body, your actions and your spirit all give you a fivefold coat of arms. Not so fast! Slowly, slowly! If only the master were the servant. What's this? Can someone catch the plague so quickly? I feel this young man's perfections creeping in at my eyes with invisible, subtle stealth. Well, let it be. Hey there, Malvolio!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Malvolio.","id":259},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Here, madam, at your service."],"id":260,"m":"Here, madam, at your service."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Run after that same peevish messenger,","The county's man: he left this ring behind him,","Would I or not; tell him I'll none of it.","Desire him not to flatter with his lord,","Nor hold him up with hopes; I am not for him:","If that the youth will come this way to-morrow,","I'll give him reasons for't. Hie thee, Malvolio."],"id":261,"m":"Run after that rude messenger, the Count's servant. He left this ring behind, whether I wanted it or not; tell him I'll have none of it. Tell him not to flatter his lord or keep his hopes up; I am not for him. If the youth will come this way tomorrow, I'll give him my reasons. Hurry, Malvolio."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Madam, I will."],"id":262,"m":"I will, madam."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":263},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["I do I know not what: and fear to find","Mine eye too great a flatterer for my mind.","Fate, show thy force. Ourselves we do not owe:","What is decreed must be; and be this so!"],"id":264,"m":"I'm doing I don't know what, and I'm afraid my eyes have flattered my judgment. Fate, show your power. We don't own ourselves. What is destined must be, and let it be so!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":265},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"The sea-coast.","id":266},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antonio and Sebastian.","id":267},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Will you stay no longer; nor will you not that I go with you?"],"id":268,"m":"Won't you stay any longer, and won't you let me go with you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["By your patience, no; my stars shine darkly over me; the","malignancy of my fate might, perhaps, distemper yours; therefore","I shall crave of you your leave that I may bear my evils alone.","It were a bad recompense for your love, to lay any of them on","you."],"id":269,"m":"With your permission, no. My stars shine darkly over me, and the evil of my fate might perhaps infect yours. So I must ask your leave to bear my troubles alone. It would be a poor reward for your love to put any of them on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Let me know of you whither you are bound."],"id":270,"m":"At least tell me where you are going."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["No, 'sooth, sir; my determinate voyage is mere","extravagancy. But I perceive in you so excellent a touch of","modesty, that you will not extort from me what I am willing to","keep in; therefore it charges me in manners the rather to express","myself. You must know of me then, Antonio, my name is Sebastian,","which I called Rodorigo; my father was that Sebastian of","Messaline whom I know you have heard of: he left behind him","myself and a sister, both born in an hour; if the heavens had","been pleased, would we had so ended! but you, sir, altered that;","for some hours before you took me from the breach of the sea was","my sister drowned."],"id":271,"m":"No, honestly, sir; the journey I've decided on is just wandering. But I see in you such a fine touch of modesty that you won't force from me what I want to keep to myself; so good manners oblige me all the more to reveal myself. You must know, then, Antonio, that my name is Sebastian, though I called myself Roderigo. My father was that Sebastian of Messaline whom I know you have heard of. He left behind me and a sister, both born in the same hour. If heaven had been willing, I wish we had ended together too! But you, sir, changed that, because a few hours before you pulled me out of the breaking waves my sister was drowned."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Alas the day!"],"id":272,"m":"What a terrible day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A lady, sir, though it was said she much resembled me,","was yet of many accounted beautiful: but though I could not, with","such estimable wonder, overfar believe that, yet thus far I will","boldly publish her,—she bore mind that envy could not but call","fair. She is drowned already, sir, with salt water, though I seem","to drown her remembrance again with more."],"id":273,"m":"She was a lady, sir, who was thought beautiful by many, although people said she looked very like me. Though I can't go so far as to believe such generous praise, I will boldly declare this much: she had a mind that even envy would have to call beautiful. She is drowned already, sir, in salt water, though I seem to drown her memory again with more."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Pardon me, sir, your bad entertainment."],"id":274,"m":"Forgive me, sir, for the poor hospitality I've given you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["O, good Antonio, forgive me your trouble."],"id":275,"m":"Oh, good Antonio, forgive me for all the trouble I've caused you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["If you will not murder me for my love, let me be your servant."],"id":276,"m":"If you don't want to kill me for my love, let me be your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["If you will not undo what you have done—that is, kill","him whom you have recovered—desire it not. Fare ye well at once;","my bosom is full of kindness; and I am yet so near the manners of","my mother that, upon the least occasion more, mine eyes will tell","tales of me. I am bound to the Count Orsino's court: farewell."],"id":277,"m":"If you don't want to undo what you've done, that is, kill the man you saved, don't ask it. Goodbye at once. My heart is full of feeling, and I'm still so close to my mother's ways that at the slightest further cause my eyes will give me away with tears. I am going to Count Orsino's court. Farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":278},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["The gentleness of all the gods go with thee!","I have many enemies in Orsino's court,","Else would I very shortly see thee there:","But come what may, I do adore thee so","That danger shall seem sport, and I will go."],"id":279,"m":"May the kindness of all the gods go with you! I have many enemies in Orsino's court, or I would soon see you there. But whatever happens, I love you so much that danger will seem like a game, and I will go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":280},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"A street.","id":281},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Viola; Malvolio following.","id":282},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Were you not even now with the Countess Olivia?"],"id":283,"m":"Weren't you with the Countess Olivia just now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Even now, sir; on a moderate pace I have since arrived but","hither."],"id":284,"m":"Just now, sir; walking at a moderate pace, I've only got as far as here since."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["She returns this ring to you, sir; you might have saved","me my pains, to have taken it away yourself. She adds moreover,","that you should put your lord into a desperate assurance she will","none of him: and one thing more: that you be never so hardy to","come again in his affairs, unless it be to report your lord's","taking of this. Receive it so."],"id":285,"m":"She returns this ring to you, sir. You could have saved me the trouble by taking it away yourself. She adds that you should make your lord hopelessly certain that she will have nothing to do with him. And one thing more: that you should never be so bold as to come again on his business, unless it's to report how your lord takes this. Take it, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["She took the ring of me: I'll none of it."],"id":286,"m":"She took the ring from me. I won't have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Come, sir, you peevishly threw it to her; and her will is","it should be so returned. If it be worth stooping for, there it","lies in your eye; if not, be it his that finds it."],"id":287,"m":"Come, sir, you threw it to her rudely, and she wants it returned the same way. If it's worth bending down for, there it lies in front of you; if not, let whoever finds it keep it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":288},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I left no ring with her; what means this lady?","Fortune forbid my outside have not charm'd her!","She made good view of me; indeed, so much,","That methought her eyes had lost her tongue,","For she did speak in starts distractedly.","She loves me, sure: the cunning of her passion","Invites me in this churlish messenger.","None of my lord's ring! why, he sent her none.","I am the man; —if it be so,—as 'tis,—","Poor lady, she were better love a dream.","Disguise, I see thou art a wickedness","Wherein the pregnant enemy does much.","How easy is it for the proper-false","In women's waxen hearts to set their forms!","Alas, our frailty is the cause, not we;","For such as we are made of, such we be.","How will this fadge? My master loves her dearly,","And I, poor monster, fond as much on him;","And she, mistaken, seems to dote on me.","What will become of this? As I am man,","My state is desperate for my master's love;","As I am woman, now alas the day!","What thriftless sighs shall poor Olivia breathe!","O time, thou must untangle this, not I;","It is too hard a knot for me to untie!"],"id":289,"m":"I left no ring with her. What does this lady mean? Heaven forbid that my appearance has charmed her! She looked me over carefully; so carefully, in fact, that I thought her eyes had made her lose her tongue, because she spoke in broken, distracted bursts. She loves me, surely. The cunning of her passion invites me through this rude messenger. She'll have none of my lord's ring! Why, he sent her none. I am the man. If that's so, and it is, poor lady, she would do better to love a dream. Disguise, I see you are a wickedness that the cunning devil makes much use of. How easy it is for handsome deceivers to stamp their image on women's soft, wax-like hearts! Alas, our weakness is the cause, not we ourselves, for we are what we are made of. How will this work out? My master loves her dearly, and I, poor monster, love him just as much, and she, mistaken, seems to dote on me. What will become of this? As I am a man, I have no hope of my master's love; as I am a woman, alas the day, what useless sighs poor Olivia will breathe! Oh time, you must untangle this, not I. It is too hard a knot for me to untie!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":290},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A Room in Olivia's House.","id":291},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch and Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek.","id":292},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Approach, Sir Andrew; not to be a-bed after midnight is to","be up betimes; and diluculo surgere, thou know'st."],"id":293,"m":"Come here, Sir Andrew. Not to be in bed after midnight is to be up early; and 'to rise early is healthy', as you know."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Nay; by my troth, I know not; but I know to be up late","is to be up late."],"id":294,"m":"No, honestly, I don't know; but I know that to be up late is to be up late."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["A false conclusion; I hate it as an unfilled can. To be","up after midnight, and to go to bed then is early: so that to go","to bed after midnight is to go to bed betimes. Do not our lives","consist of the four elements?"],"id":295,"m":"A false conclusion; I hate it as much as an empty mug. To be up after midnight and go to bed then is early, so going to bed after midnight is going to bed early. Aren't our lives made up of the four elements?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Faith, so they say; but I think it rather consists of","eating and drinking."],"id":296,"m":"Honestly, so they say; but I think life is made up of eating and drinking."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Thou art a scholar; let us therefore eat and drink.—","Marian, I say!—a stoup of wine."],"id":297,"m":"You're a scholar; so let's eat and drink. Marian, I say! A jug of wine!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Feste.","id":298},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Here comes the fool, i' faith."],"id":299,"m":"Here comes the fool, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["How now, my hearts? Did you never see the picture of we three?"],"id":300,"m":"Hello, my hearties! Haven't you ever seen the picture of 'we three'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Welcome, ass. Now let's have a catch."],"id":301,"m":"Welcome, ass. Now let's have a round song."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["By my troth, the fool has an excellent breast. I had","rather than forty shillings I had such a leg; and so sweet a","breath to sing, as the fool has. In sooth, thou wast in very","gracious fooling last night when thou spokest of Pigrogromitus,","of the Vapians passing the equinoctial of Queubus; 'twas very","good, i' faith. I sent thee sixpence for thy leman. Hadst it?"],"id":302,"m":"Honestly, the fool has an excellent voice. I'd give forty shillings to have such a leg for dancing, and such a sweet voice for singing, as the fool has. Truly, you were fooling beautifully last night when you talked about Pigrogromitus, and the Vapians crossing the equator of Queubus. It was very good, honestly. I sent you sixpence for your sweetheart. Did you get it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I did impeticos thy gratillity; for Malvolio's nose is no","whipstock. My lady has a white hand, and the Myrmidons are no","bottle-ale houses."],"id":303,"m":"I pocketed your little tip; for Malvolio's nose is no whip handle, my lady has a white hand, and the Myrmidons are no cheap beer houses."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Excellent! Why, this is the best fooling, when all is","done. Now, a song."],"id":304,"m":"Excellent! Why, this is the best fooling, when all's said and done. Now, a song."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come on; there is sixpence for you: let's have a song."],"id":305,"m":"Come on, there's sixpence for you. Let's have a song."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["There's a testril of me too: if one knight give a—"],"id":306,"m":"There's a sixpence from me too. If one knight gives a..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Would you have a love-song, or a song of good life?"],"id":307,"m":"Would you like a love song, or a song about living well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["A love-song, a love-song."],"id":308,"m":"A love song, a love song."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Ay, ay; I care not for good life."],"id":309,"m":"Yes, yes, I don't care for living well."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["~O, mistress mine, where are you roaming?","~O, stay and hear; your true love's coming,","~That can sing both high and low:","~Trip no further, pretty sweeting;","~Journeys end in lovers meeting,","~Every wise man's son doth know."],"id":310,"m":"Oh my mistress, where are you wandering? Oh, stay and listen; your true love is coming, who can sing both high and low. Don't run any further, pretty sweetheart. Journeys end when lovers meet, as every wise man's son knows."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Excellent good, i' faith."],"id":311,"m":"Excellent, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Good, good."],"id":312,"m":"Good, good."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["~What is love? 'tis not hereafter;","~Present mirth hath present laughter;","~What's to come is still unsure.","~In delay there lies no plenty;","~Then come kiss me, sweet and twenty;","~Youth's a stuff will not endure."],"id":313,"m":"What is love? It isn't in the future. Present happiness brings present laughter; what's to come is always uncertain. There's nothing to be gained by delay. So come and kiss me, sweet one, twenty times over. Youth is a thing that won't last."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["A mellifluous voice, as I am true knight."],"id":314,"m":"A honey-sweet voice, as I'm a true knight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["A contagious breath."],"id":315,"m":"A catching voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Very sweet and contagious, i' faith."],"id":316,"m":"Very sweet and catching, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["To hear by the nose, it is dulcet in contagion. But shall","we make the welkin dance indeed? Shall we rouse the night-owl in","a catch that will draw three souls out of one weaver? shall we do","that?"],"id":317,"m":"If we heard through our noses, it would be a sweet disease. But shall we make the sky dance? Shall we wake the owl with a round song that would draw three souls out of a weaver? Shall we do that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["An you love me, let's do't: I am dog at a catch."],"id":318,"m":"If you love me, let's do it. I'm a real dog at a catch."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["By'r lady, sir, and some dogs will catch well."],"id":319,"m":"By Our Lady, sir, and some dogs catch well."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Most certain: let our catch be, 'Thou knave.'"],"id":320,"m":"Absolutely. Let our song be 'You knave'."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'Hold thy peace, thou knave' knight? I shall be constrain'd","in't to call thee knave, knight."],"id":321,"m":"'Hold your peace, you knave', knight? Then I'll be forced to call you knave in it, knight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["'Tis not the first time I have constrained one to call","me knave. Begin, fool; it begins 'Hold thy peace.'"],"id":322,"m":"It's not the first time I've forced someone to call me knave. Begin, fool; it begins 'Hold your peace'."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I shall never begin if I hold my peace."],"id":323,"m":"I'll never begin if I hold my peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Good, i' faith! Come, begin."],"id":324,"m":"Good, honestly! Come on, begin."},{"t":"sd","text":"They sing a catch.","id":325},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Maria.","id":326},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["What a caterwauling do you keep here! If my lady have not","called up her steward Malvolio, and bid him turn you out of","doors, never trust me."],"id":327,"m":"What a racket you're making in here! If my lady hasn't called her steward Malvolio and told him to throw you out, never trust me again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["My lady's a Cataian, we are politicians; Malvolio's a","Peg-a-Ramsey, and","[Singing.]","~'Three merry men be we.'","Am not I consanguineous? am I not of her blood? Tilly-valley,","lady.","~'There dwelt a man in Babylon, lady, lady.'"],"id":328,"m":"My lady's a Chinese trickster, we are schemers, Malvolio's a killjoy, and [Singing] 'Three merry men are we.' Aren't I related to her? Am I not of her blood? Nonsense, lady! [Singing] 'There lived a man in Babylon, lady, lady.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Beshrew me, the knight's in admirable fooling."],"id":329,"m":"Curse me, the knight is fooling wonderfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Ay, he does well enough if he be disposed, and so do I","too; he does it with a better grace, but I do it more natural."],"id":330,"m":"Yes, he does well enough when he's in the mood, and so do I. He does it more gracefully, but I do it more naturally."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["[Singing] O, the twelfth day of December,—"],"id":331,"m":"[Singing] Oh, on the twelfth day of December..."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["For the love o' God, peace!"],"id":332,"m":"For the love of God, quiet!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Malvolio","id":333},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["My masters, are you mad? or what are you? Have you no","wit, manners, nor honesty, but to gabble like tinkers at this","time of night? Do ye make an ale-house of my lady's house, that","ye squeak out your coziers' catches without any mitigation or","remorse of voice? Is there no respect of place, persons, nor","time, in you?"],"id":334,"m":"Gentlemen, are you mad, or what are you? Have you no sense, manners or decency, that you gabble like tinkers at this time of night? Are you turning my lady's house into a pub, that you screech your cobblers' songs without any softening of your voices? Have you no respect for the place, the people or the time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["We did keep time, sir, in our catches. Sneck up!"],"id":335,"m":"We kept time, sir, in our songs. Get lost!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sir Toby, I must be round with you. My lady bade me tell","you that, though she harbours you as her kinsman she's nothing","allied to your disorders. If you can separate yourself and your","misdemeanours, you are welcome to the house; if not, an it would","please you to take leave of her, she is very willing to bid you","farewell."],"id":336,"m":"Sir Toby, I must be blunt with you. My lady told me to tell you that although she gives you a home as her relative, she wants nothing to do with your disorderly behaviour. If you can separate yourself from your misbehaviour, you are welcome in the house. If not, and you would like to leave her, she is very willing to say goodbye to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Farewell, dear heart, since I must needs be gone.'"],"id":337,"m":"[Singing] 'Farewell, dear heart, since I must needs be gone.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Nay, good Sir Toby."],"id":338,"m":"No, good Sir Toby."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'His eyes do show his days are almost done.'"],"id":339,"m":"[Singing] 'His eyes do show his days are almost done.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Is't even so?"],"id":340,"m":"Is that how it is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'But I will never die.'"],"id":341,"m":"[Singing] 'But I will never die.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Sir Toby, there you lie."],"id":342,"m":"[Singing] Sir Toby, there you lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["This is much credit to you."],"id":343,"m":"This does you great credit."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["[Singing] 'Shall I bid him go?'"],"id":344,"m":"[Singing] 'Shall I tell him to go?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'What an if you do?'"],"id":345,"m":"[Singing] 'What if you do?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Shall I bid him go, and spare not?'"],"id":346,"m":"[Singing] 'Shall I tell him to go, and not spare him?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'O, no, no, no, no, you dare not.'"],"id":347,"m":"[Singing] 'Oh no, no, no, no, you don't dare.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Out o' tune? sir, ye lie. Art any more than a steward? Dost thou","think, because thou art virtuous, there shall be no more cakes","and ale?"],"id":348,"m":"Out of tune, sir? You lie. Are you anything more than a steward? Do you think that because you are virtuous there will be no more cakes and ale?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Yes, by Saint Anne; and ginger shall be hot i' the mouth","too."],"id":349,"m":"Yes, by Saint Anne, and ginger will still be hot in the mouth too."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Thou'art i' the right.—Go, sir, rub your chain with crumbs:","A stoup of wine, Maria!"],"id":350,"m":"You're right. Go, sir, polish your steward's chain with breadcrumbs. A jug of wine, Maria!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Mistress Mary, if you prized my lady's favour at anything","more than contempt, you would not give means for this uncivil","rule; she shall know of it, by this hand."],"id":351,"m":"Mistress Mary, if you valued my lady's favour at anything more than contempt, you wouldn't help this unruly behaviour. She will hear of it, by this hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":352},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Go shake your ears."],"id":353,"m":"Go and waggle your ears like the donkey you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["'Twere as good a deed as to drink when a man's a-hungry,","to challenge him the field, and then to break promise with him","and make a fool of him."],"id":354,"m":"It would be as good a deed as drinking when you're hungry to challenge him to a duel, then not turn up and make a fool of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Do't, knight; I'll write thee a challenge; or I'll","deliver thy indignation to him by word of mouth."],"id":355,"m":"Do it, knight. I'll write you a challenge, or I'll deliver your anger to him in person."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Sweet Sir Toby, be patient for to-night; since the youth of","the count's was to-day with my lady, she is much out of quiet.","For Monsieur Malvolio, let me alone with him: if I do not gull","him into a nayword, and make him a common recreation, do not","think I have wit enough to lie straight in my bed. I know I can","do it."],"id":356,"m":"Sweet Sir Toby, be patient tonight. Since the Count's young man was with my lady today, she has been very unsettled. As for Monsieur Malvolio, leave him to me. If I don't trick him into becoming a byword and make him a public joke, don't think I have the sense to lie straight in my bed. I know I can do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Possess us, possess us; tell us something of him."],"id":357,"m":"Tell us, tell us; tell us something about him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Marry, sir, sometimes he is a kind of Puritan."],"id":358,"m":"Well, sir, sometimes he is a kind of Puritan."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["O, if I thought that, I'd beat him like a dog."],"id":359,"m":"Oh, if I thought that, I'd beat him like a dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What, for being a Puritan? thy exquisite reason, dear knight?"],"id":360,"m":"What, for being a Puritan? Your excellent reason, dear knight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I have no exquisite reason for't, but I have reason good enough."],"id":361,"m":"I don't have an excellent reason for it, but I have a good enough reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["The devil a Puritan that he is, or anything constantly but a","time-pleaser: an affectioned ass that cons state without book and","utters it by great swarths; the best persuaded of himself, so","crammed, as he thinks, with excellences, that it is his grounds","of faith that all that look on him love him; and on that vice in","him will my revenge find notable cause to work."],"id":362,"m":"He's no more a Puritan than the devil is, or anything else consistently, except someone who bends with the times. He's an affected ass who learns grand phrases by heart and pours them out in great swathes. He thinks so well of himself, so crammed, as he believes, with good qualities, that it's an article of faith with him that everyone who looks at him loves him. And that's the vice in him my revenge will use to great effect."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What wilt thou do?"],"id":363,"m":"What will you do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["I will drop in his way some obscure epistles of love;","wherein, by the colour of his beard, the shape of his leg, the","manner of his gait, the expressure of his eye, forehead, and","complexion, he shall find himself most feelingly personated. I","can write very like my lady, your niece; on a forgotten matter we","can hardly make distinction of our hands."],"id":364,"m":"I'll drop some mysterious love letters in his path. In them he'll find himself described in the most touching detail: the colour of his beard, the shape of his leg, the way he walks, the look of his eyes, his forehead and complexion. I can write very like my lady, your niece; when we look at something written long ago, we can hardly tell our handwriting apart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Excellent! I smell a device."],"id":365,"m":"Excellent! I smell a trick."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I have't in my nose too."],"id":366,"m":"I've got it in my nose too."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["He shall think, by the letters that thou wilt drop, that","they come from my niece, and that she is in love with him."],"id":367,"m":"From the letters you drop, he'll think they come from my niece, and that she's in love with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["My purpose is, indeed, a horse of that colour."],"id":368,"m":"That's exactly the colour of horse I'm riding."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["And your horse now would make him an ass."],"id":369,"m":"And your horse would make him an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Ass, I doubt not."],"id":370,"m":"An ass, I don't doubt."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["O 'twill be admirable!"],"id":371,"m":"Oh, it will be wonderful!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Sport royal, I warrant you. I know my physic will work with","him. I will plant you two, and let the fool make a third, where","he shall find the letter; observe his construction of it. For","this night, to bed, and dream on the event. Farewell."],"id":372,"m":"Royal entertainment, I promise you. I know my medicine will work on him. I'll hide you two, and the fool can make a third, where he'll find the letter. Watch how he interprets it. For tonight, go to bed and dream about what will happen. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":373},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Good night, Penthesilea."],"id":374,"m":"Good night, Amazon queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Before me, she's a good wench."],"id":375,"m":"Upon my soul, she's a good girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["She's a beagle true bred, and one that adores me. What o' that?"],"id":376,"m":"She's a true-bred beagle, and she adores me. What of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I was adored once too."],"id":377,"m":"I was adored once too."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Let's to bed, knight.—Thou hadst need send for more money."],"id":378,"m":"Let's go to bed, knight. You'll need to send for more money."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["If I cannot recover your niece I am a foul way out."],"id":379,"m":"If I can't win your niece, I'm badly out of pocket."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Send for money, knight; if thou hast her not i' the end,","call me Cut."],"id":380,"m":"Send for money, knight. If you don't get her in the end, call me a nag."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["If I do not, never trust me; take it how you will."],"id":381,"m":"If I don't, never trust me, take it how you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come, come; I'll go burn some sack; 'tis too late to go","to bed now: come, knight; come, knight."],"id":382,"m":"Come on, come on, I'll go and warm some sherry. It's too late to go to bed now. Come on, knight; come on, knight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":383},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"A Room in Orsino's Palace.","id":384},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Orsino, Viola, Curio, and others.","id":385},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Give me some music:—Now, good morrow, friends:—","Now, good Cesario, but that piece of song,","That old and antique song we heard last night;","Methought it did relieve my passion much;","More than light airs and recollected terms","Of these most brisk and giddy-paced times:—","Come, but one verse."],"id":386,"m":"Give me some music. Good morning, friends. Now, good Cesario, just that bit of song, that old-fashioned song we heard last night. I thought it eased my feelings a great deal, more than the light tunes and studied phrases of these lively, fast-moving times. Come, just one verse."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURIO","lines":["He is not here, so please your lordship, that should sing it."],"id":387,"m":"If it please your lordship, the man who should sing it isn't here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Who was it?"],"id":388,"m":"Who was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CURIO","lines":["Feste, the jester, my lord; a fool that the Lady Olivia's","father took much delight in: he is about the house."],"id":389,"m":"Feste the jester, my lord, a fool that Lady Olivia's father was very fond of. He's somewhere about the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Seek him out, and play the tune the while."],"id":390,"m":"Find him, and play the tune meanwhile."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Curio.  Music.","id":391},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","cont":1,"lines":["Come hither, boy. If ever thou shalt love,","In the sweet pangs of it remember me:","For, such as I am, all true lovers are;","Unstaid and skittish in all motions else,","Save in the constant image of the creature","That is belov'd.—How dost thou like this tune?"],"id":392,"m":"Come here, boy. If you ever fall in love, remember me in its sweet pains. For all true lovers are like me: unsettled and restless in every feeling except the constant image of the one they love. How do you like this tune?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["It gives a very echo to the seat","Where Love is throned."],"id":393,"m":"It echoes the very place where Love sits enthroned."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Thou dost speak masterly:","My life upon't, young though thou art, thine eye","Hath stayed upon some favour that it loves;","Hath it not, boy?"],"id":394,"m":"You speak like a master. I'd bet my life that, young as you are, your eye has rested on some face it loves. Hasn't it, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["A little, by your favour."],"id":395,"m":"A little, if you'll allow it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["What kind of woman is't?"],"id":396,"m":"What kind of woman is she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Of your complexion."],"id":397,"m":"Like you in looks."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["She is not worth thee, then. What years, i' faith?"],"id":398,"m":"Then she isn't worthy of you. How old, honestly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["About your years, my lord."],"id":399,"m":"About your age, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Too old, by heaven! Let still the woman take","An elder than herself; so wears she to him,","So sways she level in her husband's heart.","For, boy, however we do praise ourselves,","Our fancies are more giddy and unfirm,","More longing, wavering, sooner lost and won,","Than women's are."],"id":400,"m":"Too old, by heaven! A woman should always take someone older than herself. That way she adapts to him and keeps a steady place in her husband's heart. For, boy, however we praise ourselves, our affections are more giddy and unsteady, more full of longing and wavering, sooner lost and won, than women's are."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I think it well, my lord."],"id":401,"m":"I believe that, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Then let thy love be younger than thyself,","Or thy affection cannot hold the bent:","For women are as roses, whose fair flower,","Being once display'd, doth fall that very hour."],"id":402,"m":"Then let your love be younger than you, or your affection can't stay at full stretch. For women are like roses: once their lovely flower has opened, it falls that very hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And so they are: alas, that they are so;","To die, even when they to perfection grow!"],"id":403,"m":"And so they are. Alas, that they are like that: to die just when they grow to perfection!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Curio and Feste.","id":404},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["O, fellow, come, the song we had last night:—","Mark it, Cesario; it is old and plain:","The spinsters and the knitters in the sun,","And the free maids, that weave their thread with bones,","Do use to chant it: it is silly sooth,","And dallies with the innocence of love","Like the old age."],"id":405,"m":"Oh, fellow, come, the song we had last night. Listen to it, Cesario; it is old and simple. The women spinning and knitting in the sun, and the carefree girls weaving lace with bone bobbins, often sing it. It's plain truth, and it plays with the innocence of love like the good old days."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Are you ready, sir?"],"id":406,"m":"Are you ready, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Ay; pr'ythee, sing.  [Music]"],"id":407,"m":"Yes, please sing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["~Come away, come away, death.","~And in sad cypress let me be laid;","~Fly away, fly away, breath;","~I am slain by a fair cruel maid.","~My shroud of white, stuck all with yew,","~O, prepare it!","~My part of death no one so true","~Did share it.","~Not a flower, not a flower sweet,","~On my black coffin let there be strown:","~Not a friend, not a friend greet","~My poor corpse where my bones shall be thrown:","~A thousand thousand sighs to save,","~Lay me, O, where","~Sad true lover never find my grave,","~To weep there!"],"id":408,"m":"Come away, come away, death, and let me be laid in a sad coffin of cypress. Fly away, fly away, breath; I am killed by a beautiful, cruel girl. My white shroud, decorated all over with yew, oh, prepare it! No one so faithful ever played his part in death as I do. Let not a flower, not a sweet flower, be scattered on my black coffin. Let not a friend, not a friend greet my poor body where my bones are thrown. To save a thousand thousand sighs, lay me, oh, where no sad true lover can find my grave to weep there!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["There's for thy pains."],"id":409,"m":"There's something for your pains."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["No pains, sir; I take pleasure in singing, sir."],"id":410,"m":"No pains, sir. I take pleasure in singing, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["I'll pay thy pleasure, then."],"id":411,"m":"Then I'll pay for your pleasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Truly, sir, and pleasure will be paid one time or another."],"id":412,"m":"Truly, sir, pleasure has to be paid for sooner or later."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Give me now leave to leave thee."],"id":413,"m":"Allow me now to dismiss you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Now the melancholy god protect thee; and the tailor make thy","doublet of changeable taffeta, for thy mind is a very opal!—I","would have men of such constancy put to sea, that their business","might be everything, and their intent everywhere; for that's it","that always makes a good voyage of nothing.—Farewell."],"id":414,"m":"Now may the god of melancholy protect you, and may the tailor make your jacket of shot silk that changes colour, because your mind is a real opal! I'd like men as constant as you to be sent to sea, where their business could be anything and their destination anywhere, because that's how you make a good voyage out of nothing. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Feste.","id":415},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Let all the rest give place.—"],"id":416,"m":"Let all the rest of you leave."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Curio and Attendants.","id":417},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","cont":1,"lines":["Once more, Cesario,","Get thee to yond same sovereign cruelty:","Tell her my love, more noble than the world,","Prizes not quantity of dirty lands;","The parts that fortune hath bestow'd upon her,","Tell her, I hold as giddily as fortune;","But 'tis that miracle and queen of gems","That Nature pranks her in attracts my soul."],"id":418,"m":"Once more, Cesario, go to that queen of cruelty. Tell her my love, which is nobler than the world, doesn't value quantities of muddy land. Tell her I regard the wealth that fortune has given her as lightly as fortune itself does. It is the miracle, that queen of jewels, her beauty, with which Nature has adorned her, that draws my soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["But if she cannot love you, sir?"],"id":419,"m":"But what if she cannot love you, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["I cannot be so answer'd."],"id":420,"m":"I can't accept that answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["'Sooth, but you must.","Say that some lady, as perhaps there is,","Hath for your love as great a pang of heart","As you have for Olivia: you cannot love her;","You tell her so. Must she not then be answer'd?"],"id":421,"m":"Truly, you must. Suppose some lady, as perhaps there is, feels as great a pain in her heart for love of you as you feel for Olivia. You cannot love her; you tell her so. Mustn't she accept that answer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["There is no woman's sides","Can bide the beating of so strong a passion","As love doth give my heart: no woman's heart","So big to hold so much; they lack retention.","Alas, their love may be called appetite,—","No motion of the liver, but the palate,—","That suffer surfeit, cloyment, and revolt;","But mine is all as hungry as the sea,","And can digest as much: make no compare","Between that love a woman can bear me","And that I owe Olivia."],"id":422,"m":"No woman's body could withstand the beating of such a strong passion as love gives my heart. No woman's heart is big enough to hold so much; they can't hold on to it. Alas, their love could be called appetite, a craving not of the liver, the seat of passion, but of the tongue, which gets overfed, sick of it, and disgusted. But mine is as hungry as the sea and can digest as much. Don't compare the love a woman could feel for me with the love I have for Olivia."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Ay, but I know,—"],"id":423,"m":"Yes, but I know..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["What dost thou know?"],"id":424,"m":"What do you know?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Too well what love women to men may owe.","In faith, they are as true of heart as we.","My father had a daughter loved a man,","As it might be perhaps, were I a woman,","I should your lordship."],"id":425,"m":"Only too well what love women can feel for men. Truly, they are as faithful in heart as we are. My father had a daughter who loved a man, as I might perhaps love your lordship, if I were a woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["And what's her history?"],"id":426,"m":"And what is her story?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["A blank, my lord. She never told her love,","But let concealment, like a worm i' the bud,","Feed on her damask cheek: she pined in thought;","And with a green and yellow melancholy,","She sat like patience on a monument,","Smiling at grief. Was not this love, indeed?","We men may say more, swear more; but indeed,","Our shows are more than will; for still we prove","Much in our vows, but little in our love."],"id":427,"m":"A blank page, my lord. She never told of her love, but let the secret, like a worm in the bud, feed on her rose-pink cheek. She pined away in thought, and with a pale, sickly sadness she sat like a statue of Patience on a tomb, smiling at grief. Wasn't this love indeed? We men may say more and swear more, but really our displays are greater than our feelings, because we always turn out to be strong in our vows but weak in our love."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["But died thy sister of her love, my boy?"],"id":428,"m":"But did your sister die of her love, my boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I am all the daughters of my father's house,","And all the brothers too;—and yet I know not.—","Sir, shall I to this lady?"],"id":429,"m":"I am all the daughters of my father's house, and all the brothers too, and yet I don't know. Sir, shall I go to this lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Ay, that's the theme.","To her in haste: give her this jewel; say","My love can give no place, bide no denay."],"id":430,"m":"Yes, that's the point. Go to her quickly. Give her this jewel, and say my love will not give way or accept a refusal."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":431},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"V","loc":"Olivia's garden.","id":432},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch, Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek, and Fabian.","id":433},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come thy ways, Signior Fabian."],"id":434,"m":"Come along, Signor Fabian."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Nay, I'll come; if I lose a scruple of this sport let me be","boiled to death with melancholy."],"id":435,"m":"Oh, I'll come. If I miss a scrap of this fun, let me be boiled to death in melancholy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Wouldst thou not be glad to have the niggardly rascally","sheep-biter come by some notable shame?"],"id":436,"m":"Wouldn't you be glad to see the stingy, rascally, sneaking dog brought to some great shame?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["I would exult, man; you know he brought me out o' favour","with my lady about a bear-baiting here."],"id":437,"m":"I'd be overjoyed, man. You know he got me into trouble with my lady over a bear-baiting here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["To anger him we'll have the bear again; and we will fool","him black and blue:—shall we not, Sir Andrew?"],"id":438,"m":"To make him angry, we'll bring the bear back again, and we'll make a fool of him till he's black and blue. Won't we, Sir Andrew?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["An we do not, it is pity of our lives."],"id":439,"m":"If we don't, it'll be a shame on our lives."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Maria.","id":440},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Here comes the little villain:—How now, my nettle of India?"],"id":441,"m":"Here comes the little villain. Well now, my golden treasure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Get ye all three into the box-tree: Malvolio's coming down","this walk; he has been yonder i' the sun practising behaviour to","his own shadow this half hour: observe him, for the love of","mockery; for I know this letter will make a contemplative idiot","of him. Close, in the name of jesting! [The men hide themselves.]","Lie thou there; [Throws down a letter] for here comes the trout","that must be caught with tickling."],"id":442,"m":"Get into the box hedge, all three of you. Malvolio's coming down this path. He's been over there in the sun for the last half hour practising his manners to his own shadow. Watch him, for the love of mockery, because I know this letter will make a daydreaming idiot of him. Hide, in the name of fun! [The men hide] You lie there [She drops a letter], because here comes the trout that must be caught by tickling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Maria.","id":443},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Malvolio.","id":444},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Tis but fortune; all is fortune. Maria once told me she","did affect me: and I have heard herself come thus near, that,","should she fancy, it should be one of my complexion. Besides, she","uses me with a more exalted respect than any one else that","follows her. What should I think on't?"],"id":445,"m":"It's all luck; everything is luck. Maria once told me that she liked me, and I've heard my lady herself come close to saying that, if she ever fell in love, it would be with someone of my type. Besides, she treats me with more respect than anyone else who serves her. What should I make of that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Here's an overweening rogue!"],"id":446,"m":"What an arrogant rogue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["O, peace! Contemplation makes a rare turkey-cock of him;","how he jets under his advanced plumes!"],"id":447,"m":"Oh, quiet! Daydreaming makes a splendid turkey-cock of him. Look how he struts with his feathers spread!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["'Slight, I could so beat the rogue:—"],"id":448,"m":"By God's light, I could beat the rogue..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Peace, I say."],"id":449,"m":"Quiet, I tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["To be Count Malvolio;—"],"id":450,"m":"To be Count Malvolio..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Ah, rogue!"],"id":451,"m":"Ah, rogue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Pistol him, pistol him."],"id":452,"m":"Shoot him, shoot him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Peace, peace."],"id":453,"m":"Quiet, quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["There is example for't; the lady of the Strachy married","the yeoman of the wardrobe."],"id":454,"m":"There's a precedent for it: the Lady of Strachy married the servant in charge of her wardrobe."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Fie on him, Jezebel!"],"id":455,"m":"Shame on him, the Jezebel!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["O, peace! now he's deeply in; look how imagination blows him."],"id":456,"m":"Oh, quiet! Now he's deep in it. Look how his imagination puffs him up."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Having been three months married to her, sitting in my state,—"],"id":457,"m":"After three months of marriage to her, sitting in my chair of state..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["O for a stone-bow to hit him in the eye!"],"id":458,"m":"Oh, for a catapult to hit him in the eye!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Calling my officers about me, in my branched velvet gown;","having come from a day-bed, where I have left Olivia sleeping."],"id":459,"m":"Calling my officers around me, in my embroidered velvet gown, having come from a couch where I've left Olivia sleeping..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Fire and brimstone!"],"id":460,"m":"Fire and brimstone!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["O, peace, peace."],"id":461,"m":"Oh, quiet, quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["And then to have the humour of state: and after a demure","travel of regard,—telling them I know my place as I would they","should do theirs,—to ask for my kinsman Toby."],"id":462,"m":"And then to adopt the mood of a great man, and after a serious glance around the room, telling them I know my place as I want them to know theirs, to ask for my relative Toby..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Bolts and shackles!"],"id":463,"m":"Chains and shackles!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["O, peace, peace, peace! Now, now."],"id":464,"m":"Oh, quiet, quiet, quiet! Now, now."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Seven of my people, with an obedient start, make out for","him: I frown the while, and perchance, wind up my watch, or play","with some rich jewel. Toby approaches; court'sies there to me:"],"id":465,"m":"Seven of my servants, jumping to obey, go out to fetch him. I frown meanwhile, and perhaps wind up my watch, or play with some rich jewel. Toby comes in and bows to me there..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Shall this fellow live?"],"id":466,"m":"Shall this fellow be allowed to live?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Though our silence be drawn from us with cars, yet peace."],"id":467,"m":"Even if our silence is dragged out of us with carts, keep quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["I extend my hand to him thus, quenching my familiar smile with an","austere regard of control:"],"id":468,"m":"I hold out my hand to him like this, cooling my friendly smile with a stern look of authority..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["And does not Toby take you a blow o' the lips then?"],"id":469,"m":"And doesn't Toby give you a punch on the mouth then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Saying 'Cousin Toby, my fortunes having cast me on your","niece, give me this prerogative of speech':—"],"id":470,"m":"Saying, 'Cousin Toby, since my good fortune has given me your niece, allow me the right to say this...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What, what?"],"id":471,"m":"What, what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'You must amend your drunkenness.'"],"id":472,"m":"'You must cure your drunkenness.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Out, scab!"],"id":473,"m":"Get out, you scab!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Nay, patience, or we break the sinews of our plot."],"id":474,"m":"No, be patient, or we'll cut the sinews of our plot."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Besides, you waste the treasure of your time with a","foolish knight';"],"id":475,"m":"'Besides, you waste the treasure of your time with a foolish knight...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["That's me, I warrant you."],"id":476,"m":"That's me, I bet."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'One Sir Andrew':"],"id":477,"m":"'One Sir Andrew.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I knew 'twas I; for many do call me fool."],"id":478,"m":"I knew it was me, because many people call me a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["What employment have we here?"],"id":479,"m":"What business do we have here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Taking up the letter.","id":480},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Now is the woodcock near the gin."],"id":481,"m":"Now the stupid bird is near the trap."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["O, peace! And the spirit of humours intimate reading aloud to","him!"],"id":482,"m":"Oh, quiet! And may the spirit of whims make him read it out loud!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["By my life, this is my lady's hand: these be her very","C's, her U's, and her T's; and thus makes she her great P's. It","is in contempt of question, her hand."],"id":483,"m":"Upon my life, this is my lady's handwriting. These are her very C's, her U's and her T's, and this is how she makes her capital P's. It's her handwriting beyond any question."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Her C's, her U's, and her T's. Why that?"],"id":484,"m":"Her C's, her U's and her T's. Why that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["[Reads] 'To the unknown beloved, this, and my good","wishes.' Her very phrases!—By your leave, wax.—Soft!—and the","impressure her Lucrece, with which she uses to seal: 'tis my","lady. To whom should this be?"],"id":485,"m":"[Reads] 'To the unknown beloved, this, and my good wishes.' Her very phrases! Excuse me, wax. Wait! And the seal is her Lucrece, which she always uses to seal her letters. It's my lady. Who can this be for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["This wins him, liver and all."],"id":486,"m":"This will win him over completely, liver and all."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["[Reads]","~'Jove knows I love,","~But who?","~Lips, do not move,","~No man must know.'","'No man must know.'—What follows? the numbers alter'd!—'No man","must know':—If this should be thee, Malvolio?"],"id":487,"m":"[Reads] 'Jove knows I love, but who? Lips, do not move; no man must know.' 'No man must know.' What comes next? The metre changes! 'No man must know.' What if this were you, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Marry, hang thee, brock!"],"id":488,"m":"Hang you, you stinking badger!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["~'I may command where I adore:","~But silence, like a Lucrece knife,","~With bloodless stroke my heart doth gore;","~M, O, A, I, doth sway my life.'"],"id":489,"m":"[Reads] 'I may command where I adore, but silence, like Lucrece's knife, stabs my heart with a bloodless blow. M.O.A.I. rules my life.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["A fustian riddle!"],"id":490,"m":"A pompous riddle!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Excellent wench, say I."],"id":491,"m":"An excellent girl, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'M, O, A, I, doth sway my life.'—Nay, but first let me see,—let","me see,—let me see."],"id":492,"m":"'M.O.A.I. rules my life.' No, but first let me see, let me see, let me see."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["What dish of poison has she dressed him!"],"id":493,"m":"What a dish of poison she has prepared for him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["And with what wing the stannyel checks at it!"],"id":494,"m":"And how quickly the hawk swoops at it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'I may command where I adore.' Why, she may command me: I","serve her, she is my lady. Why, this is evident to any formal","capacity; there is no obstruction in this;—And the end,—What","should that alphabetical position portend? If I could make that","resemble something in me.—Softly!—M, O, A, I.—"],"id":495,"m":"'I may command where I adore.' Why, she can command me; I serve her, she is my lady. Why, this is obvious to any normal intelligence; there's no difficulty in this. And the end: what could that arrangement of letters mean? If I could make that resemble something in me. Gently! M.O.A.I."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["O, ay, make up that:—he is now at a cold scent."],"id":496,"m":"Oh yes, work that out. He's lost the scent now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Sowter will cry upon't for all this, though it be as rank as a","fox."],"id":497,"m":"The hound will still bark at it, though it stinks as strongly as a fox."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["M,—Malvolio; M,—why, that begins my name."],"id":498,"m":"M: Malvolio. M: why, that's the first letter of my name."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Did not I say he would work it out?","The cur is excellent at faults."],"id":499,"m":"Didn't I say he'd work it out? The dog is excellent at following a false trail."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["M,—But then there is no consonancy in the sequel; that","suffers under probation: A should follow, but O does."],"id":500,"m":"M. But then the rest doesn't fit; that fails when tested. A should come next, but O does."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["And O shall end, I hope."],"id":501,"m":"And O will end it, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Ay, or I'll cudgel him, and make him cry 'O!'"],"id":502,"m":"Yes, or I'll beat him and make him cry 'Oh!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["And then I comes behind."],"id":503,"m":"And then I comes behind."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Ay, an you had any eye behind you, you might see more","detraction at your heels than fortunes before you."],"id":504,"m":"Yes, and if you had an eye behind you, you'd see more disgrace at your heels than good fortune in front of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["M, O, A, I;—This simulation is not as the former:—and","yet, to crush this a little, it would bow to me, for every one of","these letters are in my name. Soft; here follows prose.—","'If this fall into thy hand, revolve. In my stars I am above","thee; but be not afraid of greatness. Some are born great, some","achieve greatness, and some have greatness thrust upon them. Thy","fates open their hands; let thy blood and spirit embrace them.","And, to inure thyself to what thou art like to be, cast thy","humble slough and appear fresh. Be opposite with a kinsman, surly","with servants: let thy tongue tang arguments of state; put","thyself into the trick of singularity: She thus advises thee that","sighs for thee. Remember who commended thy yellow stockings, and","wished to see thee ever cross-gartered. I say, remember. Go to;","thou art made, if thou desirest to be so; if not, let me see thee","a steward still, the fellow of servants, and not worthy to touch","fortune's fingers. Farewell. She that would alter services with","thee,","~'The fortunate-unhappy.'","Daylight and champian discovers not more: this is open. I will be","proud, I will read politic authors, I will baffle Sir Toby, I","will wash off gross acquaintance, I will be point-device, the","very man. I do not now fool myself to let imagination jade me;","for every reason excites to this, that my lady loves me. She did","commend my yellow stockings of late, she did praise my leg being","cross-gartered; and in this she manifests herself to my love, and","with a kind of injunction, drives me to these habits of her","liking. I thank my stars I am happy. I will be strange, stout, in","yellow stockings, and cross-gartered, even with the swiftness of","putting on. Jove and my stars be praised!—Here is yet a","postscript. 'Thou canst not choose but know who I am. If thou","entertainest my love, let it appear in thy smiling; thy smiles","become thee well: therefore in my presence still smile, dear my","sweet, I pr'ythee.' Jove, I thank thee. I will smile; I will do","everything that thou wilt have me."],"id":505,"m":"M.O.A.I. This disguise isn't as clear as the earlier part; and yet, if I squeeze it a little, it would bend to fit me, because every one of these letters is in my name. Wait, here comes some prose. [Reads] 'If this falls into your hands, think it over. My stars place me above you, but don't be afraid of greatness. Some are born great, some achieve greatness, and some have greatness thrust upon them. Your fates are holding out their hands; let your courage and spirit embrace them. And to get used to what you are likely to become, throw off your humble skin and appear new. Be hostile to a relative and rude to servants; let your tongue ring out political arguments, and adopt the habit of being different. This is the advice of the one who sighs for you. Remember who praised your yellow stockings and wanted always to see you with your garters crossed. I say, remember. Go on, your fortune is made if you want it to be; if not, let me see you a steward still, the companion of servants, and not worthy to touch Fortune's fingers. Goodbye. She who would change places with you, The Fortunate-Unhappy.' Broad daylight and open country couldn't show more; this is clear. I will be proud, I will read political writers, I will humiliate Sir Toby, I will drop my low acquaintances, I will be perfect in every detail, exactly the man described. I'm not fooling myself now, letting my imagination trick me, because every reason points to this: my lady loves me. She did praise my yellow stockings recently, she did praise my leg in cross-garters, and in this she reveals her love for me, and with a kind of command urges me to dress the way she likes. I thank my stars I'm fortunate. I will be distant and haughty, in yellow stockings and cross-gartered, as fast as I can put them on. Jove and my stars be praised! Here's a postscript too. [Reads] 'You must know who I am. If you accept my love, let it show in your smiling; your smiles suit you well. So always smile in my presence, my dear sweet, I beg you.' Jove, I thank you. I will smile; I will do everything you want me to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":506},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["I will not give my part of this sport for a pension of","thousands to be paid from the Sophy."],"id":507,"m":"I wouldn't give up my share of this fun for a pension of thousands paid by the Shah of Persia."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I could marry this wench for this device:"],"id":508,"m":"I could marry this girl for this trick."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["So could I too."],"id":509,"m":"So could I."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["And ask no other dowry with her but such another jest."],"id":510,"m":"And ask for no other dowry with her than another joke like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Maria.","id":511},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Nor I neither."],"id":512,"m":"Nor I."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Here comes my noble gull-catcher."],"id":513,"m":"Here comes my noble fool-catcher."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Wilt thou set thy foot o' my neck?"],"id":514,"m":"Will you put your foot on my neck?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Or o' mine either?"],"id":515,"m":"Or on mine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Shall I play my freedom at tray-trip, and become thy bond-slave?"],"id":516,"m":"Shall I gamble my freedom at dice and become your slave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I' faith, or I either?"],"id":517,"m":"Honestly, or me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Why, thou hast put him in such a dream, that, when the","image of it leaves him, he must run mad."],"id":518,"m":"Why, you've put him into such a dream that when the vision leaves him he'll go mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Nay, but say true; does it work upon him?"],"id":519,"m":"No, but tell me truly, is it working on him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Like aqua-vitae with a midwife."],"id":520,"m":"Like brandy on a midwife."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["If you will then see the fruits of the sport, mark his","first approach before my lady: he will come to her in yellow","stockings, and 'tis a colour she abhors, and cross-gartered, a","fashion she detests; and he will smile upon her, which will now","be so unsuitable to her disposition, being addicted to a","melancholy as she is, that it cannot but turn him into a notable","contempt; if you will see it, follow me."],"id":521,"m":"If you want to see the results of the joke, then watch how he first approaches my lady. He'll come to her in yellow stockings, a colour she hates, and cross-gartered, a fashion she detests. And he'll smile at her, which will be so out of keeping with her mood, given over to melancholy as she is, that it's bound to make him a figure of contempt. If you want to see it, follow me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["To the gates of Tartar, thou most excellent devil of wit!"],"id":522,"m":"To the gates of hell, you most excellent devil of wit!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I'll make one too."],"id":523,"m":"I'll come too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":524},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Olivia's garden.","id":525},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Viola, and Feste with a tabor.","id":526},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Save thee, friend, and thy music. Dost thou live by thy tabor?"],"id":527,"m":"God save you, friend, and your music. Do you make your living by your drum?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["No, sir, I live by the church."],"id":528,"m":"No, sir, I live by the church."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Art thou a churchman?"],"id":529,"m":"Are you a clergyman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["No such matter, sir: I do live by the church; for I do live","at my house, and my house doth stand by the church."],"id":530,"m":"Nothing like that, sir. I live by the church, because I live at my house, and my house stands by the church."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["So thou mayst say the king lies by a beggar, if a beggar","dwell near him; or the church stands by thy tabor, if thy tabor","stand by the church."],"id":531,"m":"In that case you could say the king lies with a beggar, if a beggar lives near him; or that the church stands by your drum, if your drum stands by the church."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["You have said, sir.—To see this age!—A sentence is but a","cheveril glove to a good wit. How quickly the wrong side may be","turned outward!"],"id":532,"m":"You've said it, sir. What an age we live in! A sentence is just a soft kid glove to a clever wit. How quickly it can be turned inside out!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Nay, that's certain; they that dally nicely with words may","quickly make them wanton."],"id":533,"m":"Yes, that's certain. People who play cleverly with words can quickly make them loose."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I would, therefore, my sister had had no name, sir."],"id":534,"m":"Then I wish my sister had had no name, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Why, man?"],"id":535,"m":"Why, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Why, sir, her name's a word; and to dally with that word","might make my sister wanton. But indeed words are very rascals,","since bonds disgraced them."],"id":536,"m":"Why, sir, her name's a word, and playing with that word might make my sister loose. But really, words have become complete rascals since people needed written contracts to be trusted."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Thy reason, man?"],"id":537,"m":"Your reason, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Troth, sir, I can yield you none without words; and words","are grown so false I am loath to prove reason with them."],"id":538,"m":"Honestly, sir, I can't give you one without words, and words have become so false that I hate to prove anything with them."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I warrant, thou art a merry fellow, and carest for nothing."],"id":539,"m":"I'm sure you're a cheerful fellow who cares about nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Not so, sir, I do care for something: but in my conscience,","sir, I do not care for you; if that be to care for nothing, sir,","I would it would make you invisible."],"id":540,"m":"Not so, sir, I do care about something. But honestly, sir, I don't care about you; and if that means caring about nothing, sir, I wish it would make you invisible."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Art not thou the Lady Olivia's fool?"],"id":541,"m":"Aren't you Lady Olivia's fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["No, indeed, sir; the Lady Olivia has no folly: she will keep","no fool, sir, till she be married; and fools are as like husbands","as pilchards are to herrings, the husband's the bigger; I am,","indeed, not her fool, but her corrupter of words."],"id":542,"m":"No, indeed, sir. Lady Olivia has no foolishness. She won't keep a fool, sir, until she's married; and fools are as like husbands as sardines are like herrings: the husband's the bigger. I'm not really her fool, but her twister of words."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I saw thee late at the Count Orsino's."],"id":543,"m":"I saw you recently at Count Orsino's."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Foolery, sir, does walk about the orb like the sun; it","shines everywhere. I would be sorry, sir, but the fool should be","as oft with your master as with my mistress: I think I saw your","wisdom there."],"id":544,"m":"Foolery, sir, walks around the world like the sun; it shines everywhere. I'd be sorry, sir, if the fool weren't with your master as often as with my mistress. I think I saw your wise self there."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Nay, an thou pass upon me, I'll no more with thee.","Hold, there's expenses for thee."],"id":545,"m":"No, if you're going to make fun of me, I'll have no more to do with you. Wait, here's something for your expenses."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Now Jove, in his next commodity of hair, send thee a beard!"],"id":546,"m":"Now may Jove, the next time he's handing out hair, send you a beard!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["By my troth, I'll tell thee, I am almost sick for one; though I","would not have it grow on my chin. Is thy lady within?"],"id":547,"m":"Honestly, I'll tell you, I'm almost sick with longing for one, though I wouldn't want it to grow on my chin. Is your lady in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Would not a pair of these have bred, sir?"],"id":548,"m":"Wouldn't a pair of these coins have bred, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Yes, being kept together and put to use."],"id":549,"m":"Yes, if they were kept together and put out to earn interest."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I would play Lord Pandarus of Phrygia, sir, to bring a","Cressida to this Troilus."],"id":550,"m":"I'd like to play Lord Pandarus of Phrygia, sir, and bring a Cressida to this Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I understand you, sir; 'tis well begged."],"id":551,"m":"I understand you, sir. That's cleverly begged."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["The matter, I hope, is not great, sir, begging but a beggar:","Cressida was a beggar. My lady is within, sir. I will construe to","them whence you come; who you are and what you would are out of","my welkin: I might say element; but the word is overworn."],"id":552,"m":"It's no great thing, I hope, sir, begging for a beggar: Cressida ended up a beggar. My lady is in, sir. I'll explain to them where you've come from. Who you are and what you want is outside my sky; I might say 'element', but that word is worn out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":553},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["This fellow's wise enough to play the fool;","And, to do that well, craves a kind of wit:","He must observe their mood on whom he jests,","The quality of persons, and the time;","And, like the haggard, check at every feather","That comes before his eye. This is a practice","As full of labour as a wise man's art:","For folly, that he wisely shows, is fit;","But wise men, folly-fallen, quite taint their wit."],"id":554,"m":"This fellow is wise enough to play the fool, and to do that well needs a kind of intelligence. He has to watch the mood of the people he jokes about, their rank, and the right moment; and like a wild hawk, he has to swoop at every feather that comes in front of his eye. This is a skill as hard as a wise man's profession. The folly he shows so wisely is fitting; but wise men who fall into folly completely ruin their reputation for sense."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch and Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek.","id":555},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Save you, gentleman."],"id":556,"m":"God save you, gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And you, sir."],"id":557,"m":"And you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Dieu vous garde, monsieur."],"id":558,"m":"God protect you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Et vous aussi; votre serviteur."],"id":559,"m":"And you too; your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I hope, sir, you are; and I am yours."],"id":560,"m":"I hope you are, sir; and I am yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Will you encounter the house? my niece is desirous you","should enter, if your trade be to her."],"id":561,"m":"Will you approach the house? My niece wants you to come in, if your business is with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I am bound to your niece, sir: I mean, she is the list of my","voyage."],"id":562,"m":"I am bound for your niece, sir; I mean, she is the destination of my voyage."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Taste your legs, sir; put them to motion."],"id":563,"m":"Try your legs, sir; put them in motion."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["My legs do better understand me, sir, than I understand what","you mean by bidding me taste my legs."],"id":564,"m":"My legs understand me better, sir, than I understand what you mean by telling me to try my legs."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I mean, to go, sir, to enter."],"id":565,"m":"I mean to go, sir, to enter."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I will answer you with gait and entrance: but we are prevented."],"id":566,"m":"I'll answer you by going and entering. But we've been anticipated."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia and Maria.","id":567},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","cont":1,"lines":["Most excellent accomplished lady, the heavens rain odours on you!"],"id":568,"m":"Most excellent, accomplished lady, may the heavens rain perfumes on you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["That youth's a rare courtier: 'Rain odours'! well."],"id":569,"m":"That youth is a fine courtier. 'Rain perfumes'! Very good."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["My matter hath no voice, lady, but to your own most pregnant","and vouchsafed ear."],"id":570,"m":"My business can be spoken only to your own most receptive and gracious ear, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["'Odours,' 'pregnant,' and 'vouchsafed':—I'll get 'em all","three ready."],"id":571,"m":"'Perfumes', 'receptive' and 'gracious'. I'll learn all three of those ready for use."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Let the garden door be shut, and leave me to my hearing."],"id":572,"m":"Let the garden door be shut, and leave me to hear him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Sir Toby, Sir Andrew, and Maria.","id":573},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Give me your hand, sir."],"id":574,"m":"Give me your hand, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["My duty, madam, and most humble service."],"id":575,"m":"My duty, madam, and most humble service."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What is your name?"],"id":576,"m":"What is your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Cesario is your servant's name, fair princess."],"id":577,"m":"Cesario is your servant's name, fair princess."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["My servant, sir! 'Twas never merry world,","Since lowly feigning was call'd compliment:","You are servant to the Count Orsino, youth."],"id":578,"m":"My servant, sir! The world has never been happy since false humility was called good manners. You are Count Orsino's servant, young man."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And he is yours, and his must needs be yours;","Your servant's servant is your servant, madam."],"id":579,"m":"And he is yours, and what is his must be yours. Your servant's servant is your servant, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["For him, I think not on him: for his thoughts,","Would they were blanks rather than fill'd with me!"],"id":580,"m":"As for him, I don't think about him. As for his thoughts, I wish they were blank rather than filled with me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Madam, I come to whet your gentle thoughts","On his behalf:—"],"id":581,"m":"Madam, I have come to sharpen your gentle thoughts on his behalf..."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O, by your leave, I pray you:","I bade you never speak again of him:","But, would you undertake another suit,","I had rather hear you to solicit that","Than music from the spheres."],"id":582,"m":"Oh, excuse me, please. I told you never to speak of him again. But if you would plead for someone else, I'd rather hear you ask for that than hear the music of the heavenly spheres."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Dear lady,—"],"id":583,"m":"Dear lady..."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Give me leave, beseech you: I did send,","After the last enchantment you did here,","A ring in chase of you; so did I abuse","Myself, my servant, and, I fear me, you:","Under your hard construction must I sit;","To force that on you, in a shameful cunning,","Which you knew none of yours. What might you think?","Have you not set mine honour at the stake,","And baited it with all the unmuzzl'd thoughts","That tyrannous heart can think? To one of your receiving","Enough is shown: a cypress, not a bosom,","Hides my heart: so let me hear you speak."],"id":584,"m":"Let me speak, I beg you. After the last spell you cast here, I sent a ring after you. In doing so I wronged myself, my servant and, I'm afraid, you. I must sit under your harsh judgment for forcing on you, with shameful trickery, something you knew wasn't yours. What must you think? Haven't you tied my honour to the stake and set on it all the unmuzzled thoughts a cruel heart can think? To someone as quick as you, I've shown enough: a thin veil, not a bosom, hides my heart. So let me hear you speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I pity you."],"id":585,"m":"I pity you."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["That's a degree to love."],"id":586,"m":"That's a step towards love."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["No, not a grise; for 'tis a vulgar proof","That very oft we pity enemies."],"id":587,"m":"No, not a single step, because it's common experience that we very often pity our enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Why, then, methinks 'tis time to smile again:","O world, how apt the poor are to be proud!","If one should be a prey, how much the better","To fall before the lion than the wolf!  [Clock strikes.]","The clock upbraids me with the waste of time.—","Be not afraid, good youth, I will not have you:","And yet, when wit and youth is come to harvest,","Your wife is like to reap a proper man.","There lies your way, due-west."],"id":588,"m":"Why, then, I think it's time to smile again. Oh world, how ready the poor are to be proud! If one has to be a victim, how much better to fall to the lion than to the wolf! [Clock strikes] The clock scolds me for wasting time. Don't be afraid, good youth, I won't have you. And yet, when your wit and youth have grown to harvest, your wife is likely to reap a fine man. There's your way, due west."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Then westward-ho:","Grace and good disposition 'tend your ladyship!","You'll nothing, madam, to my lord by me?"],"id":589,"m":"Then westward ho! May grace and good spirits be with your ladyship! You have no message, madam, for my lord through me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Stay:","I pr'ythee tell me what thou think'st of me."],"id":590,"m":"Wait. Please tell me what you think of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["That you do think you are not what you are."],"id":591,"m":"That you think you are not what you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["If I think so, I think the same of you."],"id":592,"m":"If I think so, I think the same of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Then think you right; I am not what I am."],"id":593,"m":"Then you think right: I am not what I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["I would you were as I would have you be!"],"id":594,"m":"I wish you were what I would like you to be!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Would it be better, madam, than I am,","I wish it might; for now I am your fool."],"id":595,"m":"Would that be better, madam, than what I am? I hope it would, because now you're making a fool of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O what a deal of scorn looks beautiful","In the contempt and anger of his lip!","A murd'rous guilt shows not itself more soon","Than love that would seem hid: love's night is noon.","Cesario, by the roses of the spring,","By maidhood, honour, truth, and everything,","I love thee so that, maugre all thy pride,","Nor wit, nor reason, can my passion hide.","Do not extort thy reasons from this clause,","For, that I woo, thou therefore hast no cause:","But rather reason thus with reason fetter:","Love sought is good, but given unsought is better."],"id":596,"m":"Oh, how beautiful scorn looks in the contempt and anger of his lip! A murderer's guilt doesn't show itself faster than a love that tries to hide; love's night is as clear as noon. Cesario, by the roses of the spring, by maidenhood, honour, truth and everything, I love you so much that, despite all your pride, neither wit nor reason can hide my passion. Don't argue from this that, because I'm the one wooing, you have no reason to woo me. Rather, tie that reasoning with this better reason: love that is sought is good, but love given unsought is better."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["By innocence I swear, and by my youth,","I have one heart, one bosom, and one truth,","And that no woman has; nor never none","Shall mistress be of it, save I alone.","And so adieu, good madam; never more","Will I my master's tears to you deplore."],"id":597,"m":"I swear by innocence and by my youth, I have one heart, one breast and one truth, and no woman has it, nor will any woman ever be mistress of it, except me alone. And so goodbye, good madam; I will never again come to you to lament my master's tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Yet come again: for thou, perhaps, mayst move","That heart, which now abhors, to like his love."],"id":598,"m":"But come again, because perhaps you can move this heart, which hates his love now, to like it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":599},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"A Room in Olivia's House.","id":600},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch, Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek, and Fabian.","id":601},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["No, faith, I'll not stay a jot longer."],"id":602,"m":"No, honestly, I won't stay a moment longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Thy reason, dear venom: give thy reason."],"id":603,"m":"Your reason, my dear angry one; give your reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["You must needs yield your reason, Sir Andrew."],"id":604,"m":"You must give your reason, Sir Andrew."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Marry, I saw your niece do more favours to the count's","servingman than ever she bestowed upon me; I saw't i' the","orchard."],"id":605,"m":"Well, I saw your niece show more favour to the Count's servant than she has ever shown me. I saw it in the orchard."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Did she see thee the while, old boy? tell me that."],"id":606,"m":"Did she see you while she was doing it, old boy? Tell me that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["As plain as I see you now."],"id":607,"m":"As plainly as I see you now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["This was a great argument of love in her toward you."],"id":608,"m":"That was a great proof of her love for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["'Slight! will you make an ass o' me?"],"id":609,"m":"By God's light! Are you making an ass of me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["I will prove it legitimate, sir, upon the oaths of judgment","and reason."],"id":610,"m":"I'll prove it's sound, sir, on the testimony of judgment and reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["And they have been grand jurymen since before Noah was a","sailor."],"id":611,"m":"And they've been grand jurors since before Noah was a sailor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["She did show favour to the youth in your sight only to","exasperate you, to awake your dormouse valour, to put fire in","your heart and brimstone in your liver. You should then have","accosted her; and with some excellent jests, fire-new from the","mint, you should have banged the youth into dumbness. This was","looked for at your hand, and this was baulked: the double gilt of","this opportunity you let time wash off, and you are now sailed","into the north of my lady's opinion; where you will hang like an","icicle on Dutchman's beard, unless you do redeem it by some","laudable attempt either of valour or policy."],"id":612,"m":"She showed favour to the youth in front of you only to provoke you, to wake up your sleepy courage, to put fire in your heart and brimstone in your liver. You should have approached her then, and with some excellent jokes, brand new from the mint, you should have knocked the youth speechless. That's what was expected of you, and you missed it. You let time wash the double gilding off this opportunity, and now you've sailed into the cold north of my lady's opinion, where you'll hang like an icicle on a Dutchman's beard unless you redeem yourself with some praiseworthy act of courage or cunning."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["And't be any way, it must be with valour: for policy I","hate; I had as lief be a Brownist as a politician."],"id":613,"m":"If it's to be either, it must be courage, because I hate cunning. I'd as soon be a religious separatist as a schemer."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Why, then, build me thy fortunes upon the basis of","valour. Challenge me the count's youth to fight with him; hurt","him in eleven places; my niece shall take note of it: and assure","thyself there is no love-broker in the world can more prevail in","man's commendation with woman than report of valour."],"id":614,"m":"Why then, build your fortune on courage. Challenge the Count's youth to fight you. Wound him in eleven places; my niece will take note of it. And believe me, there's no matchmaker in the world who can recommend a man to a woman better than a reputation for bravery."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["There is no way but this, Sir Andrew."],"id":615,"m":"There's no other way, Sir Andrew."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Will either of you bear me a challenge to him?"],"id":616,"m":"Will either of you take a challenge to him for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Go, write it in a martial hand; be curst and brief; it is","no matter how witty, so it be eloquent and full of invention;","taunt him with the licence of ink; if thou 'thou'st' him some","thrice, it shall not be amiss; and as many lies as will lie in","thy sheet of paper, although the sheet were big enough for the","bed of Ware in England, set 'em down; go about it. Let there be","gall enough in thy ink; though thou write with a goose-pen, no","matter. About it."],"id":617,"m":"Go and write it in a soldierly hand. Be fierce and brief. It doesn't matter how witty it is, as long as it's eloquent and full of invention. Insult him with all the freedom ink gives you. If you call him 'thou' three times or so, it won't do any harm. Put down as many lies as will fit on your sheet of paper, even if the sheet were big enough for the great bed of Ware in England. Get on with it. Let there be enough bitterness in your ink, though it doesn't matter if you write with a goose quill. Get on with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Where shall I find you?"],"id":618,"m":"Where shall I find you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["We'll call thee at the cubiculo. Go."],"id":619,"m":"We'll call for you at your little room. Go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Sir Andrew.","id":620},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["This is a dear manakin to you, Sir Toby."],"id":621,"m":"He's a dear little puppet to you, Sir Toby."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I have been dear to him, lad; some two thousand strong, or so."],"id":622,"m":"I've been dear to him, lad: about two thousand ducats' worth, or so."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["We shall have a rare letter from him: but you'll not deliver it."],"id":623,"m":"We'll get a fine letter from him. But you won't deliver it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Never trust me then; and by all means stir on the youth","to an answer. I think oxen and wainropes cannot hale them","together. For Andrew, if he were opened and you find so much","blood in his liver as will clog the foot of a flea, I'll eat the","rest of the anatomy."],"id":624,"m":"Never trust me again if I don't. And by all means provoke the youth to reply. I don't think oxen and wagon ropes could drag them together. As for Andrew, if he were cut open and you found as much blood in his liver as would clog the foot of a flea, I'll eat the rest of his body."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["And his opposite, the youth, bears in his visage no great","presage of cruelty."],"id":625,"m":"And his opponent, the youth, doesn't show much sign of cruelty in his face."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Maria.","id":626},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Look where the youngest wren of nine comes."],"id":627,"m":"Look, here comes the smallest wren of the brood."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["If you desire the spleen, and will laugh yourselves into","stitches, follow me: yond gull Malvolio is turned heathen, a very","renegado; for there is no Christian, that means to be saved by","believing rightly, can ever believe such impossible passages of","grossness. He's in yellow stockings."],"id":628,"m":"If you want a fit of laughter and to laugh yourselves into stitches, follow me. That fool Malvolio has turned heathen, a real traitor to the faith, because no Christian who hopes to be saved by believing correctly could ever believe such ridiculous nonsense. He's in yellow stockings."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["And cross-gartered?"],"id":629,"m":"And cross-gartered?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Most villainously; like a pedant that keeps a school i' the","church.—I have dogged him like his murderer. He does obey every","point of the letter that I dropped to betray him. He does smile","his face into more lines than is in the new map, with the","augmentation of the Indies: you have not seen such a thing as","'tis; I can hardly forbear hurling things at him. I know my lady","will strike him; if she do, he'll smile and take't for a great","favour."],"id":630,"m":"Horribly, like a schoolmaster who teaches in the church. I've followed him like his murderer. He's obeying every detail of the letter I dropped to trap him. He smiles his face into more lines than there are on the new map with the Indies added. You've never seen anything like it. I can hardly stop myself throwing things at him. I know my lady will strike him; if she does, he'll smile and take it as a great favour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come, bring us, bring us where he is."],"id":631,"m":"Come on, take us, take us to where he is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":632},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"A street.","id":633},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antonio and Sebastian.","id":634},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I would not by my will have troubled you;","But since you make your pleasure of your pains,","I will no further chide you."],"id":635,"m":"I wouldn't willingly have troubled you; but since you take pleasure in your trouble, I won't scold you any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I could not stay behind you: my desire,","More sharp than filed steel, did spur me forth;","And not all love to see you,—though so much,","As might have drawn one to a longer voyage,—","But jealousy what might befall your travel,","Being skilless in these parts; which to a stranger,","Unguided and unfriended, often prove","Rough and unhospitable. My willing love,","The rather by these arguments of fear,","Set forth in your pursuit."],"id":636,"m":"I couldn't stay behind. My desire, sharper than filed steel, drove me on. It wasn't only love of seeing you, though that was strong enough to make a man take a longer voyage, but anxiety about what might happen to you on your travels, since you don't know these parts, which often prove rough and unwelcoming to a stranger without a guide or friends. My willing love, made keener by these fears, set out after you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["My kind Antonio,","I can no other answer make but thanks,","And thanks, and ever thanks. Often good turns","Are shuffled off with such uncurrent pay;","But were my worth, as is my conscience, firm,","You should find better dealing. What's to do?","Shall we go see the reliques of this town?"],"id":637,"m":"My kind Antonio, I can give no answer but thanks, and thanks, and always thanks. Good deeds are often brushed off with such worthless payment; but if my wealth were as solid as my sense of obligation, you would be better rewarded. What shall we do? Shall we go and see the sights of this town?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["To-morrow, sir; best, first, go see your lodging."],"id":638,"m":"Tomorrow, sir. It's best to go and find your lodgings first."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I am not weary, and 'tis long to night;","I pray you, let us satisfy our eyes","With the memorials and the things of fame","That do renown this city."],"id":639,"m":"I'm not tired, and it's a long time till night. Please, let's feast our eyes on the monuments and famous things that make this city renowned."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Would you'd pardon me;","I do not without danger walk these streets:","Once in a sea-fight, 'gainst the count, his galleys,","I did some service; of such note, indeed,","That, were I ta'en here, it would scarce be answered."],"id":640,"m":"Please excuse me. It's dangerous for me to walk these streets. I once fought in a sea battle against the Count's galleys, and did such notable damage that if I were captured here I'd hardly be able to defend myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Belike you slew great number of his people."],"id":641,"m":"I suppose you killed a great number of his people."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["The offence is not of such a bloody nature;","Albeit the quality of the time and quarrel","Might well have given us bloody argument.","It might have since been answered in repaying","What we took from them; which, for traffic's sake,","Most of our city did: only myself stood out;","For which, if I be lapsed in this place,","I shall pay dear."],"id":642,"m":"The offence wasn't so bloody, although the circumstances of the time and the quarrel could well have given us cause for bloodshed. It could have been settled since by repaying what we took from them, which most of our city did for the sake of trade. Only I refused; and if I'm caught here, I'll pay dearly for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Do not then walk too open."],"id":643,"m":"Then don't walk about too openly."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["It doth not fit me. Hold, sir, here's my purse;","In the south suburbs, at the Elephant,","Is best to lodge: I will bespeak our diet","Whiles you beguile the time and feed your knowledge","With viewing of the town; there shall you have me."],"id":644,"m":"It wouldn't suit me. Wait, sir, here's my purse. The best place to stay is at the Elephant, in the southern suburbs. I'll order our meals while you pass the time and improve your knowledge by seeing the town. You'll find me there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Why I your purse?"],"id":645,"m":"Why should I have your purse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Haply your eye shall light upon some toy","You have desire to purchase; and your store,","I think, is not for idle markets, sir."],"id":646,"m":"Perhaps your eye will light on some trinket you'd like to buy, and I don't think your funds are meant for luxuries, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I'll be your purse-bearer, and leave you for an hour."],"id":647,"m":"I'll carry your purse and leave you for an hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["To the Elephant.—"],"id":648,"m":"To the Elephant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I do remember."],"id":649,"m":"I'll remember."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":650},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Olivia's garden.","id":651},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia and Maria.","id":652},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["I have sent after him. He says he'll come;","How shall I feast him? what bestow on him?","For youth is bought more oft than begged or borrowed.","I speak too loud.—","Where's Malvolio?—He is sad and civil,","And suits well for a servant with my fortunes;—","Where is Malvolio?"],"id":653,"m":"I've sent for him; he says he'll come. How shall I entertain him? What shall I give him? Youth is bought more often than it's begged or borrowed. I'm speaking too loudly. Where's Malvolio? He is serious and respectful, and suits a servant for someone in my situation. Where is Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["He's coming, madam:","But in very strange manner. He is sure possessed."],"id":654,"m":"He's coming, madam, but in a very strange manner. He must be possessed."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Why, what's the matter? does he rave?"],"id":655,"m":"Why, what's the matter? Is he raving?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["No, madam, he does nothing but smile: your ladyship were","best to have some guard about you if he come;","For, sure, the man is tainted in his wits."],"id":656,"m":"No, madam, he does nothing but smile. Your ladyship had better have someone to protect you if he comes, because the man is certainly sick in his mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Go call him hither.—I'm as mad as he,","If sad and merry madness equal be.—"],"id":657,"m":"Go and call him here. I'm as mad as he is, if sad madness and merry madness are equal."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Malvolio.","id":658},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Malvolio?"],"id":659,"m":"Well now, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sweet lady, ho, ho."],"id":660,"m":"Sweet lady, ho, ho."},{"t":"sd","text":"Smiles fantastically.","id":661},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Smil'st thou?","I sent for thee upon a sad occasion."],"id":662,"m":"Are you smiling? I sent for you on a serious matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sad, lady? I could be sad: this does make some","obstruction in the blood, this cross-gartering. But what of that?","If it please the eye of one, it is with me as the very true","sonnet is: 'Please one and please all.'"],"id":663,"m":"Serious, lady? I could be serious. This cross-gartering does cause some blockage in the blood. But what does that matter? If it pleases the eye of one person, then it's with me as the very true poem says: 'Please one and please all.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Why, how dost thou, man? what is the matter with thee?"],"id":664,"m":"Why, how are you, man? What's the matter with you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Not black in my mind, though yellow in my legs.","It did come to his hands, and commands shall be executed.","I think we do know the sweet Roman hand."],"id":665,"m":"Not black in my mind, though yellow in my legs. It did come into his hands, and commands shall be carried out. I think we know that sweet Italian handwriting."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Wilt thou go to bed, Malvolio?"],"id":666,"m":"Do you want to go to bed, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["To bed? ay, sweetheart; and I'll come to thee."],"id":667,"m":"To bed? Yes, sweetheart, and I'll come to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["God comfort thee! Why dost thou smile so, and kiss thy hand so","oft?"],"id":668,"m":"God help you! Why do you smile like that and kiss your hand so often?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["How do you, Malvolio?"],"id":669,"m":"How are you, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["At your request? Yes; nightingales answer daws."],"id":670,"m":"Answer you? Yes; nightingales answer jackdaws."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Why appear you with this ridiculous boldness before my lady?"],"id":671,"m":"Why do you appear before my lady with this ridiculous boldness?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Be not afraid of greatness':—'twas well writ."],"id":672,"m":"'Don't be afraid of greatness.' That was well written."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What meanest thou by that, Malvolio?"],"id":673,"m":"What do you mean by that, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Some are born great,'—"],"id":674,"m":"'Some are born great...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Ha?"],"id":675,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Some achieve greatness,'—"],"id":676,"m":"'Some achieve greatness...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What say'st thou?"],"id":677,"m":"What are you saying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'And some have greatness thrust upon them.'"],"id":678,"m":"'And some have greatness thrust upon them.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Heaven restore thee!"],"id":679,"m":"Heaven heal you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Remember who commended thy yellow stockings;'—"],"id":680,"m":"'Remember who praised your yellow stockings...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Thy yellow stockings?"],"id":681,"m":"Your yellow stockings?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'And wished to see thee cross-gartered.'"],"id":682,"m":"'And wished to see you cross-gartered.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Cross-gartered?"],"id":683,"m":"Cross-gartered?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'Go to: thou art made, if thou desirest to be so:'—"],"id":684,"m":"'Go on, your fortune is made, if you want it to be...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Am I made?"],"id":685,"m":"Is my fortune made?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["'If not, let me see thee a servant still.'"],"id":686,"m":"'If not, let me see you a servant still.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Why, this is very midsummer madness."],"id":687,"m":"Why, this is pure midsummer madness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Servant.","id":688},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Madam, the young gentleman of the Count Orsino's is","returned; I could hardly entreat him back; he attends your","ladyship's pleasure."],"id":689,"m":"Madam, Count Orsino's young gentleman has come back. I could hardly persuade him to return. He's waiting for your ladyship."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["I'll come to him."],"id":690,"m":"I'll come to him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":691},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Good Maria, let this fellow be looked to. Where's my cousin Toby?","Let some of my people have a special care of him; I would not","have him miscarry for the half of my dowry."],"id":692,"m":"Good Maria, make sure this fellow is looked after. Where's my cousin Toby? Let some of my servants take special care of him; I wouldn't want him harmed for half my fortune."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Olivia and Maria.","id":693},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["O, ho! do you come near me now? No worse man than Sir","Toby to look to me? This concurs directly with the letter: she","sends him on purpose, that I may appear stubborn to him; for she","incites me to that in the letter. 'Cast thy humble slough,' says","she;—'be opposite with a kinsman, surly with servants,—let thy","tongue tang with arguments of state,—put thyself into the trick","of singularity;—and consequently, sets down the manner how; as,","a sad face, a reverend carriage, a slow tongue, in the habit of","some sir of note, and so forth. I have limed her; but it is","Jove's doing, and Jove make me thankful! And, when she went away","now, 'Let this fellow be looked to;' Fellow! not Malvolio, nor","after my degree, but fellow. Why, everything adheres together;","that no dram of a scruple, no scruple of a scruple, no obstacle,","no incredulous or unsafe circumstance,—What can be said?","Nothing, that can be, can come between me and the full prospect","of my hopes. Well, Jove, not I, is the doer of this, and he is to","be thanked."],"id":694,"m":"Oh ho! Do you understand me now? No lesser man than Sir Toby to look after me? This agrees exactly with the letter. She's sending him on purpose so that I can be rude to him, because she urges me to that in the letter. 'Throw off your humble skin,' she says, 'be hostile to a relative, rude to servants; let your tongue ring out political arguments; adopt the habit of being different.' And then she sets down how to do it: a serious face, a dignified bearing, slow speech, in the manner of some important gentleman, and so on. I've caught her like a bird in lime; but it's Jove's doing, and may Jove make me thankful! And when she left just now, 'Let this fellow be looked after.' Fellow! Not Malvolio, not according to my rank, but fellow, meaning companion. Why, everything fits together, so that not a scrap of doubt, not a scrap of a scrap, no obstacle, no unlikely or uncertain circumstance... What can be said? Nothing that exists can come between me and the full view of my hopes. Well, Jove, not I, has done this, and he is to be thanked."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Maria, with Sir Toby Belch and Fabian.","id":695},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Which way is he, in the name of sanctity? If all the","devils of hell be drawn in little, and Legion himself possessed","him, yet I'll speak to him."],"id":696,"m":"Where is he, in the name of all that's holy? Even if all the devils in hell were squeezed into him, and Legion himself possessed him, I'll still speak to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Here he is, here he is:—How is't with you, sir? how is't with","you, man?"],"id":697,"m":"Here he is, here he is. How are you, sir? How are you, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Go off; I discard you; let me enjoy my private; go off."],"id":698,"m":"Go away. I dismiss you. Let me enjoy my privacy. Go away."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Lo, how hollow the fiend speaks within him! did not I tell","you?—Sir Toby, my lady prays you to have a care of him."],"id":699,"m":"Listen, how hollow the devil sounds inside him! Didn't I tell you? Sir Toby, my lady asks you to look after him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Ah, ha! does she so?"],"id":700,"m":"Aha! Does she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Go to, go to; peace, peace, we must deal gently with him;","let me alone. How do you, Malvolio? how is't with you? What, man!","defy the devil: consider, he's an enemy to mankind."],"id":701,"m":"Come now, come now. Quiet, quiet, we must deal gently with him. Leave him to me. How are you, Malvolio? How is it with you? Come, man, defy the devil! Remember, he's the enemy of mankind."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Do you know what you say?"],"id":702,"m":"Do you know what you're saying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["La you, an you speak ill of the devil, how he takes it at","heart! Pray God he be not bewitched."],"id":703,"m":"Look at that: if you speak ill of the devil, how he takes it to heart! Pray God he isn't bewitched."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Carry his water to the wise woman."],"id":704,"m":"Take a sample of his urine to the wise woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Marry, and it shall be done to-morrow morning, if I live. My","lady would not lose him for more than I'll say."],"id":705,"m":"Indeed, it will be done tomorrow morning, if I live. My lady wouldn't lose him for more than I can say."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["How now, mistress!"],"id":706,"m":"What's this, mistress!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["O lord!"],"id":707,"m":"Oh Lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Pr'ythee hold thy peace; this is not the way. Do you not","see you move him? let me alone with him."],"id":708,"m":"Please be quiet; this isn't the way. Don't you see you're upsetting him? Leave him to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["No way but gentleness; gently, gently: the fiend is rough,","and will not be roughly used."],"id":709,"m":"Only gentleness will work. Gently, gently. The devil is rough and won't be treated roughly."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Why, how now, my bawcock? how dost thou, chuck."],"id":710,"m":"Why, how are you, my fine fellow? How are you, chick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sir?"],"id":711,"m":"Sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Ay, Biddy, come with me. What, man! 'tis not for gravity","to play at cherry-pit with Satan. Hang him, foul collier!"],"id":712,"m":"Yes, chicken, come with me. Come, man! It's not right for a serious man like you to play children's games with Satan. Hang him, the filthy coal-man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Get him to say his prayers; good Sir Toby, get him to pray."],"id":713,"m":"Get him to say his prayers. Good Sir Toby, get him to pray."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["My prayers, minx?"],"id":714,"m":"My prayers, you hussy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["No, I warrant you, he will not hear of godliness."],"id":715,"m":"No, I promise you, he won't listen to anything godly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Go, hang yourselves all! you are idle shallow things: I","am not of your element; you shall know more hereafter."],"id":716,"m":"Go and hang yourselves, all of you! You are idle, shallow things. I am not of your kind. You will hear more later."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":717},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Is't possible?"],"id":718,"m":"Is it possible?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["If this were played upon a stage now, I could condemn it as","an improbable fiction."],"id":719,"m":"If this were acted on a stage now, I would condemn it as an unbelievable story."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["His very genius hath taken the infection of the device, man."],"id":720,"m":"His very soul has caught the infection of our trick, man."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Nay, pursue him now; lest the device take air and taint."],"id":721,"m":"No, go after him now, in case the trick is exposed to the air and goes bad."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Why, we shall make him mad indeed."],"id":722,"m":"Why, we'll really make him mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["The house will be the quieter."],"id":723,"m":"The house will be quieter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come, we'll have him in a dark room and bound. My niece","is already in the belief that he's mad; we may carry it thus, for","our pleasure and his penance, till our very pastime, tired out of","breath, prompt us to have mercy on him: at which time we will","bring the device to the bar, and crown thee for a finder of","madmen. But see, but see."],"id":724,"m":"Come on, we'll put him in a dark room and tie him up. My niece already believes he's mad. We can keep it going like this, for our fun and his punishment, until our sport itself, worn out of breath, makes us take pity on him. Then we'll bring our plot before the court and crown you as a discoverer of madmen. But look, look."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek.","id":725},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["More matter for a May morning."],"id":726,"m":"More entertainment for a holiday."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Here's the challenge, read it; I warrant there's vinegar and","pepper in't."],"id":727,"m":"Here's the challenge. Read it. I promise you there's vinegar and pepper in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Is't so saucy?"],"id":728,"m":"Is it so spicy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Ay, is't, I warrant him; do but read."],"id":729,"m":"Yes, it is, I promise. Just read it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Give me. [Reads.] 'Youth, whatsoever thou art, thou art but a","scurvy fellow.'"],"id":730,"m":"Give it to me. [Reads] 'Youth, whatever you are, you are only a scurvy fellow.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Good and valiant."],"id":731,"m":"Good and brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Wonder not, nor admire not in thy mind, why I do","call thee so, for I will show thee no reason for't.'"],"id":732,"m":"[Reads] 'Don't wonder or be amazed in your mind why I call you that, because I won't give you any reason for it.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["A good note: that keeps you from the blow of the law."],"id":733,"m":"A good point; that keeps you safe from the law."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Thou comest to the Lady Olivia, and in my sight","she uses thee kindly: but thou liest in thy throat; that is not","the matter I challenge thee for.'"],"id":734,"m":"[Reads] 'You come to Lady Olivia, and in front of me she treats you kindly. But you lie in your throat; that isn't what I'm challenging you for.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Very brief, and to exceeding good senseless."],"id":735,"m":"Very brief, and exceedingly good... nonsense."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'I will waylay thee going home; where if it be","thy chance to kill me,'—"],"id":736,"m":"[Reads] 'I will ambush you on your way home, where, if you happen to kill me...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Good."],"id":737,"m":"Good."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Thou kill'st me like a rogue and a villain.'"],"id":738,"m":"[Reads] '...you kill me like a rogue and a villain.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Still you keep o' the windy side of the law. Good."],"id":739,"m":"You still keep on the safe side of the law. Good."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["'Fare thee well; and God have mercy upon one of","our souls! He may have mercy upon mine; but my hope is better,","and so look to thyself. Thy friend, as thou usest him, and thy","sworn enemy, Andrew Ague-Cheek.'","If this letter move him not, his legs cannot: I'll give't him."],"id":740,"m":"[Reads] 'Farewell, and God have mercy on one of our souls! He may have mercy on mine, but I have better hopes, so look out for yourself. Your friend, depending on how you treat him, and your sworn enemy, Andrew Aguecheek.' If this letter doesn't move him, his legs can't. I'll give it to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["You may have very fit occasion for't; he is now in some","commerce with my lady, and will by and by depart."],"id":741,"m":"You may have a very good opportunity for it. He's talking with my lady now and will leave soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Go, Sir Andrew; scout me for him at the corner of the","orchard, like a bum-bailiff; so soon as ever thou seest him,","draw; and as thou drawest, swear horrible; for it comes to pass","oft that a terrible oath, with a swaggering accent sharply","twanged off, gives manhood more approbation than ever proof","itself would have earned him. Away."],"id":742,"m":"Go, Sir Andrew. Lie in wait for him at the corner of the orchard, like a bailiff. As soon as you see him, draw your sword, and as you draw, swear horribly, because it often happens that a terrible oath, delivered sharply in a swaggering voice, wins a man more of a reputation for courage than actually proving it would ever have earned him. Off you go."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Nay, let me alone for swearing."],"id":743,"m":"No, leave the swearing to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":744},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Now will not I deliver his letter; for the behaviour of","the young gentleman gives him out to be of good capacity and","breeding; his employment between his lord and my niece confirms","no less; therefore this letter, being so excellently ignorant,","will breed no terror in the youth: he will find it comes from a","clodpole. But, sir, I will deliver his challenge by word of","mouth, set upon Ague-cheek notable report of valour, and drive","the gentleman,—as I know his youth will aptly receive it,—into","a most hideous opinion of his rage, skill, fury, and impetuosity.","This will so fright them both that they will kill one another by","the look, like cockatrices."],"id":745,"m":"Now I won't deliver his letter, because the young gentleman's behaviour shows he is intelligent and well brought up; his role as go-between for his lord and my niece confirms that. So this letter, being so splendidly ignorant, won't frighten the youth at all; he'll realise it comes from a blockhead. Instead, sir, I'll deliver his challenge in person, give Aguecheek a great reputation for courage, and drive the gentleman, since I know his youth will easily believe it, into a terrifying opinion of his rage, skill, fury and violence. This will frighten them both so much that they'll kill each other with a look, like basilisks."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia and Viola.","id":746},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Here he comes with your niece; give them way till he take","leave, and presently after him."],"id":747,"m":"Here he comes with your niece. Keep out of their way until he says goodbye, and then go straight after him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I will meditate the while upon some horrid message for a","challenge."],"id":748,"m":"Meanwhile I'll think up some horrible message for a challenge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Sir Toby, Fabian, and Maria.","id":749},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["I have said too much unto a heart of stone,","And laid mine honour too unchary on it:","There's something in me that reproves my fault;","But such a headstrong potent fault it is","That it but mocks reproof."],"id":750,"m":"I have said too much to a heart of stone and risked my honour too carelessly on it. There's something in me that blames me for my fault, but it is such a stubborn, powerful fault that it just laughs at blame."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["With the same 'haviour that your passion bears","Goes on my master's griefs."],"id":751,"m":"My master's griefs carry on in exactly the same way as your passion."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Here, wear this jewel for me; 'tis my picture;","Refuse it not; it hath no tongue to vex you:","And, I beseech you, come again to-morrow.","What shall you ask of me that I'll deny,","That, honour saved, may upon asking give?"],"id":752,"m":"Here, wear this jewel for me; it's my portrait. Don't refuse it; it has no tongue to annoy you. And I beg you, come again tomorrow. What could you ask of me that I would refuse, if I could grant it without losing my honour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Nothing but this, your true love for my master."],"id":753,"m":"Only this: your true love for my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["How with mine honour may I give him that","Which I have given to you?"],"id":754,"m":"How can I honourably give him what I have given to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I will acquit you."],"id":755,"m":"I will release you from that."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Well, come again to-morrow. Fare thee well;","A fiend like thee might bear my soul to hell."],"id":756,"m":"Well, come again tomorrow. Goodbye. A devil like you could carry my soul to hell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":757},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Sir Toby Belch and Fabian.","id":758},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Gentleman, God save thee."],"id":759,"m":"Gentleman, God save you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And you, sir."],"id":760,"m":"And you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["That defence thou hast, betake thee to't. Of what nature","the wrongs are thou hast done him, I know not; but thy","intercepter, full of despite, bloody as the hunter, attends","thee at the orchard end: dismount thy tuck, be yare in thy","preparation, for thy assailant is quick, skilful, and deadly."],"id":761,"m":"Whatever means of defence you have, get ready to use it. I don't know what wrongs you have done him, but the man waiting to ambush you, full of spite and as bloodthirsty as a hunter, is waiting for you at the end of the orchard. Draw your rapier and be quick in getting ready, because your attacker is quick, skilful and deadly."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["You mistake, sir; I am sure no man hath any quarrel to me;","my remembrance is very free and clear from any image of offence","done to any man."],"id":762,"m":"You're mistaken, sir. I'm sure no man has any quarrel with me. My memory is completely free and clear of any offence I've done to anyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["You'll find it otherwise, I assure you: therefore, if you","hold your life at any price, betake you to your guard; for your","opposite hath in him what youth, strength, skill, and wrath, can","furnish man withal."],"id":763,"m":"You'll find otherwise, I assure you. So if you value your life at all, get on your guard, because your opponent has everything that youth, strength, skill and rage can give a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I pray you, sir, what is he?"],"id":764,"m":"Please, sir, who is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["He is knight, dubbed with unhacked rapier and on carpet","consideration; but he is a devil in private brawl; souls and","bodies hath he divorced three; and his incensement at this moment","is so implacable that satisfaction can be none but by pangs of","death and sepulchre: hob, nob is his word; give't or take't."],"id":765,"m":"He's a knight, dubbed with an unused sword for services at court, not in battle; but he's a devil in a private fight. He has separated three souls from their bodies, and his anger at this moment is so fierce that nothing will satisfy it but the pangs of death and the grave. 'Hit or miss' is his motto: give it or take it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I will return again into the house and desire some conduct","of the lady. I am no fighter. I have heard of some kind of men","that put quarrels purposely on others to taste their valour:","belike this is a man of that quirk."],"id":766,"m":"I'll go back into the house and ask the lady for an escort. I'm no fighter. I've heard of men who deliberately pick quarrels with others to test their courage; this must be a man with that quirk."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Sir, no; his indignation derives itself out of a very","competent injury; therefore, get you on and give him his desire.","Back you shall not to the house, unless you undertake that with","me which with as much safety you might answer him: therefore on,","or strip your sword stark naked; for meddle you must, that's","certain, or forswear to wear iron about you."],"id":767,"m":"No, sir. His anger comes from a very real injury, so go on and give him what he wants. You won't go back to the house unless you take on with me what you could just as safely take on with him. So go on, or strip your sword naked, because fight you must, that's certain, or else give up wearing a sword at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["This is as uncivil as strange. I beseech you, do me this","courteous office as to know of the knight what my offence to him","is; it is something of my negligence, nothing of my purpose."],"id":768,"m":"This is as rude as it is strange. I beg you, do me the courtesy of finding out from the knight what my offence against him is. It must be something I did carelessly, nothing I did on purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I will do so. Signior Fabian, stay you by this gentleman","till my return."],"id":769,"m":"I will. Signor Fabian, stay with this gentleman until I come back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Sir Toby.","id":770},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Pray you, sir, do you know of this matter?"],"id":771,"m":"Please, sir, do you know anything about this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["I know the knight is incensed against you, even to a mortal","arbitrement; but nothing of the circumstance more."],"id":772,"m":"I know the knight is furious with you, even to the point of fighting to the death, but I know nothing more about the circumstances."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I beseech you, what manner of man is he?"],"id":773,"m":"I beg you, what kind of man is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Nothing of that wonderful promise, to read him by his form,","as you are like to find him in the proof of his valour. He is","indeed, sir, the most skilful, bloody, and fatal opposite that","you could possibly have found in any part of Illyria. Will you","walk towards him? I will make your peace with him if I can."],"id":774,"m":"Judging by his appearance, he doesn't look as remarkable as you're likely to find him when he proves his courage. He is indeed, sir, the most skilful, bloodthirsty and deadly opponent you could possibly have found anywhere in Illyria. Will you walk towards him? I'll make peace for you if I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I shall be much bound to you for't. I am one that would","rather go with sir priest than sir knight: I care not who knows","so much of my mettle."],"id":775,"m":"I'll be very grateful to you for it. I'm someone who would rather keep company with a priest than a knight. I don't care who knows that much about my character."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":776},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Sir Toby with Sir Andrew.","id":777},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Why, man, he's a very devil; I have not seen such a","virago. I had a pass with him, rapier, scabbard, and all, and he","gives me the stuck-in with such a mortal motion that it is","inevitable; and on the answer, he pays you as surely as your feet","hit the ground they step on. They say he has been fencer to the","Sophy."],"id":778,"m":"Why, man, he's a real devil. I've never seen such a fierce fighter. I had a practice bout with him, rapier, scabbard and all, and he lunged at me with such a deadly movement that it was impossible to avoid. And when he parries, he hits you back as surely as your feet hit the ground they walk on. They say he was the Shah of Persia's fencing master."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Pox on't, I'll not meddle with him."],"id":779,"m":"Damn it, I'll have nothing to do with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Ay, but he will not now be pacified: Fabian can scarce","hold him yonder."],"id":780,"m":"Yes, but he won't be calmed down now. Fabian can hardly hold him back over there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Plague on't; an I thought he had been valiant, and so","cunning in fence, I'd have seen him damned ere I'd have","challenged him. Let him let the matter slip and I'll give him","my horse, grey Capilet."],"id":781,"m":"Plague on it! If I'd thought he was brave and so skilled at fencing, I'd have seen him damned before I challenged him. If he'll let the matter drop, I'll give him my horse, grey Capilet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I'll make the motion. Stand here, make a good show on't;","this shall end without the perdition of souls. [Aside.] Marry,","I'll ride your horse as well as I ride you."],"id":782,"m":"I'll make the offer. Stand here and put on a good show. This will end without anyone losing their soul. [Aside] Indeed, I'll ride your horse as well as I ride you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Fabian and Viola.","id":783},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","cont":1,"lines":["I have his horse [To Fabian.] to take up the quarrel; I have","persuaded him the youth's a devil."],"id":784,"m":"[To Fabian] I've got his horse to settle the quarrel. I've persuaded him the youth's a devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["He is as horribly conceited of him; and pants and looks pale, as","if a bear were at his heels."],"id":785,"m":"The youth has just as horrible an idea of him, and pants and looks pale, as if a bear were at his heels."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["There's no remedy, sir: he will fight with you for's oath sake:","marry, he hath better bethought him of his quarrel, and he finds","that now scarce to be worth talking of: therefore, draw for the","supportance of his vow; he protests he will not hurt you."],"id":786,"m":"There's no help for it, sir. He'll fight with you because of his oath. Though he's thought better of his quarrel and finds now that it's hardly worth talking about. So draw, to let him keep his vow; he swears he won't hurt you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["[Aside] Pray God defend me! A little thing would make me","tell them how much I lack of a man."],"id":787,"m":"[Aside] God protect me! It wouldn't take much to make me tell them how far I am from being a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Give ground if you see him furious."],"id":788,"m":"Back away if you see him getting furious."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come, Sir Andrew, there's no remedy; the gentleman will,","for his honour's sake, have one bout with you: he cannot by the","duello avoid it; but he has promised me, as he is a gentleman and","a soldier, he will not hurt you. Come on: to't."],"id":789,"m":"Come on, Sir Andrew, there's no help for it. The gentleman will have one bout with you for the sake of his honour; by the rules of duelling he can't avoid it. But he has promised me, as he's a gentleman and a soldier, that he won't hurt you. Come on, to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Pray God he keep his oath!","[Draws.]"],"id":790,"m":"Pray God he keeps his promise! [He draws]"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antonio.","id":791},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I do assure you 'tis against my will.","[Draws.]"],"id":792,"m":"I assure you it's against my will. [She draws]"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Put up your sword:—if this young gentleman","Have done offence, I take the fault on me;","If you offend him, I for him defy you.","[Drawing.]"],"id":793,"m":"Put away your sword. If this young gentleman has done anything wrong, I take the blame on myself. If you are the one wronging him, I challenge you on his behalf. [He draws]"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["You, sir! why, what are you?"],"id":794,"m":"You, sir! Why, who are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["One, sir, that for his love dares yet do more","Than you have heard him brag to you he will."],"id":795,"m":"Someone, sir, who for love of him dares to do even more than you've heard him boast to you that he will."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Nay, if you be an undertaker, I am for you.","[Draws.]"],"id":796,"m":"Well, if you're going to take on other people's fights, I'm ready for you. [He draws]"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Officers.","id":797},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["O good Sir Toby, hold; here come the officers."],"id":798,"m":"Oh good Sir Toby, stop! Here come the officers."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["[To Antonio] I'll be with you anon."],"id":799,"m":"[To Antonio] I'll deal with you later."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["[To Sir Andrew.] Pray, sir, put your sword up, if you please."],"id":800,"m":"[To Sir Andrew] Please, sir, put your sword away, if you don't mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Marry, will I, sir; and for that I promised you, I'll be","as good as my word. He will bear you easily and reins well."],"id":801,"m":"Indeed I will, sir. And as for what I promised you, I'll be as good as my word. He's an easy ride and responds well to the reins."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["This is the man; do thy office."],"id":802,"m":"This is the man. Do your duty."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OFFICER","lines":["Antonio, I arrest thee at the suit","Of Count Orsino."],"id":803,"m":"Antonio, I arrest you on the orders of Count Orsino."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["You do mistake me, sir."],"id":804,"m":"You've mistaken me for someone else, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["No, sir, no jot; I know your favour well,","Though now you have no sea-cap on your head.—","Take him away; he knows I know him well."],"id":805,"m":"No, sir, not at all. I know your face well, though you don't have a sailor's cap on your head now. Take him away; he knows I know him well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I must obey.—This comes with seeking you;","But there's no remedy; I shall answer it.","What will you do? Now my necessity","Makes me to ask you for my purse. It grieves me","Much more for what I cannot do for you","Than what befalls myself. You stand amazed;","But be of comfort."],"id":806,"m":"I must obey. [To Viola] This comes of looking for you; but there's no help for it. I'll have to answer for it. What will you do? Now my need forces me to ask you for my purse. I'm much more upset about what I can't do for you than about what happens to me. You look amazed, but cheer up."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OFFICER","lines":["Come, sir, away."],"id":807,"m":"Come on, sir, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I must entreat of you some of that money."],"id":808,"m":"I must ask you for some of that money."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["What money, sir?","For the fair kindness you have showed me here,","And part being prompted by your present trouble,","Out of my lean and low ability","I'll lend you something; my having is not much;","I'll make division of my present with you:","Hold, there is half my coffer."],"id":809,"m":"What money, sir? Because of the kindness you've shown me here, and partly moved by your present trouble, I'll lend you something from my small and meagre means. I don't have much; I'll share what I have with you. Here, that's half my money."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Will you deny me now?","Is't possible that my deserts to you","Can lack persuasion? Do not tempt my misery,","Lest that it make me so unsound a man","As to upbraid you with those kindnesses","That I have done for you."],"id":810,"m":"Are you going to deny me now? Is it possible that what I've done for you can fail to persuade you? Don't push my misery too far, in case it makes me so ungentlemanly that I reproach you with the kindnesses I've done for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["I know of none,","Nor know I you by voice or any feature:","I hate ingratitude more in a man","Than lying, vainness, babbling, drunkenness,","Or any taint of vice whose strong corruption","Inhabits our frail blood."],"id":811,"m":"I don't know of any, and I don't know you by your voice or by any of your features. I hate ingratitude in a man more than lying, vanity, chattering, drunkenness, or any trace of vice whose strong corruption lives in our weak flesh."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["O heavens themselves!"],"id":812,"m":"Oh, heavens above!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OFFICER","lines":["Come, sir, I pray you go."],"id":813,"m":"Come on, sir, please go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Let me speak a little. This youth that you see here","I snatched one half out of the jaws of death,","Relieved him with such sanctity of love,—","And to his image, which methought did promise","Most venerable worth, did I devotion."],"id":814,"m":"Let me speak a little. This youth you see here I snatched half dead from the jaws of death, cared for him with such holy love, and worshipped his image, which I thought promised the most honourable worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["What's that to us? The time goes by; away."],"id":815,"m":"What's that to us? Time is passing. Away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["But O how vile an idol proves this god!","Thou hast, Sebastian, done good feature shame.","In nature there's no blemish but the mind;","None can be call'd deform'd but the unkind:","Virtue is beauty; but the beauteous-evil","Are empty trunks, o'erflourished by the devil."],"id":816,"m":"But oh, how vile an idol this god turns out to be! Sebastian, you have shamed your good looks. In nature there's no flaw except in the mind; no one can be called deformed except the unkind. Virtue is beauty; but people who are beautiful and evil are empty chests, decorated all over by the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["The man grows mad; away with him. Come, come, sir."],"id":817,"m":"The man's going mad. Away with him! Come on, come on, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Lead me on."],"id":818,"m":"Lead me away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Officers with Antonio.","id":819},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Methinks his words do from such passion fly","That he believes himself; so do not I.","Prove true, imagination; O prove true,","That I, dear brother, be now ta'en for you!"],"id":820,"m":"His words come from such strong feeling that he believes them himself. I don't, yet. Imagination, prove true; oh, prove true, that I, dear brother, have just been mistaken for you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come hither, knight; come hither, Fabian; we'll whisper","o'er a couplet or two of most sage saws."],"id":821,"m":"Come here, knight; come here, Fabian. We'll whisper a wise saying or two."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["He named Sebastian; I my brother know","Yet living in my glass; even such and so","In favour was my brother; and he went","Still in this fashion, colour, ornament,","For him I imitate. O, if it prove,","Tempests are kind, and salt waves fresh in love!"],"id":822,"m":"He named Sebastian. I know my brother is still alive in my mirror. My brother looked exactly like this, and he always went dressed in this style, colour and decoration, because it's him I'm imitating. Oh, if it proves true, storms are kind, and salt waves are fresh with love!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":823},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["A very dishonest paltry boy, and more a coward than a","hare: his dishonesty appears in leaving his friend here in","necessity, and denying him; and for his cowardship, ask Fabian."],"id":824,"m":"A very dishonourable, worthless boy, and more of a coward than a hare. His dishonour shows in leaving his friend here in need and disowning him; and as for his cowardice, ask Fabian."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["A coward, a most devout coward, religious in it."],"id":825,"m":"A coward, a most devoted coward, religious about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["'Slid, I'll after him again and beat him."],"id":826,"m":"By God's eyelid, I'll go after him again and beat him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Do, cuff him soundly, but never draw thy sword."],"id":827,"m":"Do. Thump him soundly, but never draw your sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["And I do not,—"],"id":828,"m":"If I don't..."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":829},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Come, let's see the event."],"id":830,"m":"Come on, let's see what happens."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["I dare lay any money 'twill be nothing yet."],"id":831,"m":"I'll bet any money it will come to nothing yet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":832},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The Street before Olivia's House.","id":833},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sebastian and Feste.","id":834},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Will you make me believe that I am not sent for you?"],"id":835,"m":"Are you trying to make me believe I wasn't sent for you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Go to, go to, thou art a foolish fellow;","Let me be clear of thee."],"id":836,"m":"Come on, come on, you're a foolish fellow. Leave me alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Well held out, i' faith! No, I do not know you; nor I am not","sent to you by my lady, to bid you come speak with her; nor your","name is not Master Cesario; nor this is not my nose neither.—","Nothing that is so is so."],"id":837,"m":"Well kept up, honestly! No, I don't know you; and I haven't been sent to you by my lady to ask you to come and speak with her; and your name isn't Master Cesario; and this isn't my nose either. Nothing that is so is so."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I pr'ythee vent thy folly somewhere else. Thou know'st not me."],"id":838,"m":"Please go and vent your foolery somewhere else. You don't know me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Vent my folly! he has heard that word of some great man, and","now applies it to a fool. Vent my folly! I am afraid this great","lubber, the world, will prove a cockney.—I pr'ythee now, ungird","thy strangeness, and tell me what I shall vent to my lady. Shall","I vent to her that thou art coming?"],"id":839,"m":"Vent my folly! He's heard that word from some great man and now he applies it to a fool. Vent my folly! I'm afraid this great lump, the world, will turn out to be a sissy. Please now, drop your strange act and tell me what I shall vent to my lady. Shall I vent to her that you're coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I pr'ythee, foolish Greek, depart from me;","There's money for thee; if you tarry longer","I shall give worse payment."],"id":840,"m":"Please, you silly joker, go away. There's money for you. If you stay any longer I'll pay you worse."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["By my troth, thou hast an open hand:—These wise men that","give fools money get themselves a good report after fourteen","years' purchase."],"id":841,"m":"Honestly, you've got a generous hand. These wise men who give fools money earn themselves a good reputation, though it costs them fourteen years' rent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Andrew, Sir Toby, and Fabian.","id":842},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Now, sir, have I met you again? there's for you.","[Striking Sebastian.]"],"id":843,"m":"Now, sir, have I met you again? Take that! [He strikes Sebastian]"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Why, there's for thee, and there, and there.","Are all the people mad?"],"id":844,"m":"Why, take that, and that, and that! Is everyone mad?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Beating Sir Andrew.","id":845},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Hold, sir, or I'll throw your dagger o'er the house."],"id":846,"m":"Stop, sir, or I'll throw your dagger over the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["This will I tell my lady straight. I would not be in some of","your coats for twopence."],"id":847,"m":"I'll tell my lady about this straight away. I wouldn't be in some of your shoes for twopence."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Feste.","id":848},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come on, sir; hold."],"id":849,"m":"Come on, sir, stop."},{"t":"sd","text":"Holding Sebastian.","id":850},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Nay, let him alone; I'll go another way to work with","him; I'll have an action of battery against him, if there be any","law in Illyria: though I struck him first, yet it's no matter for","that."],"id":851,"m":"No, leave him alone. I'll find another way to deal with him. I'll sue him for assault, if there's any law in Illyria. Though I hit him first, that doesn't matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Let go thy hand."],"id":852,"m":"Let go of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Come, sir, I will not let you go. Come, my young soldier,","put up your iron: you are well fleshed; come on."],"id":853,"m":"Come on, sir, I won't let you go. Come, my young soldier, put away your sword. You've had a taste of blood; come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I will be free from thee. What wouldst thou now?","If thou dar'st tempt me further, draw thy sword.","[Draws.]"],"id":854,"m":"I'll be free of you. What do you want now? If you dare to provoke me further, draw your sword. [He draws]"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["What, what? Nay, then I must have an ounce or two of this","malapert blood from you.","[Draws.]"],"id":855,"m":"What, what? Well then, I must have an ounce or two of your cheeky blood. [He draws]"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia.","id":856},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Hold, Toby; on thy life, I charge thee hold."],"id":857,"m":"Stop, Toby! On your life, I order you to stop."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Madam?"],"id":858,"m":"Madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Will it be ever thus? Ungracious wretch,","Fit for the mountains and the barbarous caves,","Where manners ne'er were preach'd! Out of my sight!","Be not offended, dear Cesario!—","Rudesby, be gone!—I pr'ythee, gentle friend,"],"id":859,"m":"Will it always be like this? Graceless wretch, fit for the mountains and the savage caves, where good manners were never taught! Get out of my sight! Don't be offended, dear Cesario. Ruffian, be gone! Please, gentle friend,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Sir Toby, Sir Andrew, and Fabian.","id":860},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Let thy fair wisdom, not thy passion, sway","In this uncivil and unjust extent","Against thy peace. Go with me to my house,","And hear thou there how many fruitless pranks","This ruffian hath botch'd up, that thou thereby","Mayst smile at this: thou shalt not choose but go;","Do not deny. Beshrew his soul for me,","He started one poor heart of mine in thee."],"id":861,"m":"let your good sense, not your anger, rule you in this rude and unjust attack on your peace. Come with me to my house, and hear there how many pointless pranks this ruffian has cobbled together, so that you can smile at this one. You must come; don't refuse. Curse his soul for me; he frightened my poor heart, which lives in you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["What relish is in this? how runs the stream?","Or I am mad, or else this is a dream:—","Let fancy still my sense in Lethe steep;","If it be thus to dream, still let me sleep!"],"id":862,"m":"What does this mean? Which way is the current flowing? Either I'm mad, or else this is a dream. Let my imagination keep my senses drowned in forgetfulness; if this is what it is to dream, let me sleep on!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Nay, come, I pr'ythee. Would thou'dst be ruled by me!"],"id":863,"m":"Come, please. I wish you'd let me guide you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Madam, I will."],"id":864,"m":"Madam, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O, say so, and so be!"],"id":865,"m":"Oh, say so, and let it be so!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":866},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"A Room in Olivia's House.","id":867},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Maria and Feste.","id":868},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Nay, I pr'ythee, put on this gown and this beard; make him","believe thou art Sir Topas the curate; do it quickly: I'll call","Sir Toby the whilst."],"id":869,"m":"Now please, put on this gown and this beard. Make him believe you're Sir Topas the curate. Be quick. I'll call Sir Toby meanwhile."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Maria.","id":870},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Well, I'll put it on, and I will dissemble myself in't; and","I would I were the first that ever dissembled in such a gown. I","am not tall enough to become the function well: nor lean enough","to be thought a good student: but to be said, an honest man and a","good housekeeper, goes as fairly as to say, a careful man and a","great scholar. The competitors enter."],"id":871,"m":"Well, I'll put it on and disguise myself in it; and I wish I were the first man who ever told lies in a gown like this. I'm not tall enough to fill the role well, nor thin enough to be thought a good scholar. But to be called an honest man and a good host is as fine as being called a careful man and a great scholar. Here come my partners."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch and Maria.","id":872},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Jove bless thee, Master Parson."],"id":873,"m":"Jove bless you, Master Parson."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Bonos dies, Sir Toby: for as the old hermit of Prague, that","never saw pen and ink, very wittily said to a niece of King","Gorboduc, 'That that is, is'; so I, being master parson, am","master parson: for what is that but that? and is but is?"],"id":874,"m":"Good day, Sir Toby. For as the old hermit of Prague, who never saw pen and ink, very wittily said to a niece of King Gorboduc, 'That which is, is.' So I, being Master Parson, am Master Parson; for what is 'that' but 'that', and 'is' but 'is'?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["To him, Sir Topas."],"id":875,"m":"Go to him, Sir Topas."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["What, hoa, I say,—Peace in this prison!"],"id":876,"m":"Hello there, I say! Peace be in this prison!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["The knave counterfeits well; a good knave."],"id":877,"m":"The rascal is a good actor; a good rascal."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["[In an inner chamber.] Who calls there?"],"id":878,"m":"[From a dark inner room] Who's calling there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Sir Topas the curate, who comes to visit Malvolio the","lunatic."],"id":879,"m":"Sir Topas the curate, who has come to visit Malvolio the lunatic."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sir Topas, Sir Topas, good Sir Topas, go to my lady."],"id":880,"m":"Sir Topas, Sir Topas, good Sir Topas, go to my lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Out, hyperbolical fiend! how vexest thou this man? talkest thou","nothing but of ladies?"],"id":881,"m":"Out, you raving devil! How you torment this man! Do you talk about nothing but ladies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Well said, master parson."],"id":882,"m":"Well said, Master Parson."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sir Topas, never was man thus wronged: good Sir Topas, do","not think I am mad; they have laid me here in hideous darkness."],"id":883,"m":"Sir Topas, no man was ever wronged like this. Good Sir Topas, don't think I'm mad. They've shut me up here in terrible darkness."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Fie, thou dishonest Sathan! I call thee by the most modest","terms; for I am one of those gentle ones that will use the devil","himself with courtesy. Say'st thou that house is dark?"],"id":884,"m":"Shame on you, you lying Satan! I call you by the politest name I can, because I'm one of those gentle people who treat even the devil with courtesy. Do you say that room is dark?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["As hell, Sir Topas."],"id":885,"m":"As dark as hell, Sir Topas."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Why, it hath bay windows transparent as barricadoes, and the","clear storeys toward the south-north are as lustrous as ebony;","and yet complainest thou of obstruction?"],"id":886,"m":"Why, it has bay windows as transparent as barricades, and the high windows facing south-north are as bright as ebony; and yet you complain it's blocked?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["I am not mad, Sir Topas; I say to you this house is dark."],"id":887,"m":"I'm not mad, Sir Topas. I tell you, this room is dark."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Madman, thou errest. I say there is no darkness but","ignorance; in which thou art more puzzled than the Egyptians in","their fog."],"id":888,"m":"Madman, you are wrong. I say there is no darkness but ignorance, and you're more lost in that than the Egyptians in their fog."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["I say this house is as dark as ignorance, though","ignorance were as dark as hell; and I say there was never man","thus abused. I am no more mad than you are; make the trial of it","in any constant question."],"id":889,"m":"I say this room is as dark as ignorance, even if ignorance were as dark as hell; and I say no man was ever so badly treated. I'm no more mad than you are. Test me with any reasonable question."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["What is the opinion of Pythagoras concerning wild-fowl?"],"id":890,"m":"What is the opinion of Pythagoras about wild birds?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["That the soul of our grandam might haply inhabit a bird."],"id":891,"m":"That the soul of our grandmother might perhaps live in a bird."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["What thinkest thou of his opinion?"],"id":892,"m":"What do you think of his opinion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["I think nobly of the soul, and no way approve his opinion."],"id":893,"m":"I think nobly of the soul, and don't agree with his opinion at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Fare thee well. Remain thou still in darkness: thou shalt","hold the opinion of Pythagoras ere I will allow of thy wits; and","fear to kill a woodcock, lest thou dispossess the soul of thy","grandam. Fare thee well."],"id":894,"m":"Goodbye. Stay in the dark. You'll have to accept the opinion of Pythagoras before I'll declare you sane, and be afraid to kill a woodcock in case you evict your grandmother's soul. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sir Topas, Sir Topas!"],"id":895,"m":"Sir Topas, Sir Topas!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["My most exquisite Sir Topas!"],"id":896,"m":"My most excellent Sir Topas!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Nay, I am for all waters."],"id":897,"m":"Oh, I can turn my hand to anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIA","lines":["Thou mightst have done this without thy beard and gown: he","sees thee not."],"id":898,"m":"You could have done this without your beard and gown. He can't see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["To him in thine own voice, and bring me word how thou","findest him; I would we were well rid of this knavery. If he may","be conveniently delivered, I would he were; for I am now so far","in offence with my niece that I cannot pursue with any safety","this sport to the upshot. Come by and by to my chamber."],"id":899,"m":"Go to him in your own voice and let me know how you find him. I wish we were well out of this trick. If he can be released without trouble, I wish he would be, because I'm now so far out of favour with my niece that I can't safely carry this game through to the end. Come to my room shortly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Sir Toby and Maria.","id":900},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["[Singing.] 'Hey, Robin, jolly Robin,","Tell me how thy lady does.'"],"id":901,"m":"[Singing] 'Hey, Robin, jolly Robin, tell me how your lady is.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Fool,—"],"id":902,"m":"Fool..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'My lady is unkind, perdy.'"],"id":903,"m":"[Singing] 'My lady is unkind, by God.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Fool,—"],"id":904,"m":"Fool..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'Alas, why is she so?'"],"id":905,"m":"[Singing] 'Alas, why is she so?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Fool, I say;—"],"id":906,"m":"Fool, I say..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["'She loves another'—Who calls, ha?"],"id":907,"m":"[Singing] 'She loves another.' Who's calling, eh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Good fool, as ever thou wilt deserve well at my hand,","help me to a candle, and pen, ink, and paper; as I am a","gentleman, I will live to be thankful to thee for't."],"id":908,"m":"Good fool, if you ever want to be rewarded by me, help me to a candle, and pen, ink and paper. As I'm a gentleman, I'll live to be grateful to you for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Master Malvolio!"],"id":909,"m":"Master Malvolio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Ay, good fool."],"id":910,"m":"Yes, good fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Alas, sir, how fell you besides your five wits?"],"id":911,"m":"Oh dear, sir, how did you come to lose your five wits?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Fool, there was never man so notoriously abused; I am as well in","my wits, fool, as thou art."],"id":912,"m":"Fool, no man was ever so outrageously wronged. I'm as sane as you are, fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["But as well? then you are mad indeed, if you be no better in","your wits than a fool."],"id":913,"m":"Only as sane? Then you're truly mad, if your wits are no better than a fool's."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["They have here propertied me; keep me in darkness, send","ministers to me, asses, and do all they can to face me out of my","wits."],"id":914,"m":"They've treated me like a piece of furniture here, keep me in the dark, send ministers to me, the asses, and do everything they can to bully me out of my wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Advise you what you say: the minister is here.—Malvolio, thy","wits the heavens restore! endeavour thyself to sleep, and leave","thy vain bibble-babble."],"id":915,"m":"Be careful what you say; the minister is here. [In Sir Topas's voice] Malvolio, Malvolio, may the heavens restore your wits! Try to sleep, and stop your empty babbling."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Sir Topas,—"],"id":916,"m":"Sir Topas..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Maintain no words with him, good fellow. Who, I, sir? not","I, sir. God b' wi' you, good Sir Topas.—Marry, amen.—I will","sir, I will."],"id":917,"m":"[As Sir Topas] Have no conversation with him, good fellow. [As himself] Who, me, sir? Never, sir. Goodbye, good Sir Topas. [As Sir Topas] Indeed, amen. [As himself] I will, sir, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Fool, fool, fool, I say,—"],"id":918,"m":"Fool, fool, fool, I say..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Alas, sir, be patient. What say you, sir? I am shent for","speaking to you."],"id":919,"m":"Oh dear, sir, be patient. What do you say, sir? I've been scolded for talking to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Good fool, help me to some light and some paper;","I tell thee I am as well in my wits as any man in Illyria."],"id":920,"m":"Kind fool, fetch me a light and some paper. My mind is as sound as any man's in Illyria, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Well-a-day,—that you were, sir!"],"id":921,"m":"Alas, I wish you were, sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["By this hand, I am: Good fool, some ink, paper, and","light, and convey what I will set down to my lady; it shall","advantage thee more than ever the bearing of letter did."],"id":922,"m":"By this hand, I am. Good fool, some ink, paper and light, and take what I write down to my lady. It will profit you more than carrying any letter ever did."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["I will help you to't. But tell me true, are you not mad","indeed? or do you but counterfeit?"],"id":923,"m":"I'll help you get them. But tell me truly, aren't you really mad? Or are you just pretending?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Believe me, I am not; I tell thee true."],"id":924,"m":"Believe me, I'm not. I'm telling you the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Nay, I'll ne'er believe a madman till I see his brains.","I will fetch you light, and paper, and ink."],"id":925,"m":"No, I'll never believe a madman till I see his brains. I'll fetch you light and paper and ink."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Fool, I'll requite it in the highest degree: I pr'ythee be","gone."],"id":926,"m":"Fool, I'll reward you as highly as possible. Please go."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["[Singing.]","~'I am gone, sir,","~And anon, sir,","~I'll be with you again,","~In a trice,","~Like to the old vice,","~Your need to sustain;","~Who with dagger of lath,","~In his rage and his wrath,","~Cries ah, ha! to the devil:","~Like a mad lad,","~Pare thy nails, dad.","~Adieu, goodman drivel."],"id":927,"m":"[Singing] 'I'm gone, sir, and soon, sir, I'll be with you again in a moment, like the old Vice in the plays, to help you in your need.' 'With his wooden dagger, in his rage and his anger, he cries ah ha to the devil, like a mad lad: cut your nails, dad; goodbye, Mister Drivel.'"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":928},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Olivia's Garden.","id":929},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sebastian.","id":930},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["This is the air; that is the glorious sun;","This pearl she gave me, I do feel't and see't:","And though 'tis wonder that enwraps me thus,","Yet 'tis not madness. Where's Antonio, then?","I could not find him at the Elephant;","Yet there he was; and there I found this credit,","That he did range the town to seek me out.","His counsel now might do me golden service;","For though my soul disputes well with my sense,","That this may be some error, but no madness,","Yet doth this accident and flood of fortune","So far exceed all instance, all discourse,","That I am ready to distrust mine eyes","And wrangle with my reason, that persuades me","To any other trust but that I am mad,","Or else the lady's mad; yet if 'twere so,","She could not sway her house, command her followers,","Take and give back affairs and their despatch","With such a smooth, discreet, and stable bearing,","As I perceive she does: there's something in't","That is deceivable. But here comes the lady."],"id":931,"m":"Here is the air, and there the glorious sun; this pearl she gave me I can touch and look at. And though I'm wrapped in wonder like this, it isn't madness. Where's Antonio, then? I couldn't find him at the Elephant, yet he had been there, and I was told that he had gone round the town looking for me. His advice now could be worth gold to me. My soul agrees with my senses that this may be some mistake but not madness; yet this chance event and flood of good fortune so far exceeds any precedent or explanation that I'm ready to distrust my eyes and argue with my reason when it tries to persuade me of anything other than that I'm mad, or else the lady is. Yet if that were so, she couldn't run her house, command her servants, take charge of business and see it carried out with such a smooth, discreet and steady manner as I see she does. There's something deceptive in it. But here comes the lady."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia and a Priest.","id":932},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Blame not this haste of mine. If you mean well,","Now go with me and with this holy man","Into the chantry by: there, before him","And underneath that consecrated roof,","Plight me the full assurance of your faith,","That my most jealous and too doubtful soul","May live at peace. He shall conceal it","Whiles you are willing it shall come to note;","What time we will our celebration keep","According to my birth.—What do you say?"],"id":933,"m":"Don't blame me for this haste. If your intentions are honourable, come with me now and with this holy man into the chapel nearby. There, in front of him and under that holy roof, give me your full promise of faithfulness, so that my jealous and too anxious soul may live in peace. He will keep it secret until you are willing for it to be made known, and at that time we will hold our celebration in a manner fitting my rank. What do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I'll follow this good man, and go with you;","And, having sworn truth, ever will be true."],"id":934,"m":"I'll follow this good man and go with you, and having sworn to be true, I will always be true."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Then lead the way, good father;—And heavens so shine","That they may fairly note this act of mine!"],"id":935,"m":"Then lead the way, good father; and may the heavens shine so that they look kindly on what I am doing!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":936},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"The Street before Olivia's House.","id":937},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Feste and Fabian.","id":938},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Now, as thou lovest me, let me see his letter."],"id":939,"m":"Now, if you love me, let me see his letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Good Master Fabian, grant me another request."],"id":940,"m":"Good Master Fabian, grant me another request."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Anything."],"id":941,"m":"Anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Do not desire to see this letter."],"id":942,"m":"Don't ask to see this letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["This is to give a dog; and in recompense desire my dog again."],"id":943,"m":"That's like giving someone a dog and then asking for the dog back as payment."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Orsino, Viola, and Attendants.","id":944},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Belong you to the Lady Olivia, friends?"],"id":945,"m":"Do you belong to Lady Olivia, friends?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Ay, sir; we are some of her trappings."],"id":946,"m":"Yes, sir, we're some of her trimmings."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["I know thee well. How dost thou, my good fellow?"],"id":947,"m":"I know you well. How are you, my good fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Truly, sir, the better for my foes and the worse for my friends."],"id":948,"m":"Truly, sir, better for my enemies and worse for my friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Just the contrary; the better for thy friends."],"id":949,"m":"Just the opposite: better for your friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["No, sir, the worse."],"id":950,"m":"No, sir, worse."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["How can that be?"],"id":951,"m":"How can that be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Marry, sir, they praise me and make an ass of me; now my","foes tell me plainly I am an ass: so that by my foes, sir, I","profit in the knowledge of myself, and by my friends I am abused:","so that, conclusions to be as kisses, if your four negatives make","your two affirmatives, why then, the worse for my friends and","the better for my foes."],"id":952,"m":"Well, sir, my friends praise me and make an ass of me, while my enemies tell me plainly that I'm an ass. So through my enemies, sir, I gain in self-knowledge, and through my friends I'm deceived. So, if conclusions are like kisses, where four negatives make two affirmatives, then I'm worse for my friends and better for my enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Why, this is excellent."],"id":953,"m":"Why, this is excellent."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["By my troth, sir, no; though it please you to be one of my","friends."],"id":954,"m":"Honestly, sir, no; though you're pleased to be one of my friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Thou shalt not be the worse for me; there's gold."],"id":955,"m":"You won't be worse off because of me. There's some gold."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["But that it would be double-dealing, sir, I would you could","make it another."],"id":956,"m":"If it wouldn't be double-dealing, sir, I'd ask you to make it two."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["O, you give me ill counsel."],"id":957,"m":"Oh, you're giving me bad advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Put your grace in your pocket, sir, for this once, and let","your flesh and blood obey it."],"id":958,"m":"Put your virtue in your pocket, sir, just this once, and let your flesh and blood follow the advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Well, I will be so much a sinner to be a double-dealer: there's","another."],"id":959,"m":"Well, I'll be enough of a sinner to be a double-dealer. There's another."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Primo, secundo, tertio, is a good play; and the old saying","is, the third pays for all; the triplex, sir, is a good tripping","measure; or the bells of Saint Bennet, sir, may put you in mind;","one, two, three."],"id":960,"m":"First, second, third is a good game, and the old saying goes, 'Third time pays for all.' Triple time, sir, is a good dancing rhythm; or the bells of Saint Bennet, sir, may remind you: one, two, three."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["You can fool no more money out of me at this throw: if you","will let your lady know I am here to speak with her, and bring","her along with you, it may awake my bounty further."],"id":961,"m":"You can't fool any more money out of me with this throw. If you'll let your lady know I'm here to speak with her, and bring her with you, it may wake up my generosity again."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Marry, sir, lullaby to your bounty till I come again. I go,","sir; but I would not have you to think that my desire of having","is the sin of covetousness: but, as you say, sir, let your bounty","take a nap; I will awake it anon."],"id":962,"m":"Well, sir, sing a lullaby to your generosity till I come back. I'm going, sir; but I wouldn't want you to think that my desire to have things is the sin of greed. But, as you say, sir, let your generosity take a nap; I'll wake it up soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Feste.","id":963},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antonio and Officers.","id":964},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Here comes the man, sir, that did rescue me."],"id":965,"m":"Here comes the man who rescued me, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["That face of his I do remember well:","Yet when I saw it last it was besmeared","As black as Vulcan in the smoke of war:","A bawbling vessel was he captain of,","For shallow draught and bulk unprizable;","With which such scathful grapple did he make","With the most noble bottom of our fleet","That very envy and the tongue of loss","Cried fame and honour on him.—What's the matter?"],"id":966,"m":"I remember that face of his well, yet when I last saw it, it was smeared as black as Vulcan's with the smoke of war. He was captain of a trifling ship, worthless for its shallow draught and size, and with it he grappled so destructively with the finest ship of our fleet that even envy and the voices of the losers praised his fame and honour. What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["Orsino, this is that Antonio","That took the Phoenix and her fraught from Candy:","And this is he that did the Tiger board","When your young nephew Titus lost his leg:","Here in the streets, desperate of shame and state,","In private brabble did we apprehend him."],"id":967,"m":"Orsino, this is the Antonio who seized the Phoenix and all she carried from Crete, and the one who boarded the Tiger in the fight where your young nephew Titus lost a leg. We caught him here in the streets in a private brawl, careless of disgrace or danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["He did me kindness, sir; drew on my side;","But, in conclusion, put strange speech upon me.","I know not what 'twas, but distraction."],"id":968,"m":"He was kind to me, sir; he drew his sword on my side. But in the end he said strange things to me. I don't know what it was, unless it was madness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Notable pirate! thou salt-water thief!","What foolish boldness brought thee to their mercies,","Whom thou, in terms so bloody and so dear,","Hast made thine enemies?"],"id":969,"m":"Notorious pirate! You salt-water thief! What foolish boldness put you at the mercy of those you made your enemies in such bloody and costly ways?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Orsino, noble sir,","Be pleased that I shake off these names you give me:","Antonio never yet was thief or pirate,","Though, I confess, on base and ground enough,","Orsino's enemy. A witchcraft drew me hither:","That most ingrateful boy there, by your side","From the rude sea's enraged and foamy mouth","Did I redeem; a wreck past hope he was:","His life I gave him, and did thereto add","My love, without retention or restraint,","All his in dedication: for his sake,","Did I expose myself, pure for his love,","Into the danger of this adverse town;","Drew to defend him when he was beset:","Where being apprehended, his false cunning,—","Not meaning to partake with me in danger,—","Taught him to face me out of his acquaintance,","And grew a twenty-years-removed thing","While one would wink; denied me mine own purse,","Which I had recommended to his use","Not half an hour before."],"id":970,"m":"Orsino, noble sir, allow me to reject the names you give me. Antonio has never been a thief or pirate, although, I confess, I had solid grounds to be Orsino's enemy. Witchcraft drew me here. That most ungrateful boy there, by your side, I rescued from the rough sea's angry, foaming mouth. He was a wreck past hope. I gave him his life, and added my love to it, without holding anything back, all devoted to him. For his sake, purely out of love for him, I exposed myself to the danger of this hostile town, and drew to protect him when he was set upon. When I was arrested, his false cunning, since he didn't want to share my danger, taught him to deny he knew me to my face, and in the blink of an eye he became like someone I hadn't seen for twenty years. He refused me my own purse, which I had given him to use not half an hour before."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["How can this be?"],"id":971,"m":"How can this be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["When came he to this town?"],"id":972,"m":"When did he come to this town?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["To-day, my lord; and for three months before,—","No interim, not a minute's vacancy,—","Both day and night did we keep company."],"id":973,"m":"Today, my lord; and for three months before that, without a break, not one minute apart, we kept each other company day and night."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Olivia and Attendants.","id":974},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Here comes the countess; now heaven walks on earth.—","But for thee, fellow, fellow, thy words are madness:","Three months this youth hath tended upon me;","But more of that anon.—Take him aside."],"id":975,"m":"Here comes the Countess; now heaven walks on earth. But as for you, fellow, fellow, your words are madness. This youth has served me for three months. But more of that later. Take him aside."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What would my lord, but that he may not have,","Wherein Olivia may seem serviceable!—","Cesario, you do not keep promise with me."],"id":976,"m":"What does my lord want, apart from what he can't have, in which Olivia could be of service? Cesario, you're not keeping your promise to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Madam?"],"id":977,"m":"Madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Gracious Olivia,—"],"id":978,"m":"Gracious Olivia..."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What do you say, Cesario?—Good my lord,—"],"id":979,"m":"What do you say, Cesario? My good lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["My lord would speak, my duty hushes me."],"id":980,"m":"My lord wants to speak; my duty keeps me quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["If it be aught to the old tune, my lord,","It is as fat and fulsome to mine ear","As howling after music."],"id":981,"m":"If it's anything like the old tune, my lord, it's as gross and sickening to my ear as howling after music."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Still so cruel?"],"id":982,"m":"Still so cruel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Still so constant, lord."],"id":983,"m":"Still so constant, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["What! to perverseness? you uncivil lady,","To whose ingrate and unauspicious altars","My soul the faithfull'st offerings hath breathed out","That e'er devotion tender'd! What shall I do?"],"id":984,"m":"What, constant in stubbornness? You unkind lady, at whose ungrateful and unpromising altars my soul has breathed out the most faithful offerings that devotion ever made! What shall I do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Even what it please my lord, that shall become him."],"id":985,"m":"Whatever my lord pleases that suits him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Why should I not, had I the heart to do it.","Like to the Egyptian thief, at point of death,","Kill what I love; a savage jealousy","That sometime savours nobly.—But hear me this:","Since you to non-regardance cast my faith,","And that I partly know the instrument","That screws me from my true place in your favour,","Live you the marble-breasted tyrant still;","But this your minion, whom I know you love,","And whom, by heaven I swear, I tender dearly,","Him will I tear out of that cruel eye","Where he sits crowned in his master's sprite.—","Come, boy, with me; my thoughts are ripe in mischief:","I'll sacrifice the lamb that I do love,","To spite a raven's heart within a dove."],"id":986,"m":"Why shouldn't I, if I had the heart to do it, like the Egyptian thief at the point of death, kill what I love? A savage jealousy that sometimes has something noble about it. But listen to this. Since you throw my faithfulness aside, and since I partly know the tool that twists me out of my rightful place in your favour, go on living as the marble-hearted tyrant. But this darling of yours, whom I know you love, and whom, I swear by heaven, I hold dear, I'll tear him out of that cruel eye of yours, where he sits crowned in spite of his master. Come with me, boy. My thoughts are ripe for harm. I'll sacrifice the lamb I love, to spite a raven's heart inside a dove."},{"t":"sd","text":"Going.","id":987},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And I, most jocund, apt, and willingly,","To do you rest, a thousand deaths would die."],"id":988,"m":"And I would die a thousand deaths, most joyfully, readily and willingly, to give you peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Where goes Cesario?"],"id":989,"m":"Where is Cesario going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["After him I love","More than I love these eyes, more than my life,","More, by all mores, than e'er I shall love wife;","If I do feign, you witnesses above","Punish my life for tainting of my love!"],"id":990,"m":"After the one I love more than I love these eyes, more than my life, more, by every 'more', than I'll ever love a wife. If I'm pretending, you witnesses above, punish my life for staining my love!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Ah me, detested! how am I beguil'd!"],"id":991,"m":"Oh, I'm hated! How I've been deceived!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Who does beguile you? who does do you wrong?"],"id":992,"m":"Who is deceiving you? Who is wronging you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Hast thou forgot thyself? Is it so long?—","Call forth the holy father."],"id":993,"m":"Have you forgotten yourself? Has it been so long? Call the holy father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant.","id":994},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["[To Viola.] Come, away!"],"id":995,"m":"[To Viola] Come, away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Whither, my lord? Cesario, husband, stay."],"id":996,"m":"Where to, my lord? Cesario, husband, stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Husband?"],"id":997,"m":"Husband?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Ay, husband, can he that deny?"],"id":998,"m":"Yes, husband. Can he deny that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Her husband, sirrah?"],"id":999,"m":"Her husband, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["No, my lord, not I."],"id":1000,"m":"No, my lord, not me."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Alas, it is the baseness of thy fear","That makes thee strangle thy propriety:","Fear not, Cesario, take thy fortunes up;","Be that thou know'st thou art, and then thou art","As great as that thou fear'st—O, welcome, father!"],"id":1001,"m":"Alas, it's your cowardly fear that makes you strangle your true identity. Don't be afraid, Cesario; accept your good fortune. Be what you know you are, and then you are as great as the man you fear. Oh, welcome, father!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Attendant and Priest.","id":1002},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["Father, I charge thee, by thy reverence,","Here to unfold,—though lately we intended","To keep in darkness what occasion now","Reveals before 'tis ripe,—what thou dost know","Hath newly passed between this youth and me."],"id":1003,"m":"Father, I order you, by your holy office, to reveal here what you know has just taken place between this youth and me, though we meant to keep in the dark what circumstances now reveal before the time is ripe."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIEST","lines":["A contract of eternal bond of love,","Confirmed by mutual joinder of your hands,","Attested by the holy close of lips,","Strengthen'd by interchangement of your rings;","And all the ceremony of this compact","Sealed in my function, by my testimony:","Since when, my watch hath told me, toward my grave,","I have travelled but two hours."],"id":1004,"m":"A contract of eternal love, confirmed by the joining of your hands, witnessed by a holy kiss, strengthened by the exchange of your rings; and all the ceremony of this agreement sealed by me as priest, on my testimony. Since then, my watch tells me, I have travelled only two hours towards my grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["O thou dissembling cub! What wilt thou be,","When time hath sowed a grizzle on thy case?","Or will not else thy craft so quickly grow","That thine own trip shall be thine overthrow?","Farewell, and take her; but direct thy feet","Where thou and I henceforth may never meet."],"id":1005,"m":"Oh, you lying cub! What will you be when time has sprinkled grey on your skin? Or won't your cunning grow so fast that your own tricks trip you up? Goodbye, and take her; but take yourself where you and I may never meet again."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["My lord, I do protest,—"],"id":1006,"m":"My lord, I swear..."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["O, do not swear;","Hold little faith, though thou hast too much fear."],"id":1007,"m":"Oh, don't swear. Keep a little faith, though you have too much fear."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Andrew Ague-Cheek, with his head broke.","id":1008},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["For the love of God, a surgeon; send one presently to Sir Toby."],"id":1009,"m":"For the love of God, a surgeon! Send one to Sir Toby at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":1010,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["He has broke my head across, and has given Sir Toby a","bloody coxcomb too: for the love of God, your help: I had rather","than forty pound I were at home."],"id":1011,"m":"He's cracked my skull, and bloodied Sir Toby's head as well. For God's sake, help! I'd pay forty pounds to be home right now."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Who has done this, Sir Andrew?"],"id":1012,"m":"Who has done this, Sir Andrew?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["The Count's gentleman, one Cesario: we took him for a","coward, but he's the very devil incardinate."],"id":1013,"m":"The Count's gentleman, one Cesario. We thought he was a coward, but he's the devil incarnate."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["My gentleman, Cesario?"],"id":1014,"m":"My gentleman, Cesario?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["Od's lifelings, here he is:—You broke my head for","nothing; and that that I did, I was set on to do't by Sir Toby."],"id":1015,"m":"God's little life, here he is! You broke my head for nothing; and what I did, Sir Toby put me up to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Why do you speak to me? I never hurt you:","You drew your sword upon me without cause;","But I bespake you fair and hurt you not."],"id":1016,"m":"Why are you speaking to me? I never hurt you. You drew your sword on me without reason, but I spoke politely to you and didn't hurt you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["If a bloody coxcomb be a hurt, you have hurt me; I think","you set nothing by a bloody coxcomb."],"id":1017,"m":"If a bloody head is a hurt, you've hurt me. I suppose you think nothing of a bloody head."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Toby Belch, drunk, led by Feste.","id":1018},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes Sir Toby halting; you shall hear more: but if he had","not been in drink he would have tickled you othergates than he","did."],"id":1019,"m":"Here comes Sir Toby limping. You'll hear more. But if he hadn't been drinking, he'd have dealt with you in another way than he did."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["How now, gentleman? how is't with you?"],"id":1020,"m":"Well, sir! How are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["That's all one; he has hurt me, and there's the end on't.—","Sot, didst see Dick Surgeon, sot?"],"id":1021,"m":"It doesn't matter. He's hurt me, and that's the end of it. Fool, have you seen Dick the surgeon, fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["O, he's drunk, Sir Toby, an hour agone; his eyes were set at","eight i' the morning."],"id":1022,"m":"Oh, he's been drunk for an hour, Sir Toby. His eyes were glazed at eight in the morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Then he's a rogue. After a passy-measure, or a pavin, I hate a","drunken rogue."],"id":1023,"m":"Then he's a rogue and a slow-dancing fool. I hate a drunken rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Away with him. Who hath made this havoc with them?"],"id":1024,"m":"Take him away! Who has done this damage to them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR ANDREW","lines":["I'll help you, Sir Toby, because we'll be dressed together."],"id":1025,"m":"I'll help you, Sir Toby, because we'll have our wounds dressed together."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIR TOBY","lines":["Will you help an ass-head, and a coxcomb, and a knave? a","thin-faced knave, a gull?"],"id":1026,"m":"Will you help? You ass-head, and fool, and knave? A thin-faced knave, a dupe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Get him to bed, and let his hurt be looked to."],"id":1027,"m":"Get him to bed and have his wound seen to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Feste, Sir Toby, and Sir Andrew.","id":1028},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sebastian.","id":1029},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["I am sorry, madam, I have hurt your kinsman;","But, had it been the brother of my blood,","I must have done no less, with wit and safety.","You throw a strange regard upon me, and by that","I do perceive it hath offended you;","Pardon me, sweet one, even for the vows","We made each other but so late ago."],"id":1030,"m":"I'm sorry, madam, that I have hurt your relative. But if he had been my own brother, I couldn't have done less if I wanted to stay safe. You're looking at me strangely, and from that I see it has offended you. Forgive me, sweet one, if only for the promises we exchanged so short a time ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["One face, one voice, one habit, and two persons;","A natural perspective, that is, and is not."],"id":1031,"m":"One face, one voice, one costume, and two people! A natural optical illusion, that is and is not."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Antonio, O my dear Antonio!","How have the hours rack'd and tortur'd me","Since I have lost thee."],"id":1032,"m":"Antonio! Oh my dear Antonio! How the hours have racked and tortured me since I lost you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Sebastian are you?"],"id":1033,"m":"Are you Sebastian?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Fear'st thou that, Antonio?"],"id":1034,"m":"Do you doubt it, Antonio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["How have you made division of yourself?—","An apple, cleft in two, is not more twin","Than these two creatures. Which is Sebastian?"],"id":1035,"m":"How have you divided yourself? An apple split in two is no more alike than these two creatures. Which one is Sebastian?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Most wonderful!"],"id":1036,"m":"Most wonderful!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["Do I stand there? I never had a brother:","Nor can there be that deity in my nature","Of here and everywhere. I had a sister","Whom the blind waves and surges have devoured:—","[To Viola.] Of charity, what kin are you to me?","What countryman, what name, what parentage?"],"id":1037,"m":"Am I standing there? I never had a brother, and I don't have the godlike power to be here and everywhere at once. I had a sister, whom the blind waves and surges have swallowed up. [To Viola] Please tell me, what relation are you to me? What is your country, your name, your family?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["Of Messaline: Sebastian was my father;","Such a Sebastian was my brother too:","So went he suited to his watery tomb:","If spirits can assume both form and suit,","You come to fright us."],"id":1038,"m":"I'm from Messaline. Sebastian was my father. My brother was a Sebastian too, and he was dressed just like that when he went to his watery grave. If spirits can take on both shape and clothing, you have come to frighten us."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["A spirit I am indeed:","But am in that dimension grossly clad,","Which from the womb I did participate.","Were you a woman, as the rest goes even,","I should my tears let fall upon your cheek,","And say—Thrice welcome, drowned Viola!"],"id":1039,"m":"I am a spirit indeed, but clothed in the solid body I have had since I was born. If you were a woman, since everything else fits, I would let my tears fall on your cheek and say, 'Three times welcome, drowned Viola!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["My father had a mole upon his brow."],"id":1040,"m":"My father had a mole on his forehead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["And so had mine."],"id":1041,"m":"And so did mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And died that day when Viola from her birth","Had numbered thirteen years."],"id":1042,"m":"And he died on the day Viola turned thirteen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["O, that record is lively in my soul!","He finished, indeed, his mortal act","That day that made my sister thirteen years."],"id":1043,"m":"Oh, that memory is alive in my soul! He did end his life on the day my sister turned thirteen."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["If nothing lets to make us happy both","But this my masculine usurp'd attire,","Do not embrace me till each circumstance","Of place, time, fortune, do cohere, and jump","That I am Viola: which to confirm,","I'll bring you to a captain in this town,","Where lie my maiden weeds; by whose gentle help","I was preserv'd to serve this noble count;","All the occurrence of my fortune since","Hath been between this lady and this lord."],"id":1044,"m":"If nothing stands in the way of our happiness except these men's clothes I've taken, don't embrace me until every detail of place, time and fortune fits together and agrees that I am Viola. To confirm it, I'll take you to a captain in this town, where my women's clothes are kept. With his kind help I was saved, to serve this noble Count. Everything that has happened to me since has been between this lady and this lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEBASTIAN","lines":["[To Olivia] So comes it, lady, you have been mistook:","But nature to her bias drew in that.","You would have been contracted to a maid;","Nor are you therein, by my life, deceived;","You are betroth'd both to a maid and man."],"id":1045,"m":"[To Olivia] So it turns out, lady, that you've been mistaken; but nature followed her natural course in that. You would have been engaged to a girl; but by my life, you aren't deceived in that, because you're engaged to someone who is both a virgin and a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Be not amazed; right noble is his blood.—","If this be so, as yet the glass seems true,","I shall have share in this most happy wreck:","[To Viola] Boy, thou hast said to me a thousand times,","Thou never shouldst love woman like to me."],"id":1046,"m":"Don't be amazed; his blood is truly noble. If this is so, as the mirror still seems to show, I will share in this most happy shipwreck. [To Viola] Boy, you've said to me a thousand times that you would never love a woman as much as me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["And all those sayings will I over-swear;","And all those swearings keep as true in soul","As doth that orbed continent the fire","That severs day from night."],"id":1047,"m":"And I'll swear all those sayings over again, and keep all those oaths as faithfully in my soul as the round sphere of the sky keeps the sun's fire that separates day from night."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Give me thy hand;","And let me see thee in thy woman's weeds."],"id":1048,"m":"Give me your hand, and let me see you in your woman's clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIOLA","lines":["The captain that did bring me first on shore","Hath my maid's garments: he, upon some action,","Is now in durance, at Malvolio's suit;","A gentleman and follower of my lady's."],"id":1049,"m":"The captain who first brought me ashore has my women's clothes. Because of some legal action he is now in prison, on the complaint of Malvolio, a gentleman who serves my lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["He shall enlarge him:—Fetch Malvolio hither:—","And yet, alas, now I remember me,","They say, poor gentleman, he's much distract."],"id":1050,"m":"He'll release him. Fetch Malvolio here. And yet, alas, now I remember, they say the poor gentleman is out of his mind."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Feste, with a letter.","id":1051},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["A most extracting frenzy of mine own","From my remembrance clearly banished his.—","How does he, sirrah?"],"id":1052,"m":"My own distracting madness completely drove his out of my mind. How is he, fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Truly, madam, he holds Belzebub at the stave's end as well","as a man in his case may do: he has here writ a letter to you; I","should have given it you to-day morning, but as a madman's","epistles are no gospels, so it skills not much when they are","delivered."],"id":1053,"m":"Truly, madam, he's keeping the devil at arm's length as well as a man in his situation can. He's written a letter to you here. I should have given it to you this morning, but since a madman's letters aren't gospel, it doesn't matter much when they're delivered."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Open it, and read it."],"id":1054,"m":"Open it and read it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Look then to be well edified when the fool delivers the","madman:—'By the Lord, madam,—'"],"id":1055,"m":"Then expect to be well instructed when the fool delivers the madman's words. [Reads wildly] 'By the Lord, madam...'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["How now! art thou mad?"],"id":1056,"m":"What's this? Are you mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["No, madam, I do but read madness: an your ladyship will have","it as it ought to be, you must allow vox."],"id":1057,"m":"No, madam, I'm only reading madness. If your ladyship wants it read as it should be, you must allow the proper voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Pr'ythee, read i' thy right wits."],"id":1058,"m":"Please read it sanely."},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["So I do, madonna; but to read his right wits is to read","thus; therefore perpend, my princess, and give ear."],"id":1059,"m":"I'm doing that, madonna; but to read his sanity is to read like this. So consider, my princess, and listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["[To Fabian] Read it you, sirrah."],"id":1060,"m":"[To Fabian] You read it, fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["[Reads] 'By the Lord, madam, you wrong me, and the world","shall know it: though you have put me into darkness and given","your drunken cousin rule over me, yet have I the benefit of my","senses as well as your ladyship. I have your own letter that","induced me to the semblance I put on; with the which I doubt not","but to do myself much right or you much shame. Think of me as you","please. I leave my duty a little unthought of, and speak out of","my injury.","~The madly-used Malvolio'"],"id":1061,"m":"[Reads] 'By the Lord, madam, you wrong me, and the world will know it. Though you have shut me in darkness and given your drunken cousin power over me, I still have the use of my senses as well as your ladyship. I have your own letter which persuaded me to put on the appearance I did, and with it I have no doubt I can prove myself right or put you to shame. Think of me as you please. I am forgetting my duty a little, and speak out of my sense of injury. The madly-used Malvolio.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Did he write this?"],"id":1062,"m":"Did he write this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Ay, madam."],"id":1063,"m":"Yes, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["This savours not much of distraction."],"id":1064,"m":"This doesn't sound much like madness."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["See him delivered, Fabian: bring him hither."],"id":1065,"m":"See that he's released, Fabian. Bring him here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Fabian.","id":1066},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","cont":1,"lines":["My lord, so please you, these things further thought on,","To think me as well a sister as a wife,","One day shall crown the alliance on't, so please you,","Here at my house, and at my proper cost."],"id":1067,"m":"My lord, if you please, once you have thought these things over, and are happy to think of me as a sister rather than a wife, let one day crown both marriages, if you agree, here at my house and at my own expense."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Madam, I am most apt to embrace your offer.—","[To Viola] Your master quits you; and, for your service done him,","So much against the mettle of your sex,","So far beneath your soft and tender breeding,","And since you called me master for so long,","Here is my hand; you shall from this time be","Your master's mistress."],"id":1068,"m":"Madam, I am very ready to accept your offer. [To Viola] Your master releases you; and for the service you've done him, so contrary to your woman's nature, so far beneath your soft and gentle upbringing, and since you called me master for so long, here is my hand. From now on you will be your master's mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["A sister?—you are she."],"id":1069,"m":"A sister! You are she."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Fabian with Malvolio.","id":1070},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Is this the madman?"],"id":1071,"m":"Is this the madman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Ay, my lord, this same;","How now, Malvolio?"],"id":1072,"m":"Yes, my lord, this is him. How are you, Malvolio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Madam, you have done me wrong,","Notorious wrong."],"id":1073,"m":"Madam, you have wronged me, shamefully wronged me."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Have I, Malvolio? no."],"id":1074,"m":"Have I, Malvolio? No."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["Lady, you have. Pray you peruse that letter:","You must not now deny it is your hand,","Write from it, if you can, in hand or phrase;","Or say 'tis not your seal, not your invention:","You can say none of this. Well, grant it then,","And tell me, in the modesty of honour,","Why you have given me such clear lights of favour;","Bade me come smiling and cross-garter'd to you;","To put on yellow stockings, and to frown","Upon Sir Toby and the lighter people:","And, acting this in an obedient hope,","Why have you suffer'd me to be imprison'd,","Kept in a dark house, visited by the priest,","And made the most notorious geck and gull","That e'er invention played on? tell me why."],"id":1075,"m":"Lady, you have. Please read that letter. You can't deny now that it's your handwriting. Write differently from it if you can, in handwriting or style; or say it isn't your seal or your composition. You can't say any of this. Well then, admit it, and tell me, with the decency that honour requires, why you have given me such clear signs of favour, told me to come to you smiling and cross-gartered, to put on yellow stockings, and to frown on Sir Toby and the lesser servants. And when I did this in obedient hope, why have you allowed me to be imprisoned, kept in a dark room, visited by the priest, and made the most notorious idiot and dupe that any trick was ever played on? Tell me why."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Alas, Malvolio, this is not my writing,","Though, I confess, much like the character:","But out of question, 'tis Maria's hand.","And now I do bethink me, it was she","First told me thou wast mad; then cam'st in smiling,","And in such forms which here were presuppos'd","Upon thee in the letter. Pr'ythee, be content:","This practice hath most shrewdly pass'd upon thee:","But, when we know the grounds and authors of it,","Thou shalt be both the plaintiff and the judge","Of thine own cause."],"id":1076,"m":"Alas, Malvolio, this isn't my writing, though I admit it's very like my handwriting. But without doubt it's Maria's hand. And now I think about it, it was she who first told me you were mad. Then you came in smiling, and in the very ways the letter had suggested to you. Please, be calm. This trick has been played on you very cruelly; but when we know the reasons and who was behind it, you shall be both the plaintiff and the judge in your own case."},{"t":"sp","who":"FABIAN","lines":["Good madam, hear me speak;","And let no quarrel, nor no brawl to come,","Taint the condition of this present hour,","Which I have wonder'd at. In hope it shall not,","Most freely I confess, myself and Toby","Set this device against Malvolio here,","Upon some stubborn and uncourteous parts","We had conceiv'd against him. Maria writ","The letter, at Sir Toby's great importance;","In recompense whereof he hath married her.","How with a sportful malice it was follow'd","May rather pluck on laughter than revenge,","If that the injuries be justly weigh'd","That have on both sides past."],"id":1077,"m":"Good madam, let me speak, and don't let any quarrel or future brawl spoil the happiness of this moment, which I have marvelled at. Hoping it won't, I freely confess that Toby and I set up this trick against Malvolio here, because of some stubborn and discourteous behaviour we resented in him. Maria wrote the letter, because Sir Toby pressed her to, and in reward for it he has married her. The playful malice with which it was carried out should bring laughter rather than revenge, if the injuries on both sides are fairly weighed."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["Alas, poor fool! how have they baffled thee!"],"id":1078,"m":"Alas, poor fool, how they have humiliated you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["Why, 'some are born great, some achieve greatness, and some","have greatness thrown upon them.' I was one, sir, in this","interlude;—one Sir Topas, sir; but that's all one:—'By the","Lord, fool, I am not mad;'—But do you remember? 'Madam, why","laugh you at such a barren rascal? An you smile not, he's","gagged'? And thus the whirligig of time brings in his revenges."],"id":1079,"m":"Why, 'some are born great, some achieve greatness, and some have greatness thrown upon them.' I was one of the actors, sir, in this little play: one Sir Topas, sir; but that doesn't matter. 'By the Lord, fool, I am not mad.' But do you remember? 'Madam, why do you laugh at such an empty-headed rascal? Unless you smile, he's dumbstruck.' And so the spinning top of time brings its revenges."},{"t":"sp","who":"MALVOLIO","lines":["I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you."],"id":1080,"m":"I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1081},{"t":"sp","who":"OLIVIA","lines":["He hath been most notoriously abus'd."],"id":1082,"m":"He has been very shamefully treated."},{"t":"sp","who":"ORSINO","lines":["Pursue him, and entreat him to a peace:—","He hath not told us of the captain yet;","When that is known, and golden time convents,","A solemn combination shall be made","Of our dear souls.—Meantime, sweet sister,","We will not part from hence.—Cesario, come:","For so you shall be while you are a man;","But, when in other habits you are seen,","Orsino's mistress, and his fancy's queen."],"id":1083,"m":"Go after him and persuade him to make peace. He hasn't told us about the captain yet. When that is known, and the golden time is right, there will be a solemn union of our dear souls. Meanwhile, sweet sister, we won't leave this place. Cesario, come; for that's what you'll be while you are a man. But when you are seen in other clothes, you will be Orsino's mistress and the queen of his love."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1084},{"t":"sp","who":"FESTE","lines":["~When that I was and a little tiny boy,","~With hey, ho, the wind and the rain,","~A foolish thing was but a toy,","~For the rain it raineth every day.","~But when I came to man's estate,","~With hey, ho, the wind and the rain,","~'Gainst knave and thief men shut their gate,","~For the rain it raineth every day.","~But when I came, alas! to wive,","~With hey, ho, the wind and the rain,","~By swaggering could I never thrive,","~For the rain it raineth every day.","~But when I came unto my bed,","~With hey, ho, the wind and the rain,","~With toss-pots still had drunken head,","~For the rain it raineth every day.","~A great while ago the world begun,","~With hey, ho, the wind and the rain,","~But that's all one, our play is done,","~And we'll strive to please you every day."],"id":1085,"m":"When I was a little tiny boy, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, a foolish thing was just a toy, for it rains, and rains, every day. But when I grew up to be a man, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, men shut their gates against rogues and thieves, for it rains, and rains, every day. But when I came, alas, to marry, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, I could never get on by swaggering, for it rains, and rains, every day. But when I came to my bed, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, I still had a drunken head like the other drinkers, for it rains, and rains, every day. Long, long ago the world was made, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain. No matter: our play is over now, and we'll aim to please you every day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1086}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Viola","group":"The twins","desc":"A young noblewoman from Messaline, whose father, Sebastian of Messaline, died on the twins' thirteenth birthday. Shipwrecked in Illyria, she believes her twin brother drowned. Disguised as a page, 'Cesario', she serves Duke Orsino, falls in love with him, and is sent to woo Olivia for him, who falls for her instead. Quick, warm and patient, she keeps her secret until her brother appears."},{"name":"Sebastian","group":"The twins","desc":"Viola's twin, rescued by Antonio. Arriving in Illyria, he is taken for 'Cesario' by everyone: beaten by Sir Andrew, then claimed and married by Olivia, who he accepts with delighted bewilderment."},{"name":"Orsino","group":"Orsino's court","desc":"Duke of Illyria. Enjoys his own longing, wallows in music and melancholy, and courts Olivia through messengers. Grows close to 'Cesario', threatens him in a jealous rage, and at the end turns his love to Viola."},{"name":"Valentine","group":"Orsino's court","desc":"A gentleman of Orsino's household who brings Olivia's refusal in the first scene and notes how fast Cesario has risen."},{"name":"Curio","group":"Orsino's court","desc":"Another of Orsino's gentlemen; offers to take him hunting and fetches Feste to sing."},{"name":"Olivia","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"A rich countess mourning her father and brother. She refuses Orsino, falls in love at first sight with 'Cesario', and marries Sebastian by mistake, happily. Proud but generous, and the one person who sees Malvolio's real fault."},{"name":"Malvolio","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"Olivia's steward. Serious, efficient and 'sick of self-love', he spoils the night revels and is tricked by a forged letter into thinking Olivia loves him. Locked up as mad, he leaves vowing revenge."},{"name":"Maria","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"Olivia's lady-in-waiting, small and sharp-witted. She forges the letter that fools Malvolio, drives the joke along, and marries Sir Toby as her reward."},{"name":"Sir Toby","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"Sir Toby Belch, Olivia's uncle (she calls him 'cousin', meaning relative). A drunken, witty knight who lives in her house, takes Sir Andrew's money, and organises the tricks. Ends the play injured and married to Maria."},{"name":"Sir Andrew","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"Sir Andrew Aguecheek, a rich, foolish knight who hopes to marry Olivia. Sir Toby keeps him around for his money. Cowardly, vain about his dancing, and always echoing other people's words."},{"name":"Feste","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"Olivia's jester, a professional 'fool' who is the wisest person on stage. Moves between both houses, sings the play's songs, plays Sir Topas the curate to torment Malvolio, and closes the play."},{"name":"Fabian","group":"Olivia's household","desc":"A servant of Olivia who has a grudge against Malvolio. He joins the box-tree scene and the duel trick, and confesses the plot at the end."},{"name":"Antonio","group":"Others","desc":"A sea captain who once fought Orsino's ships (he seized the Phoenix and her cargo, and in boarding the Tiger cost Orsino's nephew a leg). He rescued Sebastian, loves him devotedly and hands him his purse in Illyria. Following him into danger, he is arrested and feels betrayed when 'Sebastian' (really Viola) does not know him."},{"name":"Captain","group":"Others","desc":"The sea captain who rescues Viola, helps her disguise, and keeps her women's clothes. He is held in prison at Malvolio's suit, so never appears again."},{"name":"Priest","group":"Others","desc":"Marries Olivia and Sebastian in secret and confirms the contract in Act 5."},{"name":"Officers","group":"Others","desc":"Orsino's officers who arrest Antonio."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Orsino, Duke of Illyria, listens to music and talks about love as an appetite. Valentine reports that Olivia will not see anyone for seven years while she mourns her brother. Orsino admires a heart so loyal and imagines how she will love once Cupid strikes.","key":["Love is shown as appetite, sickness and hunting from the first lines.","Orsino enjoys his own feelings more than he knows Olivia.","Olivia's mourning matches Viola's loss in the next scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why will Olivia not see Orsino's messenger?","o":["She is ill with a fever","She is mourning her brother","She loves someone else","She is away"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Orsino compare himself to?","o":["A hunted deer","A ship at sea","A caged bird","A dying flower"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How long does Olivia plan to stay veiled?","o":["One month and a day","One year","Seven years","For ever"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Orsino ask music to do?","o":["Play softly so that he can sleep through the night","Give him excess of it, so his appetite may die","Stop at once, because it reminds him of Olivia","Go on for ever, since it is all he has left"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Orsino think Olivia will love well one day?","o":["She has refused every other suitor in Illyria","She grieves so deeply for a mere brother","She has written to him about her feelings","She has asked her uncle to speak for him"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Viola is shipwrecked on the coast of Illyria. The Captain says her twin brother Sebastian may have survived by tying himself to a mast. Learning that Orsino rules here and that Olivia shuts out all suitors, Viola decides to disguise herself and serve the Duke.","key":["Viola's grief echoes Olivia's: both have lost a brother.","She chooses disguise to stay safe and buy time.","Her trust in time ('What else may hap to time I will commit') runs through the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Viola shipwrecked?","o":["Messaline","Illyria","Venice","Cyprus"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What gives Viola hope her brother lived?","o":["He swam ashore before the ship went down","He tied himself to a mast","A fisherman saved him from the waves","He was never on the ship"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Whom does Viola decide to serve?","o":["Olivia","The Captain","Orsino","Sir Toby"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Viola choose to serve Orsino?","o":["She has heard that Orsino is looking for a wife","She cannot serve Olivia, who sees no one","Her father had once spoken well of Orsino","The Captain was Orsino's servant long ago"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Viola give the Captain?","o":["Gold, for his help","Her mother's ring","A letter for Orsino","Her brother's sword"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"At Olivia's house, Maria warns Sir Toby that his late-night drinking annoys his niece and that his friend Sir Andrew is a fool. Sir Andrew arrives, misunderstands 'accost', and is out-witted by Maria. He wants to go home, but Sir Toby flatters him into staying and dancing.","key":["Introduces the household of revellers who clash with Malvolio later.","Sir Andrew is a comic fool who does not know he is one.","Sir Toby keeps Sir Andrew for his money."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Maria complain about?","o":["Sir Toby's late hours and drinking","Malvolio's pride and his endless rules for the household","Olivia's long mourning for her brother","Feste's long absence from the house"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Sir Toby keep Sir Andrew around?","o":["He is a good fighter","He is rich","He is Olivia's cousin","He is clever"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Sir Andrew think 'accost' means?","o":["A greeting","Maria's name","A dance","A threat"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does Sir Andrew say he would have done instead of fencing and dancing?","o":["Fought in the wars","Married a widow","Travelled to Rome","Studied the tongues"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why is Sir Andrew thinking of going home?","o":["He has spent all his money","Olivia will not see him","Sir Toby has insulted him","Maria has laughed at him"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"Three days in, 'Cesario' (Viola) is already Orsino's favourite. Orsino sends Cesario to woo Olivia, saying a youth will be heard better, and noting how girlish Cesario looks. Viola agrees, but confesses in an aside that she wants to marry Orsino herself.","key":["Dramatic irony: Orsino describes Cesario's womanly looks without guessing the truth.","The love triangle is set up in one couplet.","Viola must woo for the man she loves."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long has Cesario served Orsino?","o":["Three days","Three weeks","Three months","A year"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Orsino send Cesario to Olivia?","o":["Cesario has known her since he was a child","A youth will be heard better","He trusts no one else","Olivia asked for him"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Viola reveal in her aside?","o":["She is a spy","She loves Orsino","She hates Olivia","She wants to go home"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Viola say she will find it hard to woo Olivia?","o":["She wants to be Orsino's wife herself","She does not know what to say to a lady","She has heard that Olivia is very proud","She is afraid that Olivia will know her"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Orsino say Cesario's voice is like?","o":["A soldier's, rough and very strong","A maiden's organ, shrill and sound","A child's, sweet and full of joy","An old man's, slow and very wise"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a1s5":{"sum":"Feste returns and proves Olivia a fool for mourning a brother in heaven. Malvolio sneers at him; Olivia calls Malvolio 'sick of self-love'. Cesario insists on seeing Olivia, delivers Orsino's message with wit, and makes a passionate 'willow cabin' speech. Olivia refuses Orsino but falls for Cesario, and sends Malvolio after him with a ring.","key":["Feste's 'proof' gently mocks Olivia's long mourning.","Olivia's judgement of Malvolio sets up his downfall.","Olivia falls in love at first sight with a woman in disguise."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Feste prove Olivia a fool?","o":["She keeps a fool in her house when she is too sad to laugh","She mourns a brother whose soul is in heaven","She refuses Orsino","She trusts Malvolio"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Olivia say is wrong with Malvolio?","o":["He is lazy and sleeps all afternoon","He is sick of self-love","He drinks too much with Sir Toby","He is a Puritan who hates all fun"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Olivia send after Cesario?","o":["A letter","A ring","Her picture","A purse"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Olivia say when she unveils?","o":["She will give out schedules of her beauty","She is not as beautiful as men have said","She has been crying too much to be seen","She has only shown her face to her brother"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Viola say she would do as Orsino's lover?","o":["Write love letters every single day","Wait patiently for years and years","Leave Illyria and never come back","Make a willow cabin at Olivia's gate"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Sebastian is alive. He tells Antonio, who saved him from the sea, his real name and that he believes his twin sister drowned. He leaves for Orsino's court. Antonio, who has enemies there, decides to follow him anyway.","key":["Confirms for the audience that Sebastian survived.","Antonio's love for Sebastian is intense and self-sacrificing.","Sets up the mistaken identities to come."],"quiz":[{"q":"What false name did Sebastian use?","o":["Cesario","Roderigo","Valentine","Curio"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why is Orsino's court dangerous for Antonio?","o":["He owes money there","He fought against Orsino's ships","He is a pirate","He is banished from Illyria"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Sebastian believe about Viola?","o":["She is in Illyria","She drowned","She married","She is in Messaline"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why did Sebastian hide his name from Antonio?","o":["He wanted to keep his identity to himself","He was afraid of Antonio and of all of his sailors","He had committed a crime back home in Messaline","His father had told him never to say his name"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Antonio say he will do?","o":["Stay on the ship and wait for him","Go home to his own country now","Follow Sebastian to Orsino's court","Send a letter to Orsino for him"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Malvolio catches up with Cesario and throws down the ring, saying Olivia rejects Orsino. Alone, Viola works out that Olivia loves her and sums up the tangle: she loves Orsino, Orsino loves Olivia, Olivia loves 'Cesario'. Only time can untie the knot.","key":["Viola's soliloquy explains the love triangle clearly.","She sees disguise as a kind of 'wickedness'.","She trusts time, not herself, to solve the problem."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Malvolio return to Viola?","o":["A letter","A ring","A purse","A jewel"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Viola realise?","o":["Orsino knows her secret","Olivia loves her","Sebastian is alive","Malvolio loves Olivia"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Viola call on to untangle things?","o":["Fortune","Time","Orsino","Love"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does Viola call her disguise?","o":["A wickedness the pregnant enemy uses","A clever trick that will save her life","A gift from heaven to help her brother","A game she enjoys playing in the court"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does Viola pity Olivia?","o":["She has lost her only brother at sea","She must marry Orsino against her will","She loves a woman dressed as a man","She is being mocked by her servants"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Late at night Sir Toby, Sir Andrew and Feste drink and sing loudly. Maria warns them; Malvolio arrives and threatens to report them. Sir Toby answers with 'cakes and ale'. After Malvolio leaves, Maria plans to drop a forged love letter in his path, written in Olivia's hand.","key":["The party scene: festive freedom against strict order.","'Cakes and ale' became shorthand for harmless pleasure.","Maria's plan targets Malvolio's vanity."],"quiz":[{"q":"What song does Feste sing first?","o":["Come away, death","O mistress mine","Hold thy peace","Three merry men"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Maria plan?","o":["To tell Olivia everything about the night's noise","To forge a love letter","To lock Malvolio up","To poison his wine"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Whose handwriting can Maria imitate?","o":["Orsino's","Olivia's","Malvolio's","Sir Toby's"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Malvolio threaten?","o":["To throw them all out of the house himself tonight","To call the constable of the town to arrest them","To leave Olivia's service for good in the morning","To tell Olivia about their misbehaviour"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Maria want to trick Malvolio?","o":["He has stolen money from Olivia's household","He has told lies about Maria to her lady","He is a puritan, a time-pleaser and self-loving","He wants to marry Maria against her will"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Orsino asks for an old song and quizzes Cesario about love. Cesario admits loving someone of Orsino's age and looks. Feste sings 'Come away, death'. Orsino claims women cannot love as deeply as men; Viola tells him about a 'sister' who hid her love and pined away. Orsino sends Cesario back to Olivia with a jewel.","key":["Rich in irony: Viola tells Orsino she loves him without him knowing.","Orsino contradicts himself about men's and women's love.","Viola's 'patience on a monument' story is a portrait of herself."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Cesario say he loves?","o":["Someone like Orsino in looks and age","Olivia, the lady he visits for his master","Maria","No one"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Orsino claim about women's love?","o":["It is deeper and more lasting than any man's love","It is like appetite and cannot last","It does not exist at all, except in songs","It is just the same as men's love in every way"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is the 'history' of Viola's imaginary sister?","o":["She married","She never told her love","She died at sea","She became a nun"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Orsino say men's love is like?","o":["Constant and true until the very end","Giddier and more unfirm than women's","The same as women's in every way","Stronger than the hate of an enemy"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Orsino send Cesario to take to Olivia?","o":["A jewel","A song","A ring","A letter"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a2s5":{"sum":"Sir Toby, Sir Andrew and Fabian hide in a box tree. Malvolio daydreams aloud about marrying Olivia and lording it over Toby. He finds the letter, decodes 'M.O.A.I.' to fit his name, and obeys its orders: be haughty, wear yellow stockings cross-gartered, and always smile. The watchers are delighted.","key":["The comic high point: the audience watches the watchers watch Malvolio.","Malvolio's fantasy shows his ambition to rise in rank.","'Some are born great...' becomes the play's refrain."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do the tricksters hide?","o":["Behind a curtain","In a box tree","In the cellar","Behind the door"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What colour stockings does the letter praise?","o":["Bright crimson","Red","Yellow","White"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the letter tell Malvolio to do in Olivia's presence?","o":["Bow","Smile","Kneel","Stay silent"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Malvolio say about greatness?","o":["Some are born great, some achieve greatness, some have it thrust upon them","It belongs only to those who are born into great and noble families","It can only be won by fighting in the wars for one's own country","It comes to those who wait patiently for their turn to come"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Sir Toby want to marry Maria?","o":["She is rich and has her own lands","Olivia has ordered him to do so","He has loved her for many years","He admires her wit for the trick"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Viola trades jokes with Feste and admires his skill. Sir Toby and Sir Andrew greet her. Alone with Olivia, she hears Olivia confess her love. Viola says she can love no woman: 'I am not what I am.' Olivia begs her to return.","key":["Feste and Viola: two clever outsiders who play roles.","Olivia openly declares her love in rhyming verse.","Viola tells the literal truth in riddling form."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Viola say about Feste?","o":["He is wise enough to play the fool","He is a drunkard who should be dismissed by his lady","He should be dismissed","He is in love"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Olivia confess?","o":["She will marry Orsino","She loves Cesario","She sent the letter","She is mourning"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Viola say no woman will ever have?","o":["Her purse","Her heart","Her ring","Her name"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Feste say about words?","o":["They are the only true way to show what is in a person's heart","They should be used carefully, as they can be very dangerous","They are cheap and anyone can buy them with a few coins","They have grown so false he is loath to prove reason with them"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Olivia say when the clock strikes?","o":["The clock upbraids her with the waste of time","It is time for her to go to the chapel and to pray","It is too late for any visitors to stay in the house","It reminds her that her dear brother has died"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Sir Andrew is sulking because Olivia favours Cesario. Fabian and Sir Toby persuade him that she did it to provoke him, and that he should challenge Cesario to a duel. Maria rushes in: Malvolio is wearing yellow stockings and smiling.","key":["Sir Toby manipulates Sir Andrew again, partly to keep his money.","Two tricks now run side by side: the letter and the duel.","Maria's report builds anticipation for the next scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Sir Andrew upset?","o":["He lost money to Sir Toby at cards last night","Olivia favoured Cesario","Maria insulted him in front of the house","He was beaten in the street by a stranger"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What do they persuade him to do?","o":["Go home","Write a challenge to Cesario","Marry Maria","Sing a song"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What news does Maria bring?","o":["Olivia is coming to the garden with her ladies","Malvolio is in yellow stockings","Antonio is arrested by Orsino's officers","Orsino has arrived at the gate"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Sir Toby say the challenge should be?","o":["Curst and brief, with as many lies as will lie on the paper","Polite and long, with many fine words in Latin and Greek","Short and kind, asking Cesario to be friends with him","Angry and loud, read out in front of the whole household"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Fabian say Olivia did to provoke Andrew?","o":["She favoured Cesario to awaken his valour","She refused to speak a word to Andrew at dinner","She sent his love letter back to him unopened","She laughed at his dancing in front of the hall"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Antonio catches up with Sebastian and explains why he followed him. He cannot walk freely in the town because he fought Orsino's navy, so he gives Sebastian his purse and arranges to meet him at the Elephant inn.","key":["Antonio's generosity sets up the confusion over the purse.","Shows the danger Antonio takes on for love.","A quiet scene between two loud ones."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't Antonio walk openly in the town?","o":["He is ill","He fought Orsino's galleys","He has no money","He is banished from the inn"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Antonio give Sebastian?","o":["A ring","His purse","A sword","A letter"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where do they plan to meet?","o":["The Elephant","Olivia's garden","The harbour","Orsino's palace"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Sebastian want to see in the town?","o":["The Duke's palace","The market place","The harbour","The relics and monuments"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"Why does Antonio give Sebastian his purse?","o":["To pay for their lodging at the inn","To buy anything that takes his fancy","To bribe Orsino's officers if needed","To return a debt he owes to Sebastian"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"Malvolio appears cross-gartered and smiling, quoting the letter to a baffled Olivia, who calls it 'midsummer madness'. Sir Toby and Maria treat him as possessed and plan to lock him in a dark room. Sir Toby carries Andrew's challenge by word of mouth, terrifying both duellists. Antonio intervenes for 'Sebastian', is arrested, and asks Viola for his purse. She denies knowing him; he calls her Sebastian, and Viola dares to hope her brother lives.","key":["Malvolio's humiliation and the fake duel run together.","Mistaken identity turns serious when Antonio feels betrayed.","Viola hears her brother's name and hopes he is alive."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Olivia call Malvolio's behaviour?","o":["Midsummer madness","A winter's tale told by a fool","Plain rudeness to his lady","Love-sickness for Maria"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Who stops the duel?","o":["Olivia","Antonio","Orsino","Feste"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Antonio feel betrayed?","o":["Viola denies knowing him","Sebastian stole his ship","Viola insults him","Orsino frees him"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Sir Toby tell each duellist about the other?","o":["That the other is a coward who will run","That the other is willing to make peace","That the other has no sword at all","That the other is a terrible fighter"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Antonio call Sebastian, thinking Viola is him?","o":["A friend who has saved his life","A brother he never had before","A god turned to a vile idol","A boy who is lost and alone"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"concept"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Feste mistakes Sebastian for Cesario. Sir Andrew strikes Sebastian, who hits back hard. Sir Toby draws on him, but Olivia stops the fight, sends Toby away and invites Sebastian into her house. Astonished, he agrees to be ruled by her.","key":["The mistaken identities hit the wrong targets.","Sebastian is braver than Cesario, which confuses the tricksters.","Olivia's love lands on the right twin by accident."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who strikes Sebastian first?","o":["Sir Toby Belch","Sir Andrew","Feste the fool","Fabian, the servant"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who stops Sir Toby fighting Sebastian?","o":["Maria","Olivia","Antonio","Orsino"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Sebastian react to Olivia?","o":["He refuses her","He thinks he is dreaming and agrees","He calls the officers","He runs away"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Sebastian say about the strange events?","o":["He is happy to be in Illyria at last, after the storm","Either he is mad, or else this is a dream","He must find his good friend Antonio at once in the town","He has been here before as a child with his father"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Olivia call Sir Toby?","o":["A kind uncle who cares for her household","A brave soldier who has saved her honour","A wise man who should be listened to","An ungracious wretch, fit for mountains and caves"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"concept"}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Feste, disguised as Sir Topas the curate, visits Malvolio in his dark prison and insists he is mad. Sir Toby, worried about Olivia's anger, wants the joke ended. In his own voice Feste agrees to bring Malvolio light, paper and ink.","key":["The joke grows cruel: the audience may start to pity Malvolio.","Feste plays two voices to confuse him.","Sir Toby is already in trouble with Olivia."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Feste pretend to be?","o":["A doctor of the mind sent by Olivia","Sir Topas the curate","Orsino himself","A jailer of the town"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Malvolio keep saying about the room?","o":["It is cold","It is dark","It is small","It is haunted"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Malvolio ask Feste for?","o":["Food","Light, paper and ink","His freedom","A priest"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Feste say about the room's windows?","o":["They are boarded up with planks for the winter","They are as transparent as barricadoes","They let in all the sun of the day and the moon","They face out to the sea and the cliffs beyond"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Feste ask Malvolio about Pythagoras?","o":["His teacher and his books","His opinion of wild fowl and souls","His country and his family","His view of music and the stars"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Sebastian, holding Olivia's pearl, reasons that he is not mad and neither is she, since she runs her house so calmly. Olivia arrives with a priest and asks him to marry her secretly in the chapel. He agrees.","key":["Sebastian tests his senses against his reason.","Olivia takes the lead in courtship.","The secret marriage sets up the final scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has Olivia given Sebastian?","o":["A ring","A pearl","A purse","A letter"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Whom can Sebastian not find?","o":["Viola","Antonio","Feste","The Captain"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where does Olivia take him?","o":["The chapel","Orsino's court","The inn","The sea-shore"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Sebastian say about Olivia's household?","o":["She manages it too well to be mad","It is too large for one lady alone","It is full of fools and drunkards","It is cold and gloomy and sad"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"When will the public ceremony be?","o":["Tomorrow at dawn","In a week's time","When Antonio arrives","When Olivia chooses to reveal it"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Orsino comes to Olivia's house. Antonio, under arrest, accuses Cesario of ingratitude. Olivia calls Cesario 'husband' and the priest confirms the marriage; Orsino rages. Andrew and Toby arrive bleeding. Then Sebastian enters, and the twins see each other. Viola reveals herself; Orsino offers her his hand. Malvolio's letter is read; Fabian confesses the plot; Malvolio storms out vowing revenge. Feste sings the final song.","key":["Everything is untangled when the twins stand side by side.","Orsino turns from Olivia to Viola almost at once.","Malvolio's exit leaves a bitter note in the happy ending."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who confirms Olivia's marriage?","o":["Feste, who saw it all","The priest","Antonio, the sea captain","Maria, her woman"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What proves the twins are brother and sister?","o":["A ring","Their father's mole and death date","A letter","Their clothes"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What are Malvolio's last words about?","o":["Forgiveness","Revenge","Marriage","Leaving Illyria"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Orsino say when he sees the twins?","o":["Two faces, two voices and one single soul","One face, one voice, one habit, and two persons","A trick of light that plays on all of us","A sign from the gods that Illyria is blessed"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"What song does Feste sing at the end?","o":["Come away, come away, death","O mistress mine, where are you","Hold thy peace, thou knave","The rain it raineth every day"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]}},"notes":{"2":"Orsino treats love as hunger: he wants so much that he gets sick of it. Within seven lines he is bored with the tune. A neat picture of a man in love with being in love.","6":"The hunter Actaeon saw the goddess Diana bathing, was turned into a stag and killed by his own hounds. Orsino casts his desire as the hounds; the pun hart/heart does the rest.","8":"Olivia's vow is as extreme as Orsino's passion. Both are stuck in a mood. The comedy will shake them both out of it.","15":"Viola's first lines play on Illyria and Elysium (the classical heaven). She is practical and hopeful from the start.","31":"Disguise is Viola's choice, not a trick: a young woman alone in a strange country is safer as a boy.","37":"Sir Toby's first line is a direct answer to Olivia's mourning: life is for living. He is the spirit of holiday.","100":"Sir Toby's flattery is really mockery. Sir Andrew cannot hear the difference, which is the joke.","121":"Orsino lists every feminine feature of Cesario and still sees a boy. The audience shares the secret, and Elizabethan audiences knew the part was played by a boy.","122":"A rhyming aside ends the scene with the triangle complete: Viola loves Orsino, who loves Olivia.","142":"Feste invents a philosopher, Quinapalus, to quote. A fool who knows he is a fool is wiser than a clever person who thinks he is wise.","164":"Olivia's line is the key to Malvolio. He tastes life with a 'distempered appetite': nothing is funny to him, and he takes jokes as attacks.","203":"Malvolio's description stresses Cesario's in-between state. Even the most literal-minded character notices the androgyny.","218":"Viola argues that beauty should be shared, not locked away: the same argument as Shakespeare's early sonnets.","245":"Olivia mocks the poetic praise of beauty by turning her face into an inventory. She is witty and in control, until the next few lines.","252":"The willow was the symbol of rejected love. Viola's speech is full of real longing, which is why it works on Olivia. Her own love for Orsino gives it power.","258":"Olivia falls in love in a few minutes, just as Orsino said she would when Cupid struck, but not for him.","289":"Viola's soliloquy is one of the few moments of calm explanation. She sees her disguise as part of the problem, and blames women's 'frailty' for being easily deceived.","293":"Sir Toby's mock-logic opens the party scene. Twelfth Night was the last night of Christmas revels, when misrule was allowed.","348":"Sir Toby's most famous line defends pleasure against a joyless moralist. Malvolio is only a steward, a servant, telling a knight what to do.","362":"Maria says Malvolio is not even a real Puritan, just a self-server who wants to look important. His vanity is his weak spot.","364":"Maria plans the trick. She can copy Olivia's handwriting, which says something about how close mistress and servant are.","392":"Orsino sees himself as the standard of love. The scene keeps showing that Viola loves more truly than he does.","400":"Orsino admits men's love is giddy and changeable, then a few lines later claims no woman can love as hard as he does.","408":"Feste's song is for a lover killed by a 'fair cruel maid'. It flatters Orsino's self-pity, and Feste then mocks him for it.","422":"Orsino's claim that women's love is mere appetite echoes his own words in Act 1, Scene 1, where he described his love as appetite.","427":"Viola's imaginary sister is herself. 'Patience on a monument' is a statue on a tomb: love that suffers in silence.","429":"Viola comes very close to revealing her secret. She also believes Sebastian is dead, so the line carries grief.","445":"Malvolio's daydream starts before he finds the letter: he is already halfway to believing Olivia loves him.","457":"Malvolio imagines himself as Count, summoning 'kinsman Toby'. His fantasy is about power and rank as much as love.","483":"Malvolio's spelling-out of C, U, T and P contains a bawdy pun the audience would catch and he does not.","489":"The letters appear in Malvolio's name but not in order. He bends the riddle to fit, as he bends everything to fit his self-image.","505":"The most quoted line of the letter. It flatters Malvolio's belief that he deserves greatness. It returns twice, the last time against him.","554":"Viola respects Feste's skill. Both are performers who must read their audience: she does the same in disguise.","593":"Viola tells the exact truth. Olivia hears only a riddle.","596":"Olivia ends her confession in rhyming couplets: heightened, but also slightly comic in its neat logic.","612":"Fabian invents a theory to keep Sir Andrew paying. The pranksters live off his money and his gullibility.","636":"Antonio's love is open and generous. His language is as intense as any lover's in the play.","660":"The comedy of the yellow stockings depends on Olivia's total confusion. Every line Malvolio quotes sounds mad to her.","694":"Malvolio reads everything as proof, even Olivia calling him 'fellow'. Self-love makes him unable to see the truth.","719":"A joke about theatre inside the theatre: Fabian reminds the audience that the plot is improbable.","816":"Antonio thinks Sebastian has betrayed him. His speech on outward beauty and inner evil fits the play's theme of appearance.","822":"Viola's hope rises in rhyme. 'I my brother know / Yet living in my glass': she sees him every time she looks in a mirror.","862":"Sebastian's sense of wonder matches the audience's. He chooses the dream over questions.","888":"Feste, as a priest, preaches a real truth: Malvolio is in the dark in more ways than one.","931":"Sebastian tests reality against reason. The calm logic of this speech contrasts with Malvolio's prison scene just before.","1031":"A 'perspective' was an optical toy showing two images. The twins on stage together are the visual climax.","1044":"Viola holds off the embrace until every fact fits. The reunion is slowed down to make it richer.","1076":"Olivia is sympathetic. She promises Malvolio justice, but he will not wait for it.","1079":"Feste remembers Malvolio's insult from Act 1, Scene 5. Time has come round like a spinning top.","1080":"Malvolio's exit unsettles the ending. He is the one character not included in the celebration.","1085":"Feste's song covers a whole life in five verses, ending in rain and drink. The holiday is over; real life resumes."},"gloss":{"surfeiting":"overeating, having too much","strain":"tune, passage of music","fancy":"love, desire; imagination","high-fantastical":"highly imaginative, fanciful","hart":"male deer (pun on 'heart')","pestilence":"plague","fell":"fierce, cruel","element":"sky","cloistress":"nun","brine":"salt water, tears","season":"preserve (as with salt)","shaft":"arrow (of Cupid)","bowers":"leafy shelters","Elysium":"heaven of classical myth","perchance":"perhaps; by chance","provident":"sensible, careful","Arion":"legendary musician saved by a dolphin","eunuch":"castrated male singer or servant","abjured":"sworn off","mellow":"ripe","troth":"faith, truth","quaffing":"heavy drinking","ducats":"gold coins","prodigal":"spendthrift","viol-de-gambo":"viola da gamba, a bass viol","coystril":"knave, low fellow","parish-top":"large spinning top","accost":"approach, address","canary":"sweet wine from the Canaries","kick-shaws":"trifles","galliard":"lively dance","caper":"dancing leap; spice for mutton","coranto":"fast running dance","sink-a-pace":"five-step dance (cinquepace)","Taurus":"zodiac sign","humour":"mood, temperament","aloof":"apart","nuncio":"messenger","rubious":"ruby-red","pipe":"voice","semblative":"resembling","constellation":"nature, as set by the stars","barful":"full of obstacles","lenten":"thin, meagre","gaskins":"loose breeches","motley":"fool's multicoloured costume","madonna":"my lady (Italian)","catechize":"question (as in religious teaching)","infirmity":"weakness of old age","barren":"empty, dull","zanies":"assistants, stooges","bird-bolts":"blunt arrows","Mercury":"god of tricksters and messengers","leasing":"the gift of lying","pia mater":"brain","lethargy":"drowsiness","coroner":"official who investigates deaths","squash":"unripe pea pod","peascod":"pea pod","codling":"unripe apple","shrewishly":"sharply","comptible":"sensitive","con":"learn by heart","usurp":"wrongfully take","swabber":"deck-cleaner","hull":"float, drift","overture":"declaration","olive":"olive branch, sign of peace","maidenhead":"virginity","heresy":"false belief","in grain":"fast-dyed, natural","blent":"blended","nonpareil":"one without equal","divulged":"spoken of","cantons":"songs","reverberate":"echoing","blazon":"coat of arms; description","fee'd post":"hired messenger","peevish":"rude, perverse","county":"count","owe":"own","malignancy":"evil influence","extravagancy":"wandering","Messaline":"Sebastian's home city","fadge":"turn out","fond":"dote","thriftless":"useless","diluculo surgere":"(Latin) to rise early is healthy","stoup":"jug, mug","catch":"round song","testril":"sixpence","welkin":"sky","Cataian":"native of Cathay (China); a cheat","Peg-a-Ramsey":"spy, killjoy (from a song)","consanguineous":"related by blood","tilly-valley":"nonsense","coziers":"cobblers","sneck up":"get lost","gull":"fool, dupe; to trick","nayword":"byword, laughing-stock","Puritan":"strict Protestant","time-pleaser":"time-server","swarths":"swathes","epistles":"letters","Penthesilea":"queen of the Amazons","sack":"sherry","recollected terms":"studied phrases","giddy-paced":"whirling","spinsters":"women who spin","silly sooth":"simple truth","doublet":"close-fitting jacket","taffeta":"shot silk that changes colour","opal":"gem that changes colour","giddily":"lightly","denay":"denial","damask":"pink and white, like a damask rose","sheep-biter":"sneaking dog","nettle of India":"rare plant (or 'metal of India', gold)","box-tree":"box hedge","jets":"struts","advanced plumes":"raised feathers","stone-bow":"catapult","branched":"embroidered","woodcock":"stupid bird, easily caught","gin":"trap","Lucrece":"Roman heroine; her image on Olivia's seal","fustian":"pompous","brock":"badger","stannyel":"kestrel","checks":"swoops","sowter":"name for a hound","slough":"skin shed by a snake","surly":"rude","cross-gartered":"garters crossed round the leg","Sophy":"Shah of Persia","tray-trip":"dice game","aqua-vitae":"brandy, spirits","Tartar":"Tartarus, hell","tabor":"small drum","cheveril":"kid leather","wanton":"loose, unruly","pilchards":"small fish like herrings","Pandarus":"go-between in the Troilus and Cressida story","haggard":"wild hawk","grise":"step","cypress":"cypress-wood coffin; thin black gauze","maugre":"despite","dormouse":"sleepy","Brownist":"member of a separatist sect","bed of Ware":"famous huge bed","cubiculo":"little room","renegado":"one who abandons his faith","sad":"serious","midsummer madness":"extreme madness (linked with midsummer heat)","limed":"caught (as birds with birdlime)","dram":"tiny amount","scruple":"tiny amount; doubt","Legion":"devils who possessed a man in the Gospels","collier":"coal-man; the devil","minx":"hussy","clodpole":"blockhead","cockatrices":"basilisks, whose look could kill","unchary":"carelessly","tuck":"rapier","yare":"quick","carpet consideration":"knighted at court, not in battle","virago":"fierce fighter","duello":"rules of duelling","undertaker":"one who takes on another's quarrel","lubber":"clumsy lout","cockney":"sissy","malapert":"cheeky","rudesby":"ruffian","Lethe":"river of forgetfulness","curate":"priest","Gorboduc":"legendary British king","hyperbolical":"outrageous","Pythagoras":"Greek philosopher who believed souls could move into animals","perdy":"by God","shent":"scolded","vice":"comic devil-tempter in old plays","lath":"thin wood","chantry":"small chapel","plight":"pledge","bawbling":"trifling","scathful":"harmful","Candy":"Crete","brabble":"brawl","non-regardance":"neglect","minion":"darling","jocund":"cheerful","joinder":"joining","coxcomb":"head; fool's cap","incardinate":"(for 'incarnate') in the flesh","othergates":"otherwise","perspective":"optical device showing two images","weeds":"clothes","durance":"prison","perpend":"consider","geck":"dupe","whirligig":"spinning top","convents":"is convenient","toss-pots":"drunkards"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,7,"If music is what feeds love, keep playing. Give me too much of it, so that my appetite, overfed, grows sick and dies. Play that tune again; it had a falling, dying close. Oh, it reached my ear like a soft south wind breathing over a bank of violets, taking their scent and carrying it on. Enough, no more; it isn't as sweet now as it was before."],[8,14,"Oh spirit of love, how restless and hungry you are! Although you can swallow as much as the sea, nothing enters you, however fine and high its value, that doesn't lose its worth within a minute! Desire is so full of changing shapes that nothing is more fantastical."]],"8":[[0,1,"If it please you, my lord, I wasn't allowed in, but her maid gave me this answer."],[2,8,"The sky itself won't see her face openly until seven summers have passed; she will walk veiled like a nun and wet her room once a day with stinging tears. All this is to preserve her love for her dead brother, which she wants to keep fresh and lasting in her sad memory."]],"9":[[0,6,"Oh, if she has a heart so finely made that she pays this debt of love just to a brother, how will she love when Cupid's golden arrow has killed every other feeling that lives in her, when her liver, brain and heart, those royal thrones of passion, thought and feeling, are all filled and ruled by one single king!"],[7,8,"Go ahead of me to sweet beds of flowers; thoughts of love grow rich under a canopy of leaves."]],"18":[[0,6,"True, madam, and to comfort you with that chance, be sure of this: after our ship split, when you and the few saved with you were clinging to our drifting boat, I saw your brother, very sensible in danger, tie himself (courage and hope taught him how) to a strong mast that floated on the sea."],[7,9,"There, like Arion riding the dolphin, I saw him keep company with the waves for as long as I could see him."]],"31":[[0,4,"There is a good honesty in your manner, captain. Although nature often hides corruption behind a beautiful outside, I will believe that your mind matches your fair appearance."],[5,12,"I beg you, and I'll pay you generously, hide who I am and help me with a disguise that suits my plan. I'll serve this duke. You will present me to him as a eunuch; it will be worth your trouble, because I can sing and play him many kinds of music, which will make me worth employing."],[13,14,"Whatever else happens I'll leave to time. Just keep quiet and fit in with my scheme."]],"100":[[0,4,"Why are these talents hidden? Why do these gifts have a curtain in front of them? Are they likely to gather dust, like some lady's portrait? Why don't you go to church dancing a galliard and come home in a running dance? My very walk would be a jig; I wouldn't even piss except in a five-step dance."],[5,7,"What do you mean by it? Is this a world to hide your virtues in? Judging by the excellent shape of your leg, I thought it was formed under a dancing star."]],"121":[[0,5,"Dear boy, believe it. Anyone who says you are a man doesn't know your youth: the goddess Diana's lips are not smoother or redder than yours, and your little voice is like a girl's, high and clear, and everything about you looks like a woman's part."],[6,11,"I know your stars make you just right for this business. Four or five of you go with him, or all of you if you like, since I'm happiest when I have least company. Succeed in this, and you will live as freely as your lord and share his fortune as your own."]],"146":[[0,5,"Two faults, madonna, that drink and good advice will cure. Give the dry fool a drink, and then the fool is not dry. Tell the dishonest man to mend his ways: if he mends, he is no longer dishonest; if he can't, let the tailor mend him. Anything that's mended is only patched: virtue that goes wrong is only patched with sin, and sin that reforms is only patched with virtue."],[6,9,"If this simple logic works, good; if it doesn't, what can be done? Just as the only true cuckold is misfortune, so beauty is a flower that fades. The lady said take away the fool; so, I say again, take her away."]],"252":[[0,3,"I'd make myself a hut of willow branches at your gate and call out to my soul inside your house. I'd write faithful songs of rejected love and sing them loudly, even in the dead of night."],[4,8,"I'd shout your name to the echoing hills and make the chattering echo in the air cry out 'Olivia! Oh, you would get no rest anywhere between the air and the earth until you pitied me."]],"258":[[0,4,"'What is your family background? 'Better than my fortunes, but my position is good. I am a gentleman. I'll swear you are. Your speech, your face, your body, your actions and your spirit all give you a fivefold coat of arms. Not so fast!"],[5,10,"Slowly, slowly! If only the master were the servant. What's this? Can someone catch the plague so quickly? I feel this young man's perfections creeping in at my eyes with invisible, subtle stealth. Well, let it be. Hey there, Malvolio!"]],"271":[[0,3,"No, honestly, sir; the journey I've decided on is just wandering. But I see in you such a fine touch of modesty that you won't force from me what I want to keep to myself; so good manners oblige me all the more to reveal myself."],[4,7,"You must know, then, Antonio, that my name is Sebastian, though I called myself Roderigo. My father was that Sebastian of Messaline whom I know you have heard of. He left behind me and a sister, both born in the same hour."],[8,10,"If heaven had been willing, I wish we had ended together too! But you, sir, changed that, because a few hours before you pulled me out of the breaking waves my sister was drowned."]],"289":[[0,4,"I left no ring with her. What does this lady mean? Heaven forbid that my appearance has charmed her! She looked me over carefully; so carefully, in fact, that I thought her eyes had made her lose her tongue, because she spoke in broken, distracted bursts."],[5,11,"She loves me, surely. The cunning of her passion invites me through this rude messenger. She'll have none of my lord's ring! Why, he sent her none. I am the man. If that's so, and it is, poor lady, she would do better to love a dream. Disguise, I see you are a wickedness that the cunning devil makes much use of."],[12,18,"How easy it is for handsome deceivers to stamp their image on women's soft, wax-like hearts! Alas, our weakness is the cause, not we ourselves, for we are what we are made of. How will this work out? My master loves her dearly, and I, poor monster, love him just as much, and she, mistaken, seems to dote on me."],[19,24,"What will become of this? As I am a man, I have no hope of my master's love; as I am a woman, alas the day, what useless sighs poor Olivia will breathe! Oh time, you must untangle this, not I. It is too hard a knot for me to untie!"]],"408":[[0,7,"Come away, come away, death, and let me be laid in a sad coffin of cypress. Fly away, fly away, breath; I am killed by a beautiful, cruel girl. My white shroud, decorated all over with yew, oh, prepare it! No one so faithful ever played his part in death as I do."],[8,15,"Let not a flower, not a sweet flower, be scattered on my black coffin. Let not a friend, not a friend greet my poor body where my bones are thrown. To save a thousand thousand sighs, lay me, oh, where no sad true lover can find my grave to weep there!"]],"418":[[0,3,"Once more, Cesario, go to that queen of cruelty. Tell her my love, which is nobler than the world, doesn't value quantities of muddy land."],[4,7,"Tell her I regard the wealth that fortune has given her as lightly as fortune itself does. It is the miracle, that queen of jewels, her beauty, with which Nature has adorned her, that draws my soul."]],"422":[[0,3,"No woman's body could withstand the beating of such a strong passion as love gives my heart. No woman's heart is big enough to hold so much; they can't hold on to it."],[4,10,"Alas, their love could be called appetite, a craving not of the liver, the seat of passion, but of the tongue, which gets overfed, sick of it, and disgusted. But mine is as hungry as the sea and can digest as much. Don't compare the love a woman could feel for me with the love I have for Olivia."]],"427":[[0,5,"A blank page, my lord. She never told of her love, but let the secret, like a worm in the bud, feed on her rose-pink cheek. She pined away in thought, and with a pale, sickly sadness she sat like a statue of Patience on a tomb, smiling at grief. Wasn't this love indeed?"],[6,8,"We men may say more and swear more, but really our displays are greater than our feelings, because we always turn out to be strong in our vows but weak in our love."]],"505":[[0,6,"M.O.A.I. This disguise isn't as clear as the earlier part; and yet, if I squeeze it a little, it would bend to fit me, because every one of these letters is in my name. Wait, here comes some prose. [Reads] 'If this falls into your hands, think it over. My stars place me above you, but don't be afraid of greatness. Some are born great, some achieve greatness, and some have greatness thrust upon them. Your fates are holding out their hands; let your courage and spirit embrace them."],[7,17,"And to get used to what you are likely to become, throw off your humble skin and appear new. Be hostile to a relative and rude to servants; let your tongue ring out political arguments, and adopt the habit of being different. This is the advice of the one who sighs for you. Remember who praised your yellow stockings and wanted always to see you with your garters crossed. I say, remember. Go on, your fortune is made if you want it to be; if not, let me see you a steward still, the companion of servants, and not worthy to touch Fortune's fingers. Goodbye. She who would change places with you, The Fortunate-Unhappy."],[18,33,"Broad daylight and open country couldn't show more; this is clear. I will be proud, I will read political writers, I will humiliate Sir Toby, I will drop my low acquaintances, I will be perfect in every detail, exactly the man described. I'm not fooling myself now, letting my imagination trick me, because every reason points to this: my lady loves me. She did praise my yellow stockings recently, she did praise my leg in cross-garters, and in this she reveals her love for me, and with a kind of command urges me to dress the way she likes. I thank my stars I'm fortunate. I will be distant and haughty, in yellow stockings and cross-gartered, as fast as I can put them on. Jove and my stars be praised! Here's a postscript too. [Reads] 'You must know who I am. If you accept my love, let it show in your smiling; your smiles suit you well. So always smile in my presence, my dear sweet, I beg you. Jove, I thank you. I will smile; I will do everything you want me to."]],"554":[[0,5,"This fellow is wise enough to play the fool, and to do that well needs a kind of intelligence. He has to watch the mood of the people he jokes about, their rank, and the right moment; and like a wild hawk, he has to swoop at every feather that comes in front of his eye."],[6,8,"This is a skill as hard as a wise man's profession. The folly he shows so wisely is fitting; but wise men who fall into folly completely ruin their reputation for sense."]],"584":[[0,6,"Let me speak, I beg you. After the last spell you cast here, I sent a ring after you. In doing so I wronged myself, my servant and, I'm afraid, you. I must sit under your harsh judgment for forcing on you, with shameful trickery, something you knew wasn't yours. What must you think?"],[7,11,"Haven't you tied my honour to the stake and set on it all the unmuzzled thoughts a cruel heart can think? To someone as quick as you, I've shown enough: a thin veil, not a bosom, hides my heart. So let me hear you speak."]],"588":[[0,3,"Why, then, I think it's time to smile again. Oh world, how ready the poor are to be proud! If one has to be a victim, how much better to fall to the lion than to the wolf!"],[4,8,"[Clock strikes] The clock scolds me for wasting time. Don't be afraid, good youth, I won't have you. And yet, when your wit and youth have grown to harvest, your wife is likely to reap a fine man. There's your way, due west."]],"596":[[0,3,"Oh, how beautiful scorn looks in the contempt and anger of his lip! A murderer's guilt doesn't show itself faster than a love that tries to hide; love's night is as clear as noon."],[4,11,"Cesario, by the roses of the spring, by maidenhood, honour, truth and everything, I love you so much that, despite all your pride, neither wit nor reason can hide my passion. Don't argue from this that, because I'm the one wooing, you have no reason to woo me. Rather, tie that reasoning with this better reason: love that is sought is good, but love given unsought is better."]],"612":[[0,2,"She showed favour to the youth in front of you only to provoke you, to wake up your sleepy courage, to put fire in your heart and brimstone in your liver."],[3,5,"You should have approached her then, and with some excellent jokes, brand new from the mint, you should have knocked the youth speechless. That's what was expected of you, and you missed it."],[6,9,"You let time wash the double gilding off this opportunity, and now you've sailed into the cold north of my lady's opinion, where you'll hang like an icicle on a Dutchman's beard unless you redeem yourself with some praiseworthy act of courage or cunning."]],"617":[[0,3,"Go and write it in a soldierly hand. Be fierce and brief. It doesn't matter how witty it is, as long as it's eloquent and full of invention. Insult him with all the freedom ink gives you. If you call him 'thou' three times or so, it won't do any harm."],[4,7,"Put down as many lies as will fit on your sheet of paper, even if the sheet were big enough for the great bed of Ware in England. Get on with it. Let there be enough bitterness in your ink, though it doesn't matter if you write with a goose quill. Get on with it."]],"630":[[0,2,"Horribly, like a schoolmaster who teaches in the church. I've followed him like his murderer. He's obeying every detail of the letter I dropped to trap him."],[3,7,"He smiles his face into more lines than there are on the new map with the Indies added. You've never seen anything like it. I can hardly stop myself throwing things at him. I know my lady will strike him; if she does, he'll smile and take it as a great favour."]],"636":[[0,1,"I couldn't stay behind. My desire, sharper than filed steel, drove me on."],[2,7,"It wasn't only love of seeing you, though that was strong enough to make a man take a longer voyage, but anxiety about what might happen to you on your travels, since you don't know these parts, which often prove rough and unwelcoming to a stranger without a guide or friends."],[8,9,"My willing love, made keener by these fears, set out after you."]],"642":[[0,2,"The offence wasn't so bloody, although the circumstances of the time and the quarrel could well have given us cause for bloodshed."],[3,7,"It could have been settled since by repaying what we took from them, which most of our city did for the sake of trade. Only I refused; and if I'm caught here, I'll pay dearly for it."]],"694":[[0,13,"Oh ho! Do you understand me now? No lesser man than Sir Toby to look after me? This agrees exactly with the letter. She's sending him on purpose so that I can be rude to him, because she urges me to that in the letter. 'Throw off your humble skin,' she says, 'be hostile to a relative, rude to servants; let your tongue ring out political arguments; adopt the habit of being different. And then she sets down how to do it: a serious face, a dignified bearing, slow speech, in the manner of some important gentleman, and so on. I've caught her like a bird in lime; but it's Jove's doing, and may Jove make me thankful! And when she left just now, 'Let this fellow be looked after. Fellow! Not Malvolio, not according to my rank, but fellow, meaning companion. Why, everything fits together, so that not a scrap of doubt, not a scrap of a scrap, no obstacle, no unlikely or uncertain circumstance... What can be said?"],[14,16,"Nothing that exists can come between me and the full view of my hopes. Well, Jove, not I, has done this, and he is to be thanked."]],"745":[[0,8,"Now I won't deliver his letter, because the young gentleman's behaviour shows he is intelligent and well brought up; his role as go-between for his lord and my niece confirms that. So this letter, being so splendidly ignorant, won't frighten the youth at all; he'll realise it comes from a blockhead. Instead, sir, I'll deliver his challenge in person, give Aguecheek a great reputation for courage, and drive the gentleman, since I know his youth will easily believe it, into a terrifying opinion of his rage, skill, fury and violence."],[9,10,"This will frighten them both so much that they'll kill each other with a look, like basilisks."]],"861":[[0,2,"let your good sense, not your anger, rule you in this rude and unjust attack on your peace."],[3,7,"Come with me to my house, and hear there how many pointless pranks this ruffian has cobbled together, so that you can smile at this one. You must come; don't refuse. Curse his soul for me; he frightened my poor heart, which lives in you."]],"927":[[0,6,"[Singing] 'I'm gone, sir, and soon, sir, I'll be with you again in a moment, like the old Vice in the plays, to help you in your need."],[7,12,"'With his wooden dagger, in his rage and his anger, he cries ah ha to the devil, like a mad lad: cut your nails, dad; goodbye, Mister Drivel.'"]],"931":[[0,6,"Here is the air, and there the glorious sun; this pearl she gave me I can touch and look at. And though I'm wrapped in wonder like this, it isn't madness. Where's Antonio, then? I couldn't find him at the Elephant, yet he had been there, and I was told that he had gone round the town looking for me."],[7,20,"His advice now could be worth gold to me. My soul agrees with my senses that this may be some mistake but not madness; yet this chance event and flood of good fortune so far exceeds any precedent or explanation that I'm ready to distrust my eyes and argue with my reason when it tries to persuade me of anything other than that I'm mad, or else the lady is. Yet if that were so, she couldn't run her house, command her servants, take charge of business and see it carried out with such a smooth, discreet and steady manner as I see she does. There's something deceptive in it. But here comes the lady."]],"933":[[0,2,"Don't blame me for this haste. If your intentions are honourable, come with me now and with this holy man into the chapel nearby."],[3,6,"There, in front of him and under that holy roof, give me your full promise of faithfulness, so that my jealous and too anxious soul may live in peace."],[7,9,"He will keep it secret until you are willing for it to be made known, and at that time we will hold our celebration in a manner fitting my rank. What do you say?"]],"966":[[0,2,"I remember that face of his well, yet when I last saw it, it was smeared as black as Vulcan's with the smoke of war."],[3,8,"He was captain of a trifling ship, worthless for its shallow draught and size, and with it he grappled so destructively with the finest ship of our fleet that even envy and the voices of the losers praised his fame and honour. What's the matter?"]],"970":[[0,4,"Orsino, noble sir, allow me to reject the names you give me. Antonio has never been a thief or pirate, although, I confess, I had solid grounds to be Orsino's enemy. Witchcraft drew me here."],[5,10,"That most ungrateful boy there, by your side, I rescued from the rough sea's angry, foaming mouth. He was a wreck past hope. I gave him his life, and added my love to it, without holding anything back, all devoted to him."],[11,13,"For his sake, purely out of love for him, I exposed myself to the danger of this hostile town, and drew to protect him when he was set upon."],[14,20,"When I was arrested, his false cunning, since he didn't want to share my danger, taught him to deny he knew me to my face, and in the blink of an eye he became like someone I hadn't seen for twenty years. He refused me my own purse, which I had given him to use not half an hour before."]],"986":[[0,3,"Why shouldn't I, if I had the heart to do it, like the Egyptian thief at the point of death, kill what I love? A savage jealousy that sometimes has something noble about it. But listen to this."],[4,7,"Since you throw my faithfulness aside, and since I partly know the tool that twists me out of my rightful place in your favour, go on living as the marble-hearted tyrant."],[8,11,"But this darling of yours, whom I know you love, and whom, I swear by heaven, I hold dear, I'll tear him out of that cruel eye of yours, where he sits crowned in spite of his master."],[12,14,"Come with me, boy. My thoughts are ripe for harm. I'll sacrifice the lamb I love, to spite a raven's heart inside a dove."]],"1004":[[0,5,"A contract of eternal love, confirmed by the joining of your hands, witnessed by a holy kiss, strengthened by the exchange of your rings; and all the ceremony of this agreement sealed by me as priest, on my testimony."],[6,7,"Since then, my watch tells me, I have travelled only two hours towards my grave."]],"1044":[[0,4,"If nothing stands in the way of our happiness except these men's clothes I've taken, don't embrace me until every detail of place, time and fortune fits together and agrees that I am Viola."],[5,7,"To confirm it, I'll take you to a captain in this town, where my women's clothes are kept. With his kind help I was saved, to serve this noble Count."],[8,9,"Everything that has happened to me since has been between this lady and this lord."]],"1061":[[0,3,"[Reads] 'By the Lord, madam, you wrong me, and the world will know it. Though you have shut me in darkness and given your drunken cousin power over me, I still have the use of my senses as well as your ladyship."],[4,8,"I have your own letter which persuaded me to put on the appearance I did, and with it I have no doubt I can prove myself right or put you to shame. Think of me as you please. I am forgetting my duty a little, and speak out of my sense of injury. The madly-used Malvolio.'"]],"1075":[[0,4,"Lady, you have. Please read that letter. You can't deny now that it's your handwriting. Write differently from it if you can, in handwriting or style; or say it isn't your seal or your composition. You can't say any of this."],[5,9,"Well then, admit it, and tell me, with the decency that honour requires, why you have given me such clear signs of favour, told me to come to you smiling and cross-gartered, to put on yellow stockings, and to frown on Sir Toby and the lesser servants."],[10,14,"And when I did this in obedient hope, why have you allowed me to be imprisoned, kept in a dark room, visited by the priest, and made the most notorious idiot and dupe that any trick was ever played on? Tell me why."]],"1076":[[0,2,"Alas, Malvolio, this isn't my writing, though I admit it's very like my handwriting. But without doubt it's Maria's hand."],[3,10,"And now I think about it, it was she who first told me you were mad. Then you came in smiling, and in the very ways the letter had suggested to you. Please, be calm. This trick has been played on you very cruelly; but when we know the reasons and who was behind it, you shall be both the plaintiff and the judge in your own case."]],"1077":[[0,9,"Good madam, let me speak, and don't let any quarrel or future brawl spoil the happiness of this moment, which I have marvelled at. Hoping it won't, I freely confess that Toby and I set up this trick against Malvolio here, because of some stubborn and discourteous behaviour we resented in him. Maria wrote the letter, because Sir Toby pressed her to, and in reward for it he has married her."],[10,13,"The playful malice with which it was carried out should bring laughter rather than revenge, if the injuries on both sides are fairly weighed."]],"1083":[[0,4,"Go after him and persuade him to make peace. He hasn't told us about the captain yet. When that is known, and the golden time is right, there will be a solemn union of our dear souls. Meanwhile, sweet sister, we won't leave this place."],[5,8,"Cesario, come; for that's what you'll be while you are a man. But when you are seen in other clothes, you will be Orsino's mistress and the queen of his love."]],"1085":[[0,3,"When I was a little tiny boy, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, a foolish thing was just a toy, for it rains, and rains, every day."],[4,11,"But when I grew up to be a man, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, men shut their gates against rogues and thieves, for it rains, and rains, every day. But when I came, alas, to marry, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, I could never get on by swaggering, for it rains, and rains, every day."],[12,19,"But when I came to my bed, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain, I still had a drunken head like the other drinkers, for it rains, and rains, every day. Long, long ago the world was made, with hey, ho, the wind and the rain. No matter: our play is over now, and we'll aim to please you every day."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and desire","Love as appetite, illness, music and madness: Orsino's self-pleasing passion, Olivia's sudden fall, Viola's patient silence, Antonio's devotion."],"identity":["Disguise and identity","Viola as Cesario, Feste as Sir Topas, twins mistaken for each other: who people are versus who they seem to be."],"gender":["Gender","A woman playing a man (played by a boy actor), desire that crosses gender lines, and what men and women claim about each other's love."],"folly":["Folly and wisdom","Feste the wise fool, Sir Andrew the foolish knight, and the self-deceiving 'wise' people who are the real fools."],"madness":["Madness","Love called madness, Malvolio locked up as mad, Sebastian asking whether he dreams: the play's mix-ups make everyone doubt their senses."],"festivity":["Festivity and order","Cakes and ale against the steward's rules: holiday misrule, drinking, song, and the bill that comes after."],"class":["Ambition and class","Malvolio's dream of being Count, Maria's marriage to Sir Toby, Viola's 'Above my fortunes': rank and who may rise."],"time":["Time and fortune","Fortune, fate and time untangle the plot: 'O time, thou must untangle this, not I' and 'the whirligig of time'."],"grief":["Grief and melancholy","The play opens in mourning: Olivia for her brother, Viola for hers, Orsino in love-sickness. Comedy grows out of loss."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"ORSINO","text":"If music be the food of love, play on,","themes":["love"],"note":"Orsino's first line: love as an appetite he wants to overfeed until it dies."},{"id":"2","who":"ORSINO","text":"Even in a minute! so full of shapes is fancy,","themes":["love"],"note":"Love (fancy) keeps changing shape. Orsino describes his own fickle mind without noticing it."},{"id":"6","who":"ORSINO","text":"That instant was I turn'd into a hart;","themes":["love"],"note":"A pun on hart/heart: Orsino casts himself as Actaeon, hunted by his own desires."},{"id":"15","who":"VIOLA","text":"My brother he is in Elysium.","themes":["grief"],"note":"Viola's first thought is her brother. Grief starts both her story and Olivia's."},{"id":"31","who":"VIOLA","text":"Conceal me what I am; and be my aid","themes":["identity","gender"],"note":"Viola chooses disguise as a way to survive in a strange land."},{"id":"31","who":"VIOLA","text":"What else may hap to time I will commit;","themes":["time"],"note":"Viola trusts time to sort things out, an idea she returns to in Act 2."},{"id":"37","who":"SIR TOBY","text":"brother thus? I am sure care's an enemy to life.","themes":["festivity","grief"],"note":"Sir Toby's motto: grief and worry are bad for you, so drink."},{"id":"81","who":"SIR ANDREW","text":"or an ordinary man has; but I am great eater of beef, and, I","themes":["folly"],"note":"Sir Andrew blames beef for his lack of wit: the foolish knight in one line."},{"id":"122","who":"VIOLA","text":"Whoe'er I woo, myself would be his wife.","themes":["love","gender","identity"],"note":"Viola's aside reveals the love triangle in a rhyming couplet."},{"id":"142","who":"FESTE","text":"Quinapalus? Better a witty fool than a foolish wit.—God bless","themes":["folly"],"note":"Feste's invented proverb sums up the play's view of folly and wisdom."},{"id":"148","who":"FESTE","text":"Misprision in the highest degree!—Lady, Cucullus non facit monachum; that's as much to say, I wear not motley in my","themes":["folly","identity"],"note":"'The hood does not make the monk': costume is not identity. Feste is no fool inside."},{"id":"164","who":"OLIVIA","text":"O, you are sick of self-love, Malvolio, and taste with a","themes":["class","folly"],"note":"Olivia diagnoses Malvolio's flaw early. Self-love is what Maria's trap will feed."},{"id":"216","who":"VIOLA","text":"I swear, I am not that I play. Are you the lady of the house?","themes":["identity"],"note":"Viola tells the truth while seeming to joke: she is acting a part."},{"id":"244","who":"VIOLA","text":"Lady, you are the cruel'st she alive,","themes":["love"],"note":"Viola urges Olivia to have children; beauty should leave the world a copy."},{"id":"252","who":"VIOLA","text":"Make me a willow cabin at your gate,","themes":["love"],"note":"Viola's most passionate speech is spoken for Orsino but feels like her own. It wins Olivia."},{"id":"258","who":"OLIVIA","text":"Even so quickly may one catch the plague?","themes":["love","madness"],"note":"Olivia compares falling in love to catching a disease."},{"id":"264","who":"OLIVIA","text":"Fate, show thy force. Ourselves we do not owe:","themes":["time","love"],"note":"We do not own ourselves: Olivia gives in to fate."},{"id":"289","who":"VIOLA","text":"Disguise, I see thou art a wickedness","themes":["identity","gender"],"note":"Viola sees that her disguise has done harm: it lets the devil work."},{"id":"289","who":"VIOLA","text":"O time, thou must untangle this, not I;","themes":["time","identity"],"note":"Viola admits she cannot solve the knot herself; time must."},{"id":"313","who":"FESTE","text":"Present mirth hath present laughter;","themes":["festivity","time"],"note":"Feste's song: enjoy youth now, because the future is uncertain."},{"id":"313","who":"FESTE","text":"Youth's a stuff will not endure.","themes":["time","festivity"],"note":"The song's last line darkens the party: youth does not last."},{"id":"348","who":"SIR TOBY","text":"think, because thou art virtuous, there shall be no more cakes and ale?","themes":["festivity","class"],"note":"Sir Toby's defence of pleasure against the steward's strictness."},{"id":"362","who":"MARIA","text":"utters it by great swarths; the best persuaded of himself, so","themes":["class","folly"],"note":"Maria's character sketch of Malvolio: the vanity her trick will exploit."},{"id":"392","who":"ORSINO","text":"For, such as I am, all true lovers are;","themes":["love"],"note":"Orsino sets himself up as the pattern for every true lover."},{"id":"400","who":"ORSINO","text":"Too old, by heaven! Let still the woman take An elder than herself; so wears she to him,","themes":["gender","love"],"note":"Orsino lectures on marriage while Viola, a woman, stands in front of him."},{"id":"402","who":"ORSINO","text":"For women are as roses, whose fair flower,","themes":["gender","time"],"note":"Female beauty fades as fast as a rose: Viola agrees, sadly."},{"id":"408","who":"FESTE","text":"Come away, come away, death.","themes":["love","grief"],"note":"Feste's sad song for Orsino: a lover killed by a cruel maid."},{"id":"414","who":"FESTE","text":"doublet of changeable taffeta, for thy mind is a very opal!—I","themes":["love","folly"],"note":"Feste mocks Orsino's changeable moods to his face."},{"id":"422","who":"ORSINO","text":"But mine is all as hungry as the sea,","themes":["love","gender"],"note":"Orsino claims men love more than women, contradicting what he said a few lines earlier."},{"id":"427","who":"VIOLA","text":"A blank, my lord. She never told her love,","themes":["love","gender"],"note":"Viola's hidden love, told as a story about an imaginary sister."},{"id":"427","who":"VIOLA","text":"She sat like patience on a monument,","themes":["love","grief"],"note":"An image of silent, suffering love: Viola describing herself."},{"id":"429","who":"VIOLA","text":"I am all the daughters of my father's house,","themes":["identity","grief"],"note":"Viola nearly reveals herself; she also believes her brother is dead."},{"id":"505","who":"MALVOLIO","text":"thee; but be not afraid of greatness. Some are born great, some","themes":["class","time"],"note":"The letter's famous line feeds Malvolio's ambition. It returns twice later."},{"id":"505","who":"MALVOLIO","text":"sighs for thee. Remember who commended thy yellow stockings, and","themes":["class","folly"],"note":"The trap's details: clothes Olivia actually hates."},{"id":"505","who":"MALVOLIO","text":"sweet, I pr'ythee.' Jove, I thank thee. I will smile; I will do","themes":["class","folly"],"note":"Malvolio's total surrender to the forged letter."},{"id":"554","who":"VIOLA","text":"This fellow's wise enough to play the fool;","themes":["folly"],"note":"Viola sees that fooling well takes intelligence."},{"id":"593","who":"VIOLA","text":"Then think you right; I am not what I am.","themes":["identity"],"note":"Viola's most direct hint about her disguise."},{"id":"596","who":"OLIVIA","text":"Love sought is good, but given unsought is better.","themes":["love"],"note":"Olivia's argument for her own unasked-for love."},{"id":"687","who":"OLIVIA","text":"Why, this is very midsummer madness.","themes":["madness"],"note":"Olivia thinks Malvolio is mad. Midsummer was linked with madness."},{"id":"719","who":"FABIAN","text":"If this were played upon a stage now, I could condemn it as","themes":["folly","identity"],"note":"Fabian jokes that the plot is too unlikely for a play: a comic wink at the audience."},{"id":"816","who":"ANTONIO","text":"None can be call'd deform'd but the unkind:","themes":["identity"],"note":"Antonio's bitter lesson about beauty and character, aimed at the wrong twin."},{"id":"820","who":"VIOLA","text":"Prove true, imagination; O prove true,","themes":["identity","time"],"note":"Viola's hope that Sebastian is alive."},{"id":"837","who":"FESTE","text":"Nothing that is so is so.","themes":["identity","madness"],"note":"Feste's joke sums up a world of mistaken identities."},{"id":"862","who":"SEBASTIAN","text":"Or I am mad, or else this is a dream:—","themes":["madness","identity"],"note":"Sebastian's bewilderment when Olivia claims him."},{"id":"888","who":"FESTE","text":"Madman, thou errest. I say there is no darkness but ignorance; in which thou art more puzzled than the Egyptians in","themes":["madness","folly"],"note":"Feste as Sir Topas turns Malvolio's prison into a lesson about ignorance."},{"id":"887","who":"MALVOLIO","text":"I am not mad, Sir Topas; I say to you this house is dark.","themes":["madness","class"],"note":"Malvolio insists on his sanity: is the joke now cruel?"},{"id":"1031","who":"ORSINO","text":"One face, one voice, one habit, and two persons;","themes":["identity"],"note":"Orsino sees the twins together: a living optical illusion."},{"id":"1039","who":"SEBASTIAN","text":"And say—Thrice welcome, drowned Viola!","themes":["identity","grief"],"note":"The reunion: Viola's real name is spoken for the first time."},{"id":"1045","who":"SEBASTIAN","text":"You are betroth'd both to a maid and man.","themes":["gender","identity"],"note":"Sebastian explains Olivia's mistake: nature corrected it."},{"id":"1079","who":"FESTE","text":"gagged'? And thus the whirligig of time brings in his revenges.","themes":["time","folly","class"],"note":"Feste's revenge on Malvolio, and the play's view of time."},{"id":"1080","who":"MALVOLIO","text":"I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you.","themes":["class","festivity"],"note":"Malvolio's last words. The comedy ends with someone excluded."},{"id":"1083","who":"ORSINO","text":"Orsino's mistress, and his fancy's queen.","themes":["love","gender"],"note":"Orsino's last lines: Viola must still change clothes to become a woman."},{"id":"1085","who":"FESTE","text":"For the rain it raineth every day.","themes":["time","festivity","grief"],"note":"Feste's closing song brings the audience back to an ordinary, rainy world."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Illyria, a country on the Adriatic coast. Duke Orsino loves the Countess Olivia, who is mourning her brother and will see no one.","A shipwreck washes up Viola, who thinks her twin brother Sebastian has drowned. Dressed as a youth called 'Cesario', she enters Orsino's service, who sends her to woo Olivia for him.","Olivia falls for Cesario, Viola falls for Orsino, and below stairs Olivia's household plays a cruel trick on her proud steward Malvolio. Then Sebastian turns up alive."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Orsino pines for Olivia","id":2},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Viola is shipwrecked and plans her disguise","id":31},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Orsino sends Cesario to woo Olivia","id":122},{"s":"a1s5","t":"Olivia falls for Cesario","id":258},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Sebastian is alive","id":271},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Viola realises Olivia loves her","id":289},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The midnight party; Maria plans the letter","id":348},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Viola almost confesses her love","id":427},{"s":"a2s5","t":"Malvolio finds the forged letter","id":505},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Olivia declares her love to Cesario","id":596},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Malvolio appears in yellow stockings","id":687},{"s":"a3s4","t":"The fake duel; Antonio is arrested","id":793},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Olivia claims Sebastian","id":862},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Malvolio in the dark room","id":888},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Olivia and Sebastian marry in secret","id":933},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Olivia calls Cesario 'husband'","id":996},{"s":"a5s1","t":"The twins meet","id":1031},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Malvolio swears revenge","id":1080}],"irony":{"121":"Orsino lists every girlish feature of Cesario without guessing he is a woman.","122":"Viola must win Olivia for the man she wants herself.","216":"Viola speaks the literal truth; Olivia thinks it is a clever joke.","258":"Olivia falls for a woman she believes is a man.","261":"Olivia means to give Cesario reasons to love her, not to stay away.","425":"Viola describes her own love to the man she loves, and he does not notice.","450":"Malvolio dreams of power while his enemies watch from the box tree.","505":"Malvolio obeys a letter Olivia never wrote, and smiles at a woman who hates smiling in mourning.","719":"Fabian calls the plot unbelievable, inside a play.","820":"Viola is right: Antonio has seen her brother."},"devices":{"music":"Music and song","sea":"The sea and drowning","sick":"Sickness and appetite","hunt":"Hunting and birds","clothes":"Clothes and disguise","fool":"Fools and folly"},"dev":{"2":[["music","Music feeds love, and too much of it should kill the appetite.","If music be the food of love"],["music","Orsino replays a tune, then tires of it at once.","That strain again"],["sea","Orsino's love swallows everything, like the sea.","Receiveth as the sea"]],"6":[["sick","Olivia's beauty seems to cleanse the air of plague.","Methought she purg'd the air of pestilence"],["hunt","Orsino hunted by his own desires.","That instant was I turn'd into a hart"]],"18":[["sea","Sebastian clings to the mast as Arion rode his dolphin.","I saw him hold acquaintance with the waves"]],"31":[["clothes","Viola plans her disguise.","For such disguise as, haply, shall become"]],"142":[["fool","Feste on wise fools and foolish wits.","Better a witty fool than a foolish wit"]],"148":[["clothes","Clothes do not make the person.","Cucullus non facit monachum"],["fool","Feste's costume is not his mind.","I wear not motley in my brain"]],"164":[["sick","Malvolio's vanity diagnosed as a sickness of appetite.","O, you are sick of self-love"]],"258":[["sick","Love as an infection caught through the eyes.","Even so quickly may one catch the plague"]],"273":[["sea","Sebastian's tears 'drown' his sister's memory again.","She is drowned already, sir, with salt water"]],"289":[["clothes","Viola blames disguise for the confusion it causes.","Disguise, I see thou art a wickedness"]],"310":[["music","A song about seizing love while young.","O, mistress mine"]],"389":[["fool","Feste belonged to Olivia's father, linking him to her past.","A fool that the Lady Olivia's"]],"408":[["music","A song of love as death, to please Orsino's mood.","Come away, come away, death"]],"422":[["sea","Orsino's love as an endless sea-appetite.","But mine is all as hungry as the sea"],["sick","Orsino: women's love is an appetite that gets overfed.","That suffer surfeit, cloyment, and revolt"]],"442":[["hunt","Malvolio is a fish to be caught by tickling.","here comes the trout"]],"481":[["hunt","Malvolio is a foolish bird near the trap.","Now is the woodcock near the gin"]],"505":[["clothes","Malvolio's new clothes become his humiliation.","Remember who commended thy yellow stockings"]],"554":[["hunt","The fool, like a wild hawk, must swoop at every chance.","And, like the haggard, check at every feather"],["fool","Viola on the skill of fooling.","This fellow's wise enough to play the fool"]],"694":[["hunt","Malvolio thinks he has caught Olivia like a bird in birdlime, when he is the one caught.","I have limed her"]],"720":[["sick","The trick has infected Malvolio's mind.","His very genius hath taken the infection"]],"822":[["sea","Viola hopes the sea has been kind after all.","Tempests are kind, and salt waves fresh in love"]],"1048":[["clothes","Orsino wants to see Viola in women's clothes before she is fully 'Viola'.","in thy woman's weeds"]],"1078":[["fool","Olivia calls Malvolio a fool now: the roles have reversed.","Alas, poor fool! how have they baffled thee"]],"1085":[["music","The final song brings the play back to everyday rain.","When that I was and a little tiny boy"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A palace","shore":"The shore","house":"Indoors","garden":"A garden","street":"A street"},"why":{"0":"The opening line that ties love to appetite and music.","3":"Viola's grief mirrors Olivia's and starts her story.","4":"The disguise that drives the whole plot.","8":"Sums up the love triangle in one couplet.","9":"The play's view of wisdom and folly in one line.","11":"The key to Malvolio's downfall.","12":"Viola's truth hidden in a joke.","14":"The speech that makes Olivia fall in love.","15":"Love as sudden illness.","17":"Viola judges her own disguise.","18":"Time as the play's problem-solver.","20":"The party's darker undertone.","21":"The clash of pleasure and order.","22":"Malvolio's vanity, the target of the trick.","28":"Orsino's self-contradiction about love.","29":"Viola's silent love, told about herself.","32":"The line that traps Malvolio and returns at the end.","36":"Viola's clearest hint.","38":"The trick makes Malvolio look mad.","42":"Feste's joke that sums up the confusion.","44":"Ignorance, not the dark room, is Malvolio's prison.","46":"The visual climax of the play.","47":"The reunion and Viola's name at last.","49":"Time brings revenge round again.","50":"The unsettling note in a happy ending.","52":"Back to everyday life after the holiday."},"arc":{"Malvolio's hopes":[2,2,2,2,3,3,3,2,3,10,10,10,10,9,9,1,1,0],"Viola's hopes in love":[2,3,3,4,3,3,2,2,4,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,10]},"arcnote":"Malvolio's hopes soar when he finds the letter and crash in the dark room; Viola's stay low and secret until the twins meet.","context":[["Twelfth Night","The twelfth night after Christmas (5–6 January) ended the holiday season with feasting, games and 'misrule', when servants could mock masters for a night. The title suggests a play of holiday licence that has to end."],["Or What You Will","The subtitle is casual: 'whatever you like'. It suggests a light entertainment, but also the play's interest in desire (will) and choice."],["First performance","The earliest recorded performance was on 2 February 1602 at the Middle Temple, one of the London law schools. A law student, John Manningham, noted in his diary how the steward was fooled into thinking his lady loved him."],["Sources","The twin plot comes from an Italian comedy, Gl'Ingannati (The Deceived, 1531), and from Barnabe Riche's prose story 'Of Apolonius and Silla' (1581). Shakespeare added Malvolio, Sir Toby, Sir Andrew, Maria and Feste."],["Boy actors","No women acted on the public stage. A boy actor took the part of Viola, a woman pretending to be a youth. The audience was always aware of these layers, which makes the play's jokes about gender sharper."],["Puritans","Puritans were strict Protestants who attacked theatres, holidays and festivity. Maria calls Malvolio 'a kind of Puritan', and the revellers' revenge on him reflects the theatre's own quarrel with its enemies."],["Stewards and rank","A steward ran a great household and had real power over other servants, but he was still a servant. Malvolio's dream of marrying a countess breaks the strict social order of the time, which is why it is mocked."],["Fools","Great houses kept professional 'allowed' fools who could say what others could not. Feste belonged to Olivia's father. Robert Armin, the actor who probably played him, was famous for clever, musical fools."],["Illyria","A real region on the east coast of the Adriatic, but in the play a half-imaginary place, its name close to 'illusion' and 'delirium'. Viola puns on it at once with 'Elysium'."],["Melancholy and love-sickness","Elizabethans treated love-melancholy as a medical condition caused by an imbalance of humours. Orsino's fashionable sighing and music fit the type."]],"critics":{"festivity":[["C. L. Barber, Shakespeare's Festive Comedy (1959)","Paraphrased: the comedies follow the pattern of holiday: a release from normal rules leads to a clearer understanding of what normal life is."],["Samuel Pepys, Diary (1663)","Paraphrased: he thought it a silly play with nothing to do with its title or the day it was performed."]],"class":[["Charles Lamb, 'On Some of the Old Actors' (1823)","Paraphrased: Malvolio is not merely ridiculous but dignified, even tragic; the audience ends up pitying him."],["John Manningham, Diary (1602)","Paraphrased: an eyewitness singled out the trick on the steward as the best device in the play."]],"gender":[["Stephen Greenblatt, 'Fiction and Friction' (1988)","Paraphrased: the play keeps desire swerving across gender lines before steering it back into marriage; nature 'to her bias drew'."],["Jan Kott, Shakespeare Our Contemporary (1964)","Paraphrased: Viola's androgyny makes the play a bitter-sweet dream where desire is never fixed."]],"love":[["William Hazlitt, Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817)","Paraphrased: one of the most delightful comedies; it makes us laugh at follies with sympathy rather than scorn."],["Harold Jenkins, 'Shakespeare's Twelfth Night' (1959)","Paraphrased: Orsino and Olivia must both learn to move from self-absorbed love to real love, with Viola as the guide."]]},"insults":[{"id":53,"f":"Castiliano-vulgo! for here comes Sir Andrew Ague-face","who":"Sir Toby","n":"Mocks his friend's thin, sickly face behind his back."},{"id":48,"f":"he's a very fool, and a prodigal","who":"Maria","n":"On Sir Andrew: a fool who wastes money."},{"id":163,"f":"I marvel your ladyship takes delight in such a barren","who":"Malvolio","n":"On Feste. Feste will repeat this at the end."},{"id":145,"f":"Go to, you're a dry fool","who":"Olivia","n":"To Feste, before he wins her round."},{"id":859,"f":"Rudesby, be gone","who":"Olivia","n":"To Sir Toby, for brawling with Sebastian."},{"id":348,"f":"Art any more than a steward","who":"Sir Toby","n":"Reminds Malvolio of his rank."},{"id":353,"f":"Go shake your ears","who":"Maria","n":"Tells Malvolio he is an ass."},{"id":362,"f":"An affectioned ass","who":"Maria","n":"Her sketch of Malvolio's vanity."},{"id":446,"f":"Here's an overweening rogue","who":"Sir Toby","n":"Watching Malvolio's daydream."},{"id":488,"f":"Marry, hang thee, brock","who":"Sir Toby","n":"Calls Malvolio a stinking badger."},{"id":716,"f":"you are idle shallow things","who":"Malvolio","n":"His contempt for the household."},{"id":730,"f":"Youth, whatsoever thou art, thou art but a","who":"Sir Andrew","n":"The feeble challenge letter."},{"id":969,"f":"Notable pirate! thou salt-water thief","who":"Orsino","n":"To Antonio, who denies it."},{"id":1005,"f":"O thou dissembling cub","who":"Orsino","n":"To Cesario, believing he married Olivia."},{"id":1026,"f":"an ass-head, and a coxcomb, and a knave","who":"Sir Toby","n":"Turns on Sir Andrew at last."}],"pronouns":[{"id":145,"f":"Go to, you're a dry fool","n":"Olivia uses 'you' to Feste: mistress to servant, but annoyed."},{"id":590,"f":"I pr'ythee tell me what thou think'st of me","n":"Olivia slips into intimate 'thou' with Cesario as her feelings grow."},{"id":348,"f":"Art any more than a steward","n":"Sir Toby uses 'thou' to Malvolio as an insult to a social inferior."},{"id":1048,"f":"Give me thy hand","n":"Orsino uses loving 'thou' to Viola at the end, as he once did to his page."}],"predict":{"a1s2":["Viola finds her brother at once","Viola disguises herself and serves Orsino","Viola goes home"],"a1s4":["Olivia accepts Orsino","Olivia falls for Cesario","Olivia refuses to see Cesario"],"a1s5":["Cesario keeps the ring","Viola realises Olivia loves her","Malvolio keeps the ring"],"a2s3":["The letter fools Malvolio","Malvolio sees through the trick","Olivia catches Maria"],"a2s4":["Viola confesses her love","Viola hides her love in a story","Orsino guesses the truth"],"a2s5":["Malvolio ignores the letter","Malvolio obeys every word","Malvolio shows the letter to Toby"],"a3s1":["Viola accepts Olivia","Viola refuses Olivia's love","Viola reveals she is a woman"],"a3s3":["Antonio stays hidden","Antonio meets Viola by mistake","Antonio leaves Illyria"],"a3s4":["Malvolio is locked up as mad","Olivia rewards Malvolio","Malvolio exposes the plot"],"a4s1":["Sebastian refuses Olivia","Sebastian goes with Olivia","Sebastian finds Viola"],"a4s3":["Olivia marries Sebastian","Olivia marries Orsino","The wedding is stopped"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Viola and Orsino in love","pts":["Orsino talks endlessly about his love; Viola hides hers.","Orsino's love is for a woman he barely meets; Viola's grows from daily company.","Orsino claims men love more; Viola shows women love as truly."],"ids":[2,427]},{"t":"Feste and Malvolio","pts":["Feste is a fool who is wise; Malvolio is 'wise' but foolish.","Feste enjoys words and play; Malvolio has no sense of humour.","At the end Feste takes his revenge for Malvolio's sneer in Act 1."],"ids":[142,163]},{"t":"Viola and Sebastian","pts":["Twins in looks, opposite in action: Viola is cautious, Sebastian quick to fight and to accept.","Both believe the other drowned.","Their reunion untangles every plot."],"ids":[31,862]},{"t":"Olivia and Orsino","pts":["Both are stuck in a mood: mourning and love-sickness.","Both fall for Cesario in different ways.","Both end married to a twin."],"ids":[8,258]},{"t":"Sir Toby and Malvolio","pts":["Sir Toby stands for holiday freedom; Malvolio for order.","Toby is a gentleman who acts like a servant; Malvolio a servant who wants to be a gentleman.","Both are punished: Toby with a broken head, Malvolio with humiliation."],"ids":[37,334]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","Twelfth Night, or What You Will"],["Written","About 1600–01; first recorded performance 2 February 1602 at the Middle Temple, London; first printed in the First Folio (1623)"],["Genre","Romantic comedy (festive comedy), with a darker subplot"],["Setting","Illyria, on the Adriatic coast: Orsino's palace, Olivia's house and garden, the streets and the shore"],["Plot structure","A main plot of love and disguise (Viola, Orsino, Olivia, Sebastian) and a subplot of a practical joke (Maria, Sir Toby and the others against Malvolio)"],["Main character","Viola, who drives the plot and holds the audience's sympathy"],["Main obstacle","Viola's disguise: it lets her serve Orsino but stops her from declaring her love"],["Turning point","Olivia falls for Cesario (Act 1, Scene 5), and Malvolio finds the letter (Act 2, Scene 5)"],["Climax","The twins stand face to face (Act 5, Scene 1)"],["Ending","Three marriages (Orsino and Viola, Olivia and Sebastian, Sir Toby and Maria); Antonio and Malvolio left out"],["Verse and prose","Lovers and nobles mostly speak verse; the comic household mostly prose; Feste sings"],["Sources","The Italian play Gl'Ingannati (1531) and Barnabe Riche's story 'Of Apolonius and Silla' (1581)"],["Big themes","Love and desire, disguise and identity, gender, folly and wisdom, festivity and order, ambition, time"],["Tone","Festive and witty, but touched with melancholy, loss and cruelty"]],"analysis":{"VIOLA":["Viola arrives with nothing: no brother, no country, no protector. Her first choices show her character. She is practical (she asks where she is and who rules), hopeful (perhaps her brother lived), and careful (she hides her identity until she understands her situation).","Her disguise traps her. As Cesario she can be close to Orsino, but she cannot tell him she loves him. She must woo another woman for him, and does it well because her own feelings give her words power. The 'willow cabin' speech wins Olivia precisely because it is sincere.","She is the play's most honest character in its most deceptive situation. She tells the truth in riddles ('I am not what I am'), refuses Olivia kindly, and refuses to take Antonio's money for herself. She leaves the solution to time, and waits.","In the last scene she stays cautious, checking every detail before she embraces Sebastian. Notice that she never appears in women's clothes: the play ends with her still dressed as Cesario."],"ORSINO":["Orsino opens the play by enjoying his own feelings. He calls for music, then tires of it; he compares himself to a hunted deer; he courts Olivia only through messengers. He seems more attached to his longing than to Olivia herself.","He is changeable, as Feste tells him ('thy mind is a very opal'), and contradicts himself: men's love is 'giddy and unfirm', then no woman can love as he does. Yet he is warm towards Cesario and confides in him fully.","In Act 5 his frustration turns dark: he threatens to kill 'the lamb that I do love'. When the truth comes out, he transfers his love to Viola almost at once. Audiences disagree about whether this shows real love that was already growing, or the same fickleness as before."],"OLIVIA":["Olivia starts in deep mourning, refusing to see anyone for seven years. Feste's 'proof' that she is a fool for mourning a brother in heaven shows that her grief is partly a pose that she can laugh at.","She is witty and in control with Cesario, mocking the idea of a catalogue of her beauty, until she suddenly falls in love. From then on she pursues Cesario boldly, sending a ring, confessing her love and finally arranging a secret wedding.","She also runs a large household. She sees Malvolio's fault clearly, tolerates Feste and Sir Toby, and at the end promises Malvolio justice. Her marriage to Sebastian is a lucky accident, but she accepts it happily."],"MALVOLIO":["Malvolio is Olivia's steward: efficient, serious and hostile to fun. Olivia calls him 'sick of self-love'. He insults Feste, scolds the revellers and reminds everyone of rules. He makes enemies easily.","His secret weakness is ambition. Even before he finds the letter he is imagining being 'Count Malvolio'. The letter only feeds what he already wants. His reading of 'M.O.A.I.' shows how he bends evidence to fit his self-image.","The trick goes further than a joke. He is shut in a dark room and told he is mad. In performance this scene can make him pitiable, and his final line, 'I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you', leaves the comedy with an outsider who will not join the celebration."],"FESTE":["Feste is Olivia's 'allowed fool', licensed to mock. He uses jokes, logic games and songs to tell the truth: he proves Olivia a fool, calls Orsino's mind an opal, and calls himself a 'corrupter of words'.","He moves between the two households, earning tips from everyone. His songs set the mood: 'O mistress mine' for the party, 'Come away, death' for Orsino's melancholy, and the rain song at the end.","As Sir Topas he torments Malvolio, partly to repay the insult in Act 1. His line 'the whirligig of time brings in his revenges' shows that he never forgot. He stands slightly outside the action, watching, and speaks to the audience last."],"SIR TOBY":["Sir Toby is a gentleman who lives off his niece, drinks late into the night and believes 'care's an enemy to life'. He is the spirit of holiday and the leader of the revels.","He is also a user. He takes Sir Andrew's money with the promise of Olivia's hand, sets him up for a ridiculous duel, and finally calls him an ass to his face. His humour has an edge of cruelty.","By Act 4 he wants the Malvolio joke to end, because Olivia is angry with him. He ends the play injured, married to Maria, and having lost his easy freedom."],"SIR ANDREW":["Sir Andrew is a rich, foolish knight who believes he might win Olivia. He is vain about his dancing and his legs, cowardly, and slow to understand jokes. He keeps repeating other people's fine words.","He is the butt of nearly everyone's wit, but there is something sad about him: 'I was adored once too'. He is used by Sir Toby for money and ends the play with a broken head and no bride."],"MARIA":["Maria is Olivia's waiting-woman, small ('the youngest wren of nine') and very sharp. She can copy her mistress's handwriting and understands Malvolio's weakness perfectly.","She invents the trick and directs it, staging the box-tree scene and reporting on the yellow stockings. Her reward is marriage to Sir Toby, a rise in status that matches what Malvolio wanted but did not get."],"SEBASTIAN":["Sebastian is Viola's twin, believed dead. He is warm and grateful to Antonio, but quicker to act than his sister: he fights back at once when Sir Andrew attacks him.","When Olivia claims him he is bewildered but delighted, reasoning that if this is madness he would rather stay mad. He marries her within hours. His arrival untangles the plot."],"ANTONIO":["Antonio rescued Sebastian and loves him with complete devotion, following him into a town where he is a wanted man and giving him his purse.","When Viola, taken for Sebastian, does not know him, he feels utterly betrayed. In the final scene he is reunited with Sebastian but has no part in the marriages, and the play never settles his fate."]},"motifs":[["Disguise and doubling","Viola dresses as Cesario, Feste as Sir Topas, and the twins look identical. Characters keep being taken for someone else, which leads to love, quarrels and arrests. Disguise lets people speak truths they could not otherwise say.",[4,12,36,46]],["Love as appetite and sickness","Orsino wants music to feed love until it sickens; Olivia catches love 'like the plague'; Malvolio is 'sick of self-love'. Desire is something people suffer as much as enjoy.",[0,15,11,28]],["Madness","Love is called madness, Malvolio is treated as mad, and Sebastian wonders if he is dreaming. The confusions make people doubt their own senses.",[38,43,44,45]],["Music and song","The play has more songs than almost any other by Shakespeare. Music feeds Orsino's mood, fuels the party, and ends the play in the rain.",[0,19,26,52]],["Time and fortune","Viola trusts time to untangle the knot; Malvolio trusts fortune; Feste says time brings revenge. Characters wait for chance rather than act.",[5,18,32,49]],["Holiday and its end","The festive spirit of Twelfth Night lets the household break rules, but holidays end. Toby is hurt, Malvolio is bitter, and the last song returns to the daily rain.",[6,21,50,52]]],"symbols2":[["Yellow stockings and cross-garters","Malvolio's new outfit is the visible sign of his fantasy. Olivia hates the colour and the fashion, so the costume meant to win her makes him ridiculous."],["The letter","The forged letter turns Malvolio's private wishes into instructions. Writing in another's hand is the play's sharpest image of deception."],["The ring","Olivia's ring, sent after Cesario, is the first open sign of her love and tells Viola that her disguise has worked too well."],["The sea","The sea separates the twins and returns them. It stands for the chaos of fortune, and for Orsino the endless appetite of love."],["Viola's 'maiden weeds'","Her women's clothes are with the Captain, who is in prison. Viola ends the play still dressed as a man, a reminder that the confusion is not fully resolved."]],"bigq":[["Is Malvolio a villain, a fool or a victim?",["He is rude, self-important and spoils others' fun","His ambition to marry Olivia makes him ridiculous","But the punishment, a dark room and being called mad, goes far beyond his faults","Olivia herself says he has been 'notoriously abused'"],[11,22,45,50]],["Does Orsino really love Viola, or just the idea of love?",["He changes from Olivia to Viola in minutes","But he has grown close to Cesario for three months","He still calls her 'Cesario' at the end","Feste calls his mind an opal: changeable"],[1,23,27,51]],["Is the ending happy for everyone?",["Three couples marry","Antonio has no partner and an uncertain future","Malvolio leaves vowing revenge","Sir Andrew is injured and abandoned by Toby"],[46,47,50,52]],["Who is the wisest person in the play?",["Feste, the professional fool","Viola, who sees clearly and waits","Olivia, who judges Malvolio truly","The 'wise' Malvolio turns out the greatest fool"],[9,35,11,44]],["What does the play suggest about gender?",["A woman plays a man and is loved by a woman","Orsino claims women love less, Viola proves him wrong","Sebastian says nature 'drew to her bias' in the end","Viola never changes back into women's clothes on stage"],[8,28,29,48]],["Does the play celebrate festivity or warn about it?",["The party scene is joyful and Toby defends pleasure","But the revellers become cruel","Toby ends hurt and Andrew betrayed","Feste's final song ends in rain and drink"],[6,21,50,52]]],"symbols":{"Music and song":"\\bmusic\\b|\\bsong\\b|\\bsing\\b|\\bsung\\b|\\bcatch\\b|\\btune\\b","The sea":"\\bsea\\b|\\bwaves?\\b|\\bdrown|\\bship\\b|\\bsail","Madness":"\\bmad\\b|\\bmadman\\b|\\bmadness\\b|\\blunatic\\b|\\bdistract","Fools":"\\bfools?\\b|\\bfolly\\b|\\bfoolery\\b","Fortune and fate":"\\bfortunes?\\b|\\bfate\\b|\\bstars\\b","Yellow stockings":"\\byellow\\b|cross-garter"},"stagerules":[["sea-coast","shore"],["palace","palace"],["garden","garden"],["street","street"],["room|house","house"]],"solo":{"258":1,"264":1,"289":1,"554":1,"694":1,"745":1,"820":1,"931":1},"iago":[["a2s3","Malvolio threatens to report the revellers to Olivia."],["a2s3","Maria plans a forged love letter in Olivia's handwriting."],["a2s5","Maria hides Toby, Andrew and Fabian in the box tree and drops the letter."],["a2s5","Malvolio reads the letter and decodes M.O.A.I. as his own name."],["a2s5","He resolves to wear yellow stockings, cross-gartered, and to smile."],["a3s2","Maria reports that Malvolio follows the letter to the last detail."],["a3s4","Malvolio smiles and quotes the letter to a baffled Olivia."],["a3s4","Olivia, thinking him mad, puts him in Sir Toby's care."],["a3s4","Sir Toby plans to keep him bound in a dark room."],["a4s2","Feste, as Sir Topas, tells Malvolio he is mad."],["a4s2","Feste agrees to carry Malvolio's letter to Olivia."],["a5s1","Malvolio's letter is read; Olivia sees Maria's handwriting."],["a5s1","Fabian confesses the plot; Malvolio vows revenge."]],"rel":{"1":[["ORSINO","OLIVIA","love"],["OLIVIA","ORSINO","hate"],["VIOLA","ORSINO","love"],["ORSINO","VIOLA","trust"],["OLIVIA","VIOLA","love"],["MALVOLIO","FESTE","hate"],["SIR TOBY","SIR ANDREW","deceive"]],"2":[["ANTONIO","SEBASTIAN","love"],["MALVOLIO","SIR TOBY","hate"],["MARIA","MALVOLIO","deceive"],["SIR TOBY","MARIA","trust"],["VIOLA","ORSINO","love"],["OLIVIA","VIOLA","love"]],"3":[["OLIVIA","VIOLA","love"],["VIOLA","OLIVIA","hate"],["SIR TOBY","SIR ANDREW","deceive"],["SIR TOBY","VIOLA","deceive"],["ANTONIO","VIOLA","hate"],["MALVOLIO","OLIVIA","love"]],"4":[["OLIVIA","SEBASTIAN","love"],["SEBASTIAN","OLIVIA","love"],["FESTE","MALVOLIO","deceive"],["SIR ANDREW","SEBASTIAN","hate"]],"5":[["ORSINO","VIOLA","love"],["VIOLA","ORSINO","love"],["OLIVIA","SEBASTIAN","love"],["ANTONIO","SEBASTIAN","trust"],["MALVOLIO","OLIVIA","hate"],["SIR TOBY","SIR ANDREW","hate"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present love in Twelfth Night?",[0,14,28,29,37]],["How does Shakespeare use disguise and mistaken identity?",[4,12,17,36,46]],["How is Malvolio presented, and should we pity him?",[11,22,34,45,50]],["How does Shakespeare present Viola?",[4,8,18,29,31]],["How are folly and wisdom presented in the play?",[9,10,35,44,49]],["How does Shakespeare present festivity and order?",[6,19,21,52]],["How does the play explore gender?",[8,24,25,28,48]],["How does Shakespeare present Orsino?",[0,1,23,27,51]],["How does Shakespeare present Olivia?",[11,15,16,37,38]],["How does Shakespeare present Feste?",[9,10,26,42,49]],["How does Shakespeare present time and fortune?",[5,16,18,32,49]],["How is the ending of Twelfth Night both happy and unsettling?",[46,47,49,50,52]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, embedded quotations","Analysed key words and images, not just the meaning","Linked to context (Twelfth Night revels, boy actors, Puritans, rank)","Tracked change across the whole play: beginning, middle, end","Considered the audience and dramatic effect (irony, disguise, the box-tree scene)","Mentioned an alternative reading or critic","Wrote a conclusion that answers the question","Checked spelling of names: Viola, Sebastian, Orsino, Olivia, Malvolio, Aguecheek"],"place":{"a1s1":"Orsino's court","a1s2":"The coast of Illyria","a1s3":"Olivia's estate","a1s4":"Orsino's court","a1s5":"Olivia's estate","a2s1":"The coast of Illyria","a2s2":"The town","a2s3":"Olivia's estate","a2s4":"Orsino's court","a2s5":"Olivia's estate","a3s1":"Olivia's estate","a3s2":"Olivia's estate","a3s3":"The town","a3s4":"Olivia's estate","a4s1":"Outside Olivia's house","a4s2":"Olivia's estate","a4s3":"Olivia's estate","a5s1":"Outside Olivia's house"},"models":[[0,"Love is presented as powerful but often self-indulgent.","Orsino's metaphor \"If music be the food of love\" makes love a hunger he wants to overfeed until it dies.","The audience see a lover more in love with love than with Olivia.","Elizabethan love poetry often showed the suffering lover as a fashionable role.","Viola's quieter, patient love offers a contrast that makes Orsino's look shallow."],[4,"Disguise creates comedy but also confusion about identity.","Viola's \"Conceal me what I am\" asks for help to hide herself, starting the play's chain of mistaken identity.","The audience enjoy knowing more than the characters, which drives the dramatic irony.","Boy actors played women, so a girl disguised as a boy would add a layer for the original audience.","Some readers see disguise as freeing, as it lets Viola speak more honestly than she could as a woman."],[11,"Malvolio is shown as proud and self-important, but his punishment goes too far.","Olivia's \"sick of self-love\" names his flaw directly and links it to illness.","The audience laugh at his vanity at first, then may feel uneasy at his treatment.","Puritans were often mocked as killjoys, and Maria calls him \"a kind of puritan\".","His final \"I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you\" leaves the ending with a bitter note."],[29,"Viola is presented as patient, loving and witty.","\"She never told her love\" tells her own story in the third person, a way of confessing while still hiding.","The audience see her pain hidden in plain sight.","Women were expected to wait to be wooed, which limits Viola.","Some readers see her as passive; others see her as the play's emotional centre."],[35,"Folly and wisdom are reversed, as the fool is often the wisest person on stage.","Viola's \"wise enough to play the fool\" is a paradox, showing that real fooling takes skill.","The audience learn to value Feste's comments.","Professional fools were employed in noble households.","Malvolio's foolishness, though, is shown as lacking any wisdom."],[21,"The play sets festivity against order, and both have their place.","\"Cakes and ale\" stand for pleasure and celebration in Sir Toby's challenge to Malvolio.","The audience are invited to side with festivity.","Twelfth Night was the last night of the Christmas festivities, a time of misrule.","The ending's cold note suggests festivity cannot last."],[24,"Gender is presented as flexible and confusing.","Orsino's advice \"Let still the woman take An elder than herself\" claims men's love is changeable, said to Viola, who is a woman in disguise.","The audience enjoy the irony that he is lecturing a woman about women.","Gender roles were strictly expected, so the disguise plot challenged them.","Some critics see the play as reinforcing norms, since everyone pairs off conventionally at the end."],[1,"Orsino is presented as a romantic dreamer whose love is changeable.","\"so full of shapes is fancy\" shows imagination constantly changing, which reflects his own moods.","The audience see a lover more interested in the feeling than the person.","Courtly love conventions celebrated longing and sighing.","His quick switch from Olivia to Viola at the end supports a cynical reading."],[16,"Olivia is presented as a woman who swings from mourning to sudden love.","\"Fate, show thy force\" gives up control, showing her helpless before love.","The audience enjoy the irony that she has fallen for a disguised woman.","A noblewoman controlling her own household was unusual but possible.","Her quick marriage to Sebastian can seem comic or troubling."],[42,"Feste is presented as a wise fool who sees through others.","\"Nothing that is so is so\" sums up the play's world of illusion in a paradox.","The audience see Feste as a commentator on the confusion.","Fools were licensed to speak freely.","His final song, sad and wistful, suggests his wisdom includes melancholy."],[18,"Time and fortune are presented as forces that solve problems humans cannot.","\"O time, thou must untangle this, not I\" uses personification to hand the knot of the plot to time.","The audience trust that the comedy will resolve.","Fortune and fate were popular ideas in Renaissance thought.","Feste's \"whirligig of time\" suggests time also brings revenge."],[46,"The ending is both joyful and unsettling.","\"One face, one voice, one habit, and two persons\" shows wonder at the twins' reunion.","The audience enjoy the resolution but notice Malvolio and Antonio left out.","Comedies usually ended in marriage and reconciliation.","Malvolio's exit and Feste's rainy song leave a melancholy aftertaste."]],"lang":{"un":[[816,"In nature there's no blemish but the mind","There's no blemish in nature but the mind"],[240,"such a one I was this present","I was such a one this present"],[13,"What country, friends, is this","What country is this, friends"],[264,"ourselves we do not owe","we do not owe ourselves"]],"pun":[[528,"No, sir, I live by the church","live by","pun","Viola means make a living from; Feste pretends he means lives next to. The joke shows how Feste plays with every word."],[6,"That instant was I turn'd into a hart","hart","pun","Curio offers to hunt the hart (a deer); Orsino plays on heart, which he hunts in himself, turning a hunt into love poetry."],[370,"Ass, I doubt not","Ass","pun","Ass means a donkey and a fool, and it also sounds like as I: Maria agrees and insults Sir Andrew in one word."]],"st":[[2,"If music be the food of love, play on",[["music","Sounds like a dreamy, self-indulgent romantic."],["love","Makes love the real subject he cannot stop talking about."],["play","A command: Orsino is used to being obeyed."]]],[216,"I am not that I play",[["I","Points to the real Viola hidden under the disguise."],["not","Almost confesses the trick."],["play","Reminds the audience that everyone on stage is acting."]]],[1080,"I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you",[["I'll","A personal threat: Malvolio alone against the rest."],["revenged","Leaves the comedy with a dark promise."],["whole","Includes everyone, perhaps even the audience who laughed."]]]]},"dir":[[2,"If music be the food of love, play on","How should Orsino open the play?",[["Lying among cushions","A lazy, indulgent lover."],["Pacing, impatient","Restless and unsatisfied."],["Mocked by bored musicians","We laugh at his self-pity from the start."]]],[252,"Make me a willow cabin at your gate","How does Viola deliver the willow cabin speech?",[["As a set speech for Orsino","She performs the message she was given."],["Gradually meaning it","She speaks her own love for Orsino through it."],["Directly into Olivia's eyes","Explains why Olivia falls for her."]]],[505,"Some are born great","How does Malvolio read the letter?",[["Aloud, with growing delight","His vanity grows with each line; big laughs."],["Mouthing it, then repeating","He savours every word; we see his hunger to rise."],["With the hidden pranksters reacting","The comedy is in their reactions as much as his."]]],[427,"She never told her love","How much does Viola reveal?",[["Almost nothing: calm","Her control makes it more painful."],["Nearly breaking","The audience fear she will give herself away."],["Turning to the audience","We share her secret while Orsino misses it."]]],[887,"I am not mad","How should Malvolio be treated in the dark room?",[["As pure slapstick","The prank stays a comedy."],["Visibly suffering","The joke has gone too far; the audience feel uneasy."],["Some laugh, some don't","The audience divide, like the characters."]]],[1080,"I'll be revenged on the whole pack of you","How does Malvolio leave?",[["Shouting, absurd","A comic exit; we still laugh at him."],["Quietly, bitterly","A dark shadow over the happy ending."],["Straight at the audience","We, who laughed, are included in the threat."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["37","348","1080"],["392","264","1085"],["505","348","142"],["1031","1039","719"],["122","427","1083"],["358","362","348"],["505","348","1080"],["142","148","888"],["15","1039","1031"],["2","392","408"]],"cr":{"festivity":[["37","348","1085"],["1085","313"]],"class":[["887","1080","164"],["505","720","362"]],"gender":[["1045","122","400"],["427","289","1083"]],"love":[["148","596","1079"],["2","252","1083"]]}}};
